      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <p>Va:t(4l McDOUGALUS j'atfult sELF—RAISINC FLOUR urrii:aued. The Exarainer, Saturday, November 6, 1909,</p>
        <p>conneotion with the private Glee and Madrigal Union. Mr, White handed this sum, 10s. lid., over on November 6th, 1877, to the treasurer of the Muskat Society. Mr. Pattison stated that he had recently heard of the whereabouts of several valuable instru-</p>
        <p>organist in -place of Mr. Pattison, who resigned. Season 1885-6 started somewhat doubtfully. "The Elijah " had been selected for the first concert, and it was thought desirable to again try to organize a local band, but Dr. Hiks opposing it the idea was abandoned, and a complete professional orchestra was engaged together with good vocalists. Musically, this concert was ,:ery successful, but it was Very badly supported,' and to avoid loss on the season, it was necessary to give the final concert at small cost, and the Misses Wallington, Monks, and Podmore, Messrs. R. T. Fairclough and P.' C. Price were asked to act as principal-. This economy resulted in a gain of £1 on the season, reducing the debt to £6 17s. 6d. The following season, there was a further loss of £4 16s. 7d, it having been necessary to engage professional bands for both concerts.</p>
        <p>meats and a quantity of music, the property of the Society, and that if authorised, he thought he could get them ,restored. So far as I can discover, these instruments and music were never recovered, but a considerable quantity of music was found in a chest belonging to the Society, and was sorted and catalogued by the writer. This music is now, I _</p>
        <p>believe, in the library. About this time, the Society being but poorly sup- portea, a scheme, suggested by the Secretary, to con- vert it into a private society was considered by the Committee on August 26th, 1877, and, a resolution passed adopting the scheme, which was confirmed atl the annual meeting on September 18th, 1877, when the new rules were approved,.. but with the following amendment—" That the Committee may in their dis- cretion adopt such means for the admission of the public to the concerts as in their opinion may seem , desirable." This amendment was not acted upon in the following season. On September 11th, 1877, birth was giYen to a new society, called "The Amateur Orchestral Society." Jas. ilarson, Esq, kindly .accepted the presidency of the society, and the writer was appointed secretary and treasurer. \ The objects of this society were "To foster the growth of instrumental music in the town, and to give good orchestral and instrumental concerts."—The society was quite distinct from, but worked in ,co-operation with the Musical Society. Mr. Lawson, of Liverpool, was the first conductor, and was followed later by Mr. L. Veerman. of Halle's _</p>
        <p>About this time the Secretary (Mr. L. W. Reynolds) resigned after 22 years' service, and in recognition of his labours in the Society's interest, a presentation was made to him, consisting of a large salver and a fine edition of Grove's" Dictionary of Music." Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins was appointed in his stead, and Mr. Muir took the place of Mr. Johnson as organist; he, however, resigned the following 'January, and Mr. Driffield, of Liverpool, who very kindly offered his services for final practices and concerts was appointed. Mr. W. S. Woods also resigned the conductorship of the elementary class, and it was stated that the Rev. Edward Moore was willing to undertake the work, but there is no record of any appointment having been made.</p>
        <p>In January, 1889, vet another effort was made to get a band together, and a number of instrumentalists came forward willing to join the Society as ordinary members. A start was therefore made, and Herr Praeger was engaged as trainer. On April 2:ith, 1889, a special general meeting was called to "con- sider the financial position and to discuss other matters affecting the well-being of the Society." Dr. Hiles attended, and it was decided to give three con- certs again, to keep down the cost of professional lassistance, and to make the first a purely ballad and orchestral concert, rely ing almost entirely on local resources.</p>
        <p>ii;;;,i;ili;;;c1;e;;: O'n January 29th, 1878, Dr. Hiles expressed a wish that tile band of the Orchestral Society should accompany the chorus in " Dettingen Te Deum," which was selected for the concert on February 28th, and he personally took the band in hand at 6 p.m. on praofice nights. From this time till the W.A.O. Society became defunct on March 27th, 1882, it furnished, with. some professional help,</p>
        <p>the band for the Musical Society's 'concerts, and gal-,e' concerts of its own, when some very high class and classical music Was performed. For the final concert, wh-ch was kindly guarantee-I by the President, Mr. Charles Santley was engaged, and an excellent pro- gramme was given. The annual meeting, 1878, took place on September 30th, when the Secretary reported a further increase in membership, and also better attendance at the concerts. The new scheme had worked well, and had brought a large increase in the centre gallery sub- scriptions, and it had, in consequence, been decided to provide seats in the body of the hall, reserved at the same price. During the season the altos had been very weak, and Dr. Hiles had brought over from Manchester four males for the first concert, and Mi. Dewhurst had sent eight boys from the N*ional school to practice for and assist at the second. The accbunts showed a deficiency of 19s. 9d. The following Season, hr. Biles suggested .that three concerts should be given. This was approved, but as under the existing scheme members could not be charged for admission for three concerts, it was decided that the third should be given by the. W.A.O.</p>
        <p>_ The report at the next general meeting was, how- over, a somewhat gloomy one. Mr. Scotson, after some years of useful work as assistant secretary, resigned. and Mr. Newcombe also resigned his position on the Committee, having gone to live out of town. The treasurer's report showed a loss of £4l 17s. 10d. on the season, kind a total deficit of ±:7l 10s. against Which, donations had been received, amounting to £35 10s. The " Messiah " concert, costing 72 °guineas for professional assistance, had been very badly supperted. and had involved a serious loss. The Chairman (Mr. Rylands) referred to the Society's early r days, and remarked that they then had a great advan- tage in not having a paid conductor. He, however, did not .despair, and he moved" That instead of adopting the report, the meeting be adjourned and a special Committee be appointed to consider the best method .of dealing with the present condition of the Society." Mr. Dewhurst remarked that with a population of 45,000, it would be a disgrace to Warrington if such a Society could not be kept in existence. The first great benefit it conferred was the excellent training given to its members, the second was to the public, -and the third was to all the churches and chapels in the town, as the Society formed an invaluable recruit- ing ground for them, and their leading members were many of them leading members of the Society. the, excellent results being seen and heard Sunday after Sunda. Mr. Rylands' motion being carried, the Committee later decided to carry on the work of the Society on much the same lines, but to make renewed efforts to increase its usefulness. (The Report, and Accounts were therefore passed on October 19th, 1890). On Februiry 4th, 1891, Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins</p>
        <p>Society, assisted by the Musical SOciety, on February 4th. 1879. The experiment does not appear to have been altogether successful, for it was reported at the annual meeting on September 30th, 1879. that though there was a better attendance a loss on the season of</p>
        <p>;£2l 6s. had resulted, but this included a considerable sum incurred in alteratii,ns of the Society's orchestra, at the Public Hall. The total deficit, including the balance from 1877-8, was .£42 16s. 2d. • At the commencement of the season 1879-80, Mr.</p>
        <p>Pattison resigntd his position as honorary organist and was re-appointed at a salary of £lO 10s., per annum, to include attendance at rehearsals. During the season, the Society worked profitably, and the treasurer was sable to announce a balance of 5:11</p>
        <p>(honorary secretary) resigned, and on July 17th, the Committee passed the following resolution :—" That a General Meeting of the Society be called to discuss and consider its position. The Committee feel that the expenses connected with tilt working of the Society in the past have been so great that recon- struction with a view to effecting a saving of expense is now rendered imperatively necessary." (To lie continued).</p>
        <p>98. 10d. to the good, reducing the debt to £29 (is. 4d. The folh,wing season was not so successful finanCidlly.. The Orchestral Society's band was only available for the 'first concert, and Dr. Hiles, deeming an efficient orchestra essential for the performance of the "Widow of Nain," a professional band was engaged. This, with increased expense in respect of conductor and organist. resulted in a loss of £.1.4.35.:3d. on the season, making a deficit of £43 Os 7d., not including £l7 10s.. which it had again been necessary to spend on the ()Tan. On January 10th this year (1882) a conversazione was given to members of the choir :and band at the Art Gallery, other members being admitted at the charge of 2s. (id. There was a musical programme arranged and Dr. Hiles was present:. In October, the tlementary . Class was again established under Mr. W. S. Woods'. Mr. Patti- son being unable to undertake it. A fee 0f..28. 6d. was paid by members. Another attempt was now made to get together a band, and an advertisement was put in the papers inviting instrumentalists to join. About twelve came forward, but the music chosen for the first concert, " The'Bride of Dunkerron," being very difficult, and Mr. Karl Meyder, the conductor of the Liverpool and Buxton concerts, having offered to bring over his band at a cost of 21s. per man. Mr. T. G. Ryhinds having also offered £5 i the society accepted the offer, it was decided to dispense with the amateur band for this concert, and Mr. Meyder's Band was engaged, together with Mr. R. H. Pearson, who for some years had kindly given his services as leader of the Orchestral and Musical Societies' .bands. Meanwhile the above mentioned instrumentalists with a few others met for practice under the conductorship of Mr. F. W. Reynolds, and took part in the second concert of the season. .</p>
        <p>THE FREE CHURCHES AND THE EDUCATION POSITION. Letter from the Prime Minister. The following communications have passed between the Prime Minister and the General Committee of the National Council of the Evangel:cal Free Churches : Memorial Hall, ~ Farringdon-street, London, .E.C, Bth October, 1910. Dear Sir: --The Executive Committee of the National Council of Evangelical Free Churches have instructedl us to approach you on the question of Education, a subject in which, as you are well aware, the Free Churchmen of England and Wales are deeply interested. We fully realise that a considerable amount of Parliamentary time and Cabinet considera- tion has been given to Education, but it is lamentably true that n6thing so far has been effected. It is only fair to recognise the efforts of the Ministers for Educa- tion to better the position by more impartial adininis • tration f the 4xisting Acts. Unhappily that does not carry us very fay, and the broad features of the Act of 1902 remain unchanged and as intolerable as ever. 'I hose of us who are in touch with the Parliamrtary conditions of the day, know full well with what difficul- ties the Government are confronted, but the great body .of Free Churchmen who laboured so diligently for their cause as expressed in the promises of the Liberal Cabinet during the Election of 1906, feel disheartened and in the bitterness of their disappointment are beginning to regard the political situation as almost hopeless. They have, seen other and more clamant groups secure by parliamentary assistance a considerable remedy of their grievances, but Free Churchmen, who are essentially law-abiding and reasonable, appear to have suffered through their very moderation Unless this impression can be removed, the discontent, difficult it may be to define, but intensely felt, must have an unfortuirite effect upon Education prospects and upon the relation of Free Churchmen to a Govern- ment which they have hitherto so warmly supported. Believing as we do in the sincerity of the Government, we yet ask you as its head and as the leader of the progressive forces in the country, to give us the assurance of your intention either in this Parliament or irt thenext, if. you be returned to power to carry the mattei with yet more determined effort to a decisive conclusion at any • sacrifice of political con- venience,—Yours sincerely, (Signed) • EVAN JONES. President. THOMAS LAW, Secretary. Rt. Hon. H. H. Asquith, M.P., Downing St.. S.W.</p>
        <p>--During this season 1882-3. 59 18s. was raised to wipe off the deficit and defray the cost of the organ repairs, which with a profit on the season of £3 Gs. Id. enabled the Treasurer to close accounts with a balance in hand of .7£19 14s. 6d. Mr. Woods was able to give a good report of the Elementary Class which numbered 170 Tgembers with tan average attendance of 120. In November 1883, Dr. Hiles notified his intention of resigning at Christmas. The President (Mr Rylancls), Mr. R. T. Fairclough and the Secretary were therefore deputed to confer with him with a view to some arrangement which would retain the services of Dr. Hiles, and if possible, to include the training of the band. This resulted in Dr. Hiles stating his views in writing, and on December 14th, it was</p>
        <p>resolved (1). That committee meetings- he held at such times as will admit of the Conductor being present. (2). That rehearsals be held on Thursday. (3). That it shall not be considered necessary to avoid giving concerts in Lent. (4). That the elementary class shall be under the direct cognizance and control of the committee. In May 1884, Mr. Woods reported the numbers attending the elementary class to be 105 with an average of 55. and he stated, that he considered 19 members as likely to pass for admission to the Society's chorus. At the annual meeting on September 4th, 1884, the Secretary reported that "The Messiah" had ,been given as an extra concert with great success on December 17th, 1883, to celebrate the jubilee of the Society which fell on December 24th. The chorus now numbered 120 and the laand 21 members. For the second concert, Signor Risegari was engaged to lead the band, and he brought over with him eleven members of Halle's band, and Samuel Platt, Esq., of Oldham kindly gave his services as oboist and also brought over two members of his band. Financially, the season was unprofitable and shewed a loss of £4 15s. 10d., but leaving £l4 Bs. Bd. still to the good. Rule 10 was altered to allow of the annual meetings being held as soon after the close of the season as possible. In September, 1884, the society's band was placed under Dr. Hiles, and in order to render it as efficient as possible, an invitation was sent out to the instrumenthlists to meet at Cairo-street school to discuss.the question of re-organizing the band, with a view to placing it on a more satisfactory, basis. Tea was provided, and afterwards Dr. Hiks and the writer addressed the meeting, hoping to establish a better feeling, and arouse greater enthusiasm. A discussion followed and a considerable accession of members resulted, but at the following annual meet- ing, the Secretar: reported that the attendance of members had gradually fallen off, and that the attempt to organize a good and efficient band on a sound basis had again failed. • Otherwise at this meeting a fairly satisfactory report was forthcoming, the Society having been better supported. The expenses, however, had been specially heavy and the season's working.ended in a loss of £22 15s. 5d., leaving a deficiency of £7 16s. 7d. The accounts included a donation from James Marson, Esq., of £6 10s., the proceeds of the sale of the Orchestral Society's double bass. At the second concert the Misses Wallington and Monks took part, and Mr. F. W. Reynolds played ,Mozart's Concerto in D*minor for piano and orchestra. The secretary reported that all the Music belonging to the Society and late Orchestral Society (which had been handed over to the Musical Society) had been placed in the Public Library under an arrangement with the Museum Committee. The elementary class now numbered 107 members and met in St. Paul's schools ; Mr. Johnson was appointed</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY. HISTORY OF ITS WORK. By Mr. John P. Reynolds.]</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MUSICAL SOCIETY. HISTORY OF ITS WORK. By Mr. John P. Reynolds.]</p>
        <p>[CONTINUED.] The records of the Society are at this time, 1858 to 1862, very meagre. Four concerts appear to have been given in 1861-2, and two, in 18634, but these were not choral. There seems to have been a serious lack of vitality in the Society, and at the annual meet- on February 17th, 1862, it was resolved "That Messrs. Gray and Davison be requested to value the organ -with a view to its being sold as soon. as possible, and that the Committee be requested to dispose of the other property belonging to the Society." This reso- lution was only very partially (if at all) carried into effect, and at a committee meeting on February 13th, 1863, Messrs. Henry White and John Rylands were appointed a sub-committee to arrange a plan for the re-organisation of the Society, and to report to a future meeting, This Committee drew up a new set of rules which were adopted on April 6th,1863. On November Ist, 1865, the rules were again amended. Dr. Hiles, of Manchester, was engaged as conductor, and shortly after Mr. Grindrod, of Stockton Heath, was engaged to teach the Elementary Class, the practices being held in the" Educational School," Newton-street. In September, .1866, the Cairo-street schools not being available, the Public Hall was secured for practices at a guinea per night. and th's arrangement remained in force till October, 1868, when the Wycliffe Hall was taken and used for practices till September, 1871. After this date the Cairo street schools became avail- able and were secured. The first concert given by the Society in the new hall took place on May 15th, 1866, and Mr. Cross was asked to give a resume of the Society's proceedings. The chorus at this time seems to have been but small, the numbers in the advanced -class being : Females 25, males 48, average, 30, and in the elementary class. females 31, males 56, average 48 (fifteen males in theadvanced and 42 in the elementary class were under 15 years of age). This class is referred to in a criticism (in the "Guardian") of the concert of December 17th, 1867, "The elementary class, for youths of both sexes,under the instruction of Mr. Griudrod. is progressing satisfactorily. This is another Proof that the Musical Society is now doing good service to the town and doing it much more effectually than at any previous period of its 33 years existence." The concert on December 17th, 1868. was again the oratorio "Judas Maccabeus," and it was again repeated at popular prices on January 1 lth, 1869, when, as before, a considerable loss resulted. At the annual meeting, STtember 28th, 1860, a new code of rules was adopted and a fresh agreement was made with Or. Hiles, under which he took the risk of loss on the season's working, but .on the other hand. took a large share of ally profits made. The result of this appears to have been a profit the following season of :F-13 10s. 4d. One of the concerts was entirely amateur. It was this year prop sed to get up a bazaar in aid of the Society, but being found impracticable. Mr. L. W. Reynolds, who was elected secretary ia is;;. gave two dissolving views exhibi- tions, which resulted in a profit of about ;£l7. Mr. i Beamont and Mr. Prydr kindly acted as lecturers,. A considerable change was now made in the Rol icy of the Society, it being decided to give four concerts each season, and to obtain the very best' artistes available, and during the next two seasons the following (and others) were engaged :—Mine. Norman Neruda, Mr. Charles Halle, Edward de Jong, Mdlles, Sinico and Rosa Kannanberg ; Signors Foli, Giampi, Vizzani, Bevignani ; Mdlle Louisa Jansen (harpist). Messrs. R. Winn, Siinms Reeves. Edward Lloyd. Maybrick, Santley and Mr. Sainton (violinist), Scc. It is rather a significant fact that during these years the Society was in many ways in a more prosperous posi- t,ion than ever before. At the annual meeting ill 1870 Mr. Charles Crossley, father of our respected conductor Mr. F. H. Crossley, was appointed honorary treasurer, following Mr. John White, and officiated as such until</p>
        <p>1877. On Septcrber 28th, 1869, Mr. Pattison was appointed honorary organist in place of' Mr. Walker, of Manchester, who was engaged during th7e years 1866-9. In September, 1871, the Elementary Class was placed under Mr. T. Mee Pattison (who since 1866, had kindly given his services as honorary accompanist) with a remuneration of 10s. 6d. per Might, but being very poorly .attended it was not continue'd more than three weeks. On April 28th, 1872, over £lOO having been raised specially, the further enlargement and improvement of the organ was decided on, and anorgan recital by Mr. Best was fixed for December 4th, but on trying the organ, Mr. Best found it so incomplete and unsatis- factory. that he refused to play at the last minute, and money taken was returned. A claim was made on Messrs. Gray and Davison, which they settled for £lO 10s., and they gave a guarantee to bring Mr. Best over for a recital free of expense to the Society. On April 21st, 1873, a Sub-committee was appointed to consider the best Plana working the Society during another season, and repprted on August 21st, as follow s (1). That members of the choir be supplied with music at the expense of the Society. (2). That three concerts be given, and the sub- scriptions be reduced to 10s. 6d. reserved seats, and is. 6d. centre gallery (reserved). (3). That the choir be placed under the conductor- ship of Mr. Pattison, as the financial position does not warrant the payment of Dr. Hiles' t rms. This report was fully discussed,but was not adopted, the Secretary being requested to see Dr. Hiles, and ascertain if he would accept a reduced fee or again Share the risk of loss. Dr. Hiks, however, made no offer, but suggested that the season should be shortened, that only two concerts should be given, and be made as much as possible choral. This proposal was adopted, and Dr. Hiles consented to accept £42 for the season's work. The subscriptions were fixed at 10s. dress seats, and ss. centre gallery, non-subscribers paying 7s. 6d. and 4s. respectively, and for side gallery is. ; back seats Is. Later (in February. 1875) pries to non-subscribers were further reduced to ss. dress seats and 3s. centre gallery. At the annual meeting on Septem'ler. 21st. 1875, the accounts showed a deficiency of £52 ss. 7d., including a balance of debt on the organ of £45. During these three years, great progress was made with the chorus, the number of members-being 70, 92, and 111 respectively. Two concerts only were given, but relatively the subscription list was the highest in -the annals of the Society. In December, 1875. the Rector of Warrington, Rev. W. Quekett applied through Dr. Hiles for the Society's assistance at a grand service he contemplated holding in the parish Church on Christmas Eve, when selec- tions of "The Messiah" were to be rendered. The Committee, however, could not see their way to accede to this request, hut left it open to individual members to act as they thought fit. At this time the chorus were practis;ng " The Messiah" for the concert on December 12th. This was ...given with excellent soloists. viz.. Mmes. Blanche Cole, EnriqueZ, Messrs. Henry Guy and Lewis Thomas. It was, however, so badly supported, it became necessary to give ,the second concert almost without professional assistance, and Dr. Hiles obtained the services of several members of the Manchester Choraj Society to take the leading parts in Mendelssohn's " Christus" and Cowen 's "Rose Maiden." This proved successful. and at the annual meeting held on September 19th, 1876, the season was reported as one of the most satisfactory and successful experienced by the Society, a profition the season and on a tea party and amateur concert given in December, being sufficient to wipe out the balance of debt and leave £1 10s. in hand. In October, 1876 Mr. T. Mee Pattison _offered to form an orchestral band in connection with the Society. This offer was gladly accepted by the Com- mittde ; the necessary arrangements were made and in January, 1877, an extra (amateur) concert, in which this band took' part, wa's given under the conductor- ship of Mr. Pattison, with Mr. G. H. Greenwood as vocalist. After this and the first regular concert of the season it appeared likely that there would again be a deficit, so in order to secure better attenthnce at the final concert, three side gallery tickets were sent to each member with an urgent request to sell them and so ensure a; successful concert. This proVed beneficial and at the annual meeting on September 18th, 1877, a fairly favourable report was given, the deficit being only £6 14s. 4d. At this meeting it was announced that Mr. George Woods had kindly pre- sented to the Society the whole of the vocal and instrumental music he purchased for a concert pre- viously given by him in aid of the Infirmary. At this same meeting, Mr. Woods called attention to the fact that there was a sum of money lying at the bank in the mine of Mr. J. Chappell as representing the old Philharmonic Society, and suggested that steps should be taken to procure the transfer of, this balance to the Musical Society. Mr. Pearse also said he believed that Mr. Charles White 'had a small sum in hand in</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
