      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>REPLY</head>
        <p>REPLY</p>
        <p>10, Downing St. Whitehall, S.W. 27 Oct. 1909. ' Dear Sirs,—The fate of the Government's Educa- tion Bills has been'a great disappointment not only to the Free Churches of England and Wales, but but also to his Majesty's Ministers. The prekent 'House of Commons and Government have spared no time or labour in the effort to legislate on this subject. You need not be reminded that the Education Bill of 1906 wtis made the principal measure of the year, or of our renewed endeavours to settle the question in 1907 and 1908. In the first Session of this Parliament justice would have been done to Nonconformists, and popular control of all the public elementary schools of England and Wales would have been an accomplished fact, but for the action of the House of Lords. It is due to no fault of ours that those for whom you speak are disappointed and labour under a sense of real and serious grievance ; and, for our part. I can say that, if no opportunity arises for dealing effectively with the 'subject in this Parliament, we cannot in the next—if we are returned to power--allow the present injustices, and the limitations or absence of'popular control, to continue to deface our educational system. Yours very faithfully, (Signed) H. H. ASQUITH.</p>
        <p>The Rev. Evan Jones:. The Rev. Thomas Law.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>KEEN CONTESTS IN WARRINGTON. Liberals Win Fairfield Ward.</p>
        <p>With contests in six wards, interest in the Warring- ton municipal elections on Monday ran much higher than usual in fact in several of the wards the fights were so keen as to resemble a miniature general election. Except in the Town Hall ward, the issue in every case was a direct one between the Liberals and the Conservatives, and the candidates and their supporters entered into the fray with great energy. The Conservatives made desperate efforts to capture every seat, the local party leaders, as well as a noble lord from an adjoining division and two barristers from Liverpool, taking part in the platform campaign. As a result of the poll, the position of the two parties in the Council Chamber is unchanged, for whereas the Conservatives were victorious in Whitecross Ward by the narrow majority of 13, the Liberals achieved a handsome triumph in Fairfield Ward. The Conserva- tives had a narrow escape from disaster in St.' John's Ward where Mr. Jamts Slictw fought a splendid battle and reduced Councillor Steel's majority from 522 to 49. In the Town Hall Ward Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins, the official Conservative nominee, takes the place of Mr. John Lord, an Independent Conservative who has always refused to be bound by party ties in the Council Chamber. •</p>
        <p>In contrast to the unpleasant conditions with which November is usually ushered in, ' Monday was an eminently favourable day for electioneering and the workers made the most of their opportunities. In the vicinity of the polling booths there was great excite- ment at noon and in the evening, and large crowds of youngsters gathered to cheer or "boo," according to their partizan leanings, as each vehicle brought up,its quota of voters. 'I he, polling arrangements weie complete in every detail and no untoward incident occurred. The candidates were unanimous in their praise of the excellent work done by the Town Hall officials.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>TOWN HALL WARD</head>
        <p>TOWN HALL WARD</p>
        <p>Here the battle did not arouse as much interest as</p>
        <p>the other elections. Mr. John Lord, the retiring representative, had not canvassed either personally or through his agents and he was out of town during the day. He relied entirely upon his record of seven years' services in the Council and left it with his constituents to pass what verdict they thought fit. The only literature issued on his behalf was his address in which he stated that although he was a member of the Conservative party he was opposed by that organization. " I know not why" he added "unless it is that I have always refused to toe the party line in municipal matters. I</p>
        <p>have known for some time that the heads of . the Conservative Party determined to clear nae out of the Council if possible, and the present opf)osition there- fore comes as no surprise." Mr. Jenkins, 'the Con- servative nominee, promised to devote nis efforts especially to the needs of the poor and his well-known interest in all that appertains to the welfare of the children doubtless weighed favourably with the electors. He is, of course. the pioneer and secretary of the Warrington Poor Children's Summer Camp, and Mrs. Ransome, whose husband is the treasurer of that excellent organization, was an active worker on Mr. Jenkins's behalf, bringing up votes to the poll in a governess cart drawn by a handsome little pony. Bills on the walls exhorted the burgesses to" Plump for Jenkins."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>WHITECROSS WARD</head>
        <p>WHITECROSS WARD</p>
        <p>The contest in this. Ward was probably the keenest in its history. Air. J. T. Barton (L), the son of the retiring representative, was oppos&amp;d by Mr. W. 11. Bellian (C), and the Conservatives worked their utmost to achieve victory in this Liberal, stromzhold. Mr. Barton pointed to his long membership of a trade union, and also to the fact that it was on the proposi- tion of his fathefr that all able-bodied workmen in the employ of the Corporation were granted a minimum wage of 21s. per week. The electors were also asked to Vote for Barton and old age pensions " and for " Barton who lives in the ward." Mr. Bellion informed the burgesses that .while he was standing as a Conservative he reserved the right to exercise his ow,t judgment in matters which came, before the Council. A bill issued on the day of the election read" Bellian wants your X for White X." Mr. Bellian undoubtedly had the advantage in the number of motor cars which were placed at his disposal—in fact in this respect he was probably better off than any other candidate in the town. Although the contest was a hard one, the two candi- dates were on the best of terms, and there was an entire absence of unfriendly feeling.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>ORFORD WARD</head>
        <p>ORFORD WARD</p>
        <p>The Conservatives made a determined attempt to wrest this seat from the L;lierals and opinion as to the ultimate result was much divided. Mr. E Cunnington (L), who has done good work for the ward in the past, was opposed by Mr. J. A. Rushworth (C), wkio has previously endeavoured without success to enter the Council. The Citizens' Temperance League inter- vened on Mr. Cunnington's behalf and issued an appeal to the voters to " keep thern publican out." Mr. Rushworth was expected to receive the Roman Catholic vote, the following letter from the Rev. Father Swarbreck being circulated :—" As you (Mr. Rush- worth) have given me your guarantee to always uphold and safeguard the interests of the Catholic voters in the Orford Wsird. and in p4rticular those concerning Catholic education, I have pleasure in recommending the Catholics of the Ward to record their votes for you at the forthcoming election."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>ST. JOHN'S WARD</head>
        <p>ST. JOHN'S WARD</p>
        <p>There was a battle royal in this ward, Councillor T. S. Steel (C.) having a strong opponent in Mr. James Shaw. (L.) who has ,lived in the ward for over 50 years and has done valuable religious and temperance work. It was realized by the Conservatives that no stone would have to be left unturned if their nominee was to escape defeat, and numerous meetings were held on Councillor Steel's behalf. Mr. Shaw refrained from making specific promises but the electors were asked to return A worker for the workers." Much interest had been aroused by the circulation by the Liberal party of a little yellow handbill giving the attendances of Councillor Steel at Council and com- mittee meetings during the past twelvemonths. To this Councillor Steel replied on a large poster headed "Radical trickery exposed." He asked why the opposition had not given his attendances for his term of office—three years—and not for the past twelve months, during a portion of which time he was not, in , good health. Then he pointed out that they had included a year's meetings of some committees, whereas he had only been a member of them for a few weeks. The Liberals issued a counter-handbill asking the voters not to be" misled by the Tory vulgar 1 nguage " and contending that every word they published was "absolutely true and correct in every detail. The figures are from the official return issued by the Town 11411 authorities." _ FAIRFIELD WARD.</p>
        <p>The Liberals had secured an excellent candidate in Mr. C. Jackson Holmes and from the start he was expected to make a fine fight against Mr. G. N. Vernon (C.) who had only been in the Council a few months. Mr. Holmes promised that the street-paving in the ward would receive his special attention if elected, and Mr. Vernon made a point of the fact that he resided in the ward. LATCHFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>The election was very quietly conducted in this ward. Mr. W. J. Wilson (C.) was opposed to Mr. Thomas Hewitt (L) who declared himself in favour of halfpenny car stages, Corporation work by Warrirgton men, a week's holiday and a week's pay for the same for all Corporation workmen. Mr. Wilson pointed to his past work in the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>COUNTING THE VOTES.</head>
        <p>COUNTING THE VOTES.</p>
        <p>At 8 p.m. the polling stations were closed and the ballot boxes were promptly conveyed to the Town Hall where the votes were counted in the Council Chamber and the large committee room. Most of the c .ndidates and some ot the party leaders, as well as several ladies, were prespw. The returning officers were :—The Mayor (Orford Ward), Alderman Smethurst (Town Hall Ward), Alderman John Evhns (actine on behalf of</p>
        <p>_ Alderman Pierpoint for Whitecro.s Ward), Alderman Bolton (Latchford Ward), Alderman James Emns</p>
        <p>(Fairfield Ward) and Alderman Tinnion (St. John's Ward).</p>
        <p>THE RESULTS. The results were announced es follows:</p>
        <p>TOWN HALL WARD. Councillor Jenkins (C) 409 Mr. Lord (Ind.)</p>
        <p>Majority WHITECROSS WA RD. rourcillor flellian (C) 672 Mr. Barton (L)</p>
        <p>Majority</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <p>a beau-ideal hero of the play. Vice-Admiral the Hon. Sir Berkeley Wynne, K.C.B:, is *a study of which Mr. Mark Paton may well be proud. He was always' dignified and never once lost the true quarter deck manner. Major Thessiger is also a prominent character, and he was pourtrayed with spirit and manliness by Mr. • Frederick Leister. In the roles of mischievous " middies," yho' would have delighted the. heart of A Captain. Marryat, Messrs. MeHord and Guy Lanham are excellent. Miss Moira Creegan, as Mrs. Cameron the lady who set things to rights, was clever in all- she did, while we had some graceful acting from Miss Dorothy Ripley and Miss Jessie Winter as Lady' Hermione and Viola Hood. There are several Other roles worthy of soeeial .note, and that of Colonel . McLeod—tub-thumper McLeod—must not be over in a somewhat generalised critique. We- need hardly add that the ladies' dresses were a source of considerable interest to a large proportion of the audience. During the intervals the orchestra under the control of Mr. Norman Latham, played operatic selections it a style which afforded pleasure to the Most critical. "The Merry Widow " was, of course. a Most popular number, and the playiiig of the selection well merited the hearty applause. Mr. I atham is to be congrtu- lated on the hriprovement he has effected in musical matters at 'the. theatre. The' orchestral ,Work, frorn- being a pain has become a pleasure to the listener.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>WARRINGTON CHAMBER OF COMMERCE,</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CHAMBER OF COMMERCE,</p>
        <p>-- At the last meeting of the Council the President (p' Oswald Lotthouse) in the chair, the Secretary (Alder" man Bennett) stated that he had received a communicir' tion from the War Office asking whether it would be to the advantage of manufacturers of pliers and similar tools in the Warrington District if samples of the' articles required from time to time by the Department could be exhibited at the Chamber so that firms wh° were invited to tender might be able to inspect them ell the spot. He was instructed to ascertain the views 05 the principal local tool-makers upon the subject. He also stated that he had received a communication' from the Aldershot Chamber of Commerce asking the Warrington Chamber to support the effort which 00' ate making to alter the existing monopoly in tegard t 1" the supply of goods in the naval and military forces,- and he was requested to write to the Aldershot Chamber intimating that the Council concurred in their proposal. ' The Secretary said that he had received a communica, tion through the Mayor, from the Warrington aal District Branch of the United Kingdom Commercial' Travellets' Association asking whether they Nve,„re eligible for rnembersnip. He explained that toe "-urn' of the Chamber had been specially altered so as 1". meet such cases, and tha,t any local society of A co.rob-: mercial or industrial character might be affiliated wintr the Chamber on payment of an annual subscrieti" to guineas. had" Alderman Bennett likewise annonngd that he - received particulars of the Argentine aad JaPae-- Biitish Exhibitions, and that the President had been invited to join the GenerAl Committee of the latter. Reports had been received of the proceedings of Of. Congress of the Chamiers of Counuerce of the Enspi,r' recently held in Australia, and many important reel.; awls had been passed, the Congress expressing itse very stronßly favour of Preferential Trade, an adei quata contribution by the Colonies to the defence ° toe Empire, an.i the creation of an Imperial Council. 1I</p>
      </div>
