        <p>Oddfellowship. The distribution of the bowling prizes in connectl°,j3t. with the " Haven of Rest" Lodge of the Indepoide ince- Order of Oddfellows (Manchester Unity), too-iicb on Monday evening at the Pelican Hotel. Pro'. 'l' -the- Master G. F. Freaman presided and in presentioglent prizes congratulated the lodge upon the Wep, manner in which they had conducted the 111:0,dic:rt• which was much appreciated by all who toos:,cf 'not Although the lodge was a small one and Ou` the afford very expensive prizes, he had no dotiTe bt.„o_ winners would none the less appreciate them. hoped' pleased to see the lodge making progress, Dna the members would continue to enjoy their ann- • bowling handicaps. The prizes were won as follows • rtlm rt JHaamrryes FAo.gWgo;o4d.hpea.Gd .; .2117. PinGia.inJollvineßbbonw, Arthur Evans; and 6, Bro. James Hinde, The u-:91 votes of thanks were accorded to the Bowling c-;i; mittee and district officers. Songs were 0011°1'0, rendered by P.P.G.M. Boyle, I.P.G.M. ,Lot Brool.o Bro. Bennett and Bro. Mellor: A cornet solo ivssfi,‘ e 5 by Bro. Rutter and a recitation by P.P.C.M. 01- Ma ssey.</p>
        <p>DOES GERMANY MEAN HARM ? et I myself do not believe in any German design t°lljo as a thief in the night. lam pretty confident thsi:ept man on a front bench in either House of ParliarAtie, entertains such an apprenbension. It is creA,leclt enough that German experts have their plans—in— of we are told from Germany of a serious FChertleA descending from :Jutland, if not of making Hollo7o base of operations against the Fatherland ; but 'here told to affirm that no responsible statesman t bere believes that in any responsible German quartØeY are spoliatory designs against ourselves.— Lord "Court of Penwith in the November" Contemporary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>AIMIlisi•••••••••••••••ImmimmelemomilmlINIMIIIIid6</head>
        <p>AIMIlisi•••••••••••••••ImmimmelemomilmlINIMIIIIid6</p>
        <p>ORFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>Councillor Cnnoington (L.) 565 Mr. Rushsvorth (C.)</p>
        <p>Majorsty ST. JOHN'S WARD.</p>
        <p>Councillor St.-el (C.) 693 Mr. Shaw (L.) 644</p>
        <p>Majority FAIRNIF.LD WARY). Councillor Holmes (L.) 613 Mr. Vernon (C.)</p>
        <p>Majority L.I.TCHFORD WARD.</p>
        <p>Councillor Wilson (C.) Mr. Hewitt (L.)</p>
        <p>Majority At the close of the counting in the C umcil Chamber Councillor CIisISINOTON prov)ssd a vote of thanks to the returning officer (the Mayor). This was seconded by Mr. RusHWORTH, who said the election had been fought on gentlemanly lines. The MAYOR, in reply, congratulated Mr. Cunnington on his return, and sympathized with Mr. Rushworth on his defeat.</p>
        <p>Councillor WitsoN moved a vote of thinks to Alder- men Bolton, and the presiding officer of the ward and his staff, and said the election had been the pleasantest he had ever been engaged in. He and Mr. Hewitt had been good friends and he hoped that friendship would continue for many yea.. (Heir, hear). Mr. HEWITT seconded, and the motion was carried.</p>
        <p>Alderman BovroN : if you are satisfied I tun. Councillor STExt, pr)p )sed a vote of thanks to Alderman Tinnion and the Town Clerk and all who bad been responsible for the day's poll. The contest had been a very keen one, and there had been a great deal of excitement, outside the two candidates—( laughter)— but the arrangements had been perfect, and every courtesy had been received from the teturninst Officer and the Town Clerk and his staff, and the presiding officers. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. SHAW seconded, and said that there was a pro lase f him of bettter things. His worthy opponent hid had a foeman worthy of his steel. (Litt ;hter). The motion VMS carried, and in reply, Alder can TINNION S tid he had never been engsged in a plesssnter contest, and he hoped all future contests in the war would be fought in the same gentlem4nly manner, and in the same kindly spirit. (Hear, hear). C nincillor HOLMES, in inovin: a vote of thanks to Alderman James Evans, also complimented the officials on the courtesy they had displa3 ed. With regard to Mr. Vernon and himself, no election could have been fought on pleasanter or more agreeable lines, and if be had any regret it was that he had defeated a good man. Mr. VERNoN seconded, and referred to the impartiality which hsd been displayed by the officials during the election and also acknowledged the courtesy of his opponent. In reply, Aldorman JAMES EVANS said the election had been very amicably fought, and he was gratified with the expressions of opinions which had been given. In the No. I Committee Room. Councillor JF.SKINS thanked the officials for Town Hall Ward who had so carefully conducted the election and said he could not disguise the fact that he would much rather win than lose. Air. Lord was not present. Alderman SAIETHURif briefly replied. Councillor BELLIAN proposed a vote of thanks to the offi3ials for Whitecross Ward.</p>
        <p>Mr. BART3N, in w.conding, observed that Councillor Bellian hid fought like a gentleman. Alderman EVANS acknowledged the compliment. DECLARING THE RESULTS.</p>
        <p>A large crowd had assembled to hear the declaration of the results from the Town H4ll steps, end the flpures were received with loud cheers.</p>
        <p>Conncillor JENKINS said In wihel to thank all those who had supported him that day. He intended to do his best to represent them faithfully. (Cheers). Councillor IIF.LmAs also thanked all those electors wh ) had voted on his behalf, and all who had worked for him, for the manner in .wAch they had conducted the election. At the swim time, he would also like to extend his best wishes to his opponent for the sportsmanlike manner in which he had fought the elec- tion. (Cheers'. He was certain that they were none the less friends now than they were before the contest besan. (Cheers). Wuncillor CUNNINGTON also thanked the electors of Orford Ward for the manner in which they)iad supported him. __ _</p>
        <p>Counlinr C. JACKSON HOLMES heartily than'ted the Fairfield. Ward eleotors.</p>
        <p>Councillor WiLsos s-incerely thanked all those Nsho had supplrted him. He would endeavour to carry out all his promises, and perform all the duties of his office to all parties irrespective of political feelings. (Cheers). After the declaration of the poll. there was POMO speech-making at the Conservative Club. The Liberal candidates visited their respective committee rooms and thanked their workers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. "The Flag Lieutenant."</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE. "The Flag Lieutenant."</p>
        <p>One of the best plays, and one of the most accom- plished companies seen in Warrington for a long time, have occupied the boards at the Royal Court Theatre this week. ", The Flag Lieutenant " has deservedly had a long run of successes, and has been twice witnessed by the King, while only on Monday it was revived in London by Mr. Cyril Maude, who has had a lot to do with the selection of the Company now performing in Warrington. As the title suggests the play has a strong naval flavour. The setting of it is mostly at Malta, and in the background -there is to be seen the shimmering of the water and the lights of the fleet of which the " Royal Edward " is the flagship. On board the latter all is light and Merri- ment, and the swish of skirts to the strains of seduc- tive music is heard. The plays , opens in the Admiral's after-cabin, where most of the characters present themselves in comedy to the audience. There is much light chaff and sparkling dialogue. 'I ben the scene changes. The Admiral receives 'a call for help from a beleaguered British force at Kandia in the near East, and sends Major Thessiger of the Marines, "Dick " Lascelles, the flag lieutenant, and a company of marines and tars as reinforcPments. At Kandia tragedy and comedy stalk hand-in,hand and we are permited to see-a most realistic attack on the garrison by the Bashi-Bazouks. How the plucky little force is eventually relieved, and the heroic manner of it, and the parts played by Lascelles and Thessiger, we must leave the play itself to explain. Things get somewhat inVolved, and a woman's love and dexterity are needed to ; straighten them out when the arts of peace are once more resumed. The denouement takes place on the ,fore-deck of the " Royal Edward " and a veil effective scene it is, with the mingling of uniforms and gay feminine apparel. There is rather a big cast for the play, and we are n,ot exaggerating when we say that from the "lead " to the "walker-on" there was not a weak character. Mr. Edgar Kent, as the flag lieutenant, greatly distinguishes himself in a role demanding not only buoyancy and humour, but real histrionic ability. " Dicky " Labcelles was a rare scapegrace whom no one would take seriously, but he was a hero at heart and proves it before the end. Mr. Kent is not wanting in any respect, and made</p>
        <p>EYESIGHT HINTS. ci• o"casional dimness of vision is a sign of defect which needs careful diagnosis. "e_bich of the eyes, and a sense of having to FORCE them to do 'heir work, are troubles should lean to consultation with us. OUR CHARGES ARE LOW. ' JOHNSTON WATSON Eyesight Specialist, 84, BRIDGE ST., WARRINGTON Ansm Hours of of Business 9.30 to 7.30. Saturday 9.:30 to 9. OTE Johnston Watson attends on Monday and Tuesday from 11 to 4.30, Wednesday 11 to 5'3 ---. Thursday 11 to 1, Friday and Saturday 11 to 5,30, or by appointment.</p>
        <p>tg* PLEASE N</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>OUR FOOTBALL PHOTO PRIZE SCHEME.</head>
        <p>OUR FOOTBALL PHOTO PRIZE SCHEME.</p>
        <p>i••=•••••immiNlNlM</p>
        <p>SOP</p>
        <p>ringtorienthusiasts had been indulging in fond dreams of heavy scoring by Fish and Co., at C vendish Park but the actual reality was much different. Barrow played a surprising game—quite their best of the season—i-and if the Warrington men had not been in really good trim they would have sustained a severe reverse. Twice were Barrow in front on the score sheet, and it was only by the most resolute play that Warrington emerged triumphant in the end by 9 points to 7. Even in the last two minutes a penalty kick by Logan only missed the goal by inches so that Warrington had nothing to spare when hostilities ended.</p>
        <p>Last season Warrington ran up 26 points oft the Barrow ground, and in all probability they felt a trifle too superior when operations commenced on Satur- day. Anyhow they were clearly second best up to the interval. The-first "forty " saw Barrow as active as cats, and the Wathngton defence has rarely had a busier time. The wonder is that the score in favour of the home lot was not larger. Warrington had few chances this half. From. one -of them Dickenson and Fish engineered the usual try.</p>
        <p>In the second period the Warrington men clearly realized that they had met foes who could not be trifled with. The forwards put their backs ' into the work and broke up the home front rank in fine style, while there was some smart work behind. The second try came from O'Neill who took an inside pass from Frawley on his finger-tips, tuckbd the oval under his drm and bluffed a trio of defenders before ground- ing safely for a really artistic try. Then the Barrow men got a second goal, and Warrington were again in arrears. Hot work followed, and finally Fish pulled the game out of the fire with a characteristic try. All the day he had played a great game, and the winning of the match was a triumph for him.</p>
        <p>As indicated Barrow played really brilliant football and would have beaten most teams. Fortunately, Warrington were also in their best humour, and having the power in the pack and a little extra class behind they proved just equal to the task. But it was an exciting business. One of the best men on the field was O'Malley, who gave his finest display in the Warrington colours and worried the home backs from beginnipg to end. Ile seemed absolutely untiring. The halves also. played better and were euperior to the opposing pair. Brooks gave s,igns of coming back to form. Frawley seemed strange at centre, but be did some clever things and only needs time and encouragement to train on. A word for Tilley. On the defence he was fully the equal of Gifford, the County full back and saved Warrington repeatedly.</p>
