      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>RUGBY FOOI93ALL</head>
        <p>RUGBY FOOI93ALL</p>
        <p>THE NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>Results of Saturday's Matches.</p>
        <p>YORKSH LRE SENIOR CUP—SEcoND ROUND.</p>
        <p>G. T. P. G. T. P. , Huddersfield Batley ICeighley Halifax LANCASHIRE SENIOR CUP—SECOND ROUND. Leigh 9 3 13 Raiford Wigan Runcorn Swinton , NORTHERN RUGBY LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>Hull 5 628 Bramley Warrington Hull Kingston R. 2 4 16 Bradford Merthyr Tydvil 1 2 8 Ebbw VaN3</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>Warrington's Narrow Squeak at Barrow.</head>
        <p>Warrington's Narrow Squeak at Barrow.</p>
        <p>The Warrington men anticipated an easy victory at Barrow, as the Furness men have had a poor season so far, but they found the home lot on their mettle and only pulled the game out of the fire in the closing stages. Warrington were not quite at full strength for Taylor was unable to piny. Frawley took the centre position with Bradshaw on the wing. Barrow were fully represented. Beautiful weather favoured the future, and there was a crowd of about 3,000. Teams and officials :</p>
        <p>Warrington: Tilley, back ; Bradshaw' Frawley, Dickenson and Fish, threequarter backs; Brooks and Jenkins, half backs; O'Neil, O'Malley, Thomas, Board- man, Belton and Shugars, forwards. Barrow: H. Gifford, full back ' • S. Treloar, G. Dobson, W. Barnes and R. Bamber' threequarter backs; E. Fallowfleld and B. Rushton, half backs; J. Thompson, J. Morgan, A. Logan, J. Brockbank, S. Little and A. Hannah, forwards. Referee, Mr. Farrer, Keighley. Touch judges, Messrs. W. Cross, Preston, and J. R. Robinson, Ulverston. Barrow opened in very dashing style and forced Warrington to defend. Their speedy forward work resulted in Dickenson and Tilley having hard work to clear the line. Jenkins ran back to half-way, only to see Gifford field well and kick to Warrington quarters, where Frawley marked cleverly in reply. The game was being contested at a furious pace, and Boardman threw forward when clear. Still Warrington were called upon to defend. Smart following up by Hannah caused Tilley no end of trouble, Warrington being pinned on their line. Scrummages were formed on the Warrington line, and only fine defence by Tilley and Brooks kept them out, Barrow being almost over on two or three occasions. A mark by Tilley relieved the situation somewhat. Treloar lost ground through kicking into touch, and for a short time Warrington got to the Barrow half This was only momentarily, as Rushton kicked and Bamber was fouled, a penalty being awarded. Gifford kicked a goal. Hannah and Dobson were prominent until a free kick relieved Warrington. The latter attempted a passing move- ment, in which Dickenson and Brooks wore prominent, but Frawley knocked on. Bradshaw cross-kicked, and in the race for the ball, Troloar touched down. Tilley was having any amount of work to do, and did it well. Never this season had Barrow been seen to such advantage. The ball was kicked across to Bamber' who took it cleverly, andlbeating all opposition scored at the corner, Logan failing at goal. Warrington improved splendidly and fine forward work caused Barrow to fall back. Eventually the Warrington three-quarters combined effectively, and after Dicken- son had had a futile shot at goal, he made an opening for Fish, who after beating Treloar and then Gifford, scored a capital try. Thomas's kick at goal failed. Fish soon had another go, but this time was grassed, and followling a pick up and kick by Gifford, the Barrow side made another att ick, and limber was away beauti- fully. when he put, his foot into touch. Fish got hold, and Treloar pushed him into touch a few feet off his own line. Warrington had a narrow escape, for the ball rolling over the lina, B amber was only robbed of a try by Tilley making dead. Barnes was away in fine style, but came to grief over Frawley. Then Dobson got away with Fallow field in close attendance. They had only Tilley to boat when Fallowfield knocked on. Fish weat away at a rare pace, hut Treloar forced him into _touch. The game was still as fast as ever, with Barrow quite holding their own. Half-time :--Barrow, one goal one try (five points) ; Warrington,- one try (three points). Crossing over, Warrington forwards forced the pace and the ball was taken with a rush well into the home half. Then Frawley passed to Bradshaw, and the latter made off, but lost possession, A long kick took the</p>
        <p>hall over the half-way. when %tuber secnred the bill and ran. Bradshaw, however tackled him. Next O'Malley came away, and after a bout of passing Neil got over at the corner. The kick at goal by 'lliomas Jailed. Warrington now had a lead of one odd point. Some exciting play was seen in the Warrington half, Barrow having the better of it, but the defence was keen. Logan took a penalty kick and landed a splendid goal amid cheers. Barrow had now the odd point lead. Keenness was the prominent feature in the play of both sides, every inch of ground being fought. Fol- lowing scrambling play' the ball went out to Dicken- son, and he passed to Fish, who ran in at the corner. The kick at goal failed badly. It was now Warrington who had the lead with a couple of points in their favour. A few moments of quietness was followed by a posing movement between the Warrington three- quarters and Fibh looked like do!ng something. He however came to grief at Gifford, who ran and kicked. A penalty was given against Warrington, and in a favourable position, Gifford tried at goal. His kick failed, and a chance of equalizing was lost. Play was then contested in the Barrow half. Short kicks by the visitors' for wards helped them along, and then Barrow had the Lest of some scrambling play. The visiting forwards came away again with a rush. Gifford was alert, and got away, but &amp;tuber, to whom he passed, was forced into touch. Result :—Warrlngton, three tries (nine points) ; Barrow-, two goals one try (seven points).</p>
        <p>Lancashire Cup ST. HELENS UNLUCKY Ar SWINTON.</p>
        <p>At Swinton, before 7,C00 spectators. The home side were without Johnson, for whom Wallwork was played, while the" Saints" were short of Barton; whose place Charles Creevey acted. A really fine game of the cup- tie order resulted. Keenness was the order, and the tackling was sure and safe throughout. In the first half of the game the home forwards dominated the pro: ceedings, nt their efforts found poor support. At the interval the sides were on level terms. Alter changing ends Swinton forced the pace, and owing to obstruction by one of the " Saints " Swinton were awarded a penalty allowance. H. Davies took the kick, and landed a fine goal, which set the seal on the match. Although the visitors attacked hotly towardi the close they could not break through the home defence, and Swinton retired lucky winners of a well-fought game. Result : —Swinton, 1 goal (2 points). St. Helens nil. Teams : Swinton—Wallwork, back ; H. Davies, Parker' E. Young, and James Wharton, three-quarter backs; Jack Wharton and Fairhurst, half hacks ; W. Preston, Neon, Cullis, Dan Davies, Gartroll, and Pollitt, forwards. St. Helens—Turtill, back ; Prescott, C. Creevey, J Greenwood and Flanagan, three-quarter backs; Briers' Helsby, Lee, Bradley, Pope, and Mavitty, forwards. Referee, Mr. Arnott Smith, Halifax. RUNCORN'S BRILLIANT DEFEAT OF OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn, before 5.000 spectators, Runcorn were early on the (Mack following a grand bit of-passing by Flanagan and Butterworth, but Csash and Llewollyn transferred play to midfield. Padbury and Ferguson each had a shot at goal without success. Oldham gradually worked their way to the Runcorn line, where Anlezaik, getting possession from the pack. threw out to White, who dashed over before the home backs could recover themselves, Wood adding a goal. Resum- ing, Kennedy sent the ball well into the Oldham half. Following a scrummage, Watton passed to Butter- worth, who scored at the corner, Padnury faiiing with the goal kick. Before the interval White got the ball from the pack, and handed to Watton, who ran to the line, and passed back to White, who scored in grind fashion, Butterworth, however, failing at goal. Half- time—Runcorn 2 tiles (6' points), Oldhaml goal I try (5 points). After the interval the Oldham Custodian was caught in possession, and a moment later Atilezark was forced to touch down. Resuming, Tyson made a desperate efforts to break through but was stopped by Moran. Ferguson then had a shot at goal, the ball falling just outside. Exciting play followed in the Oldham half, where from a scrummage White passed out to Watton, who dashed over in brilliant fashion, Richardson failing to improve: Result :—Runcorn 3 tries (9 points), Oldham 1 goal 1 try (5 points). Teams and officials -- Runcorn—Richardson, • back; Flanagan, Moran, Butterworth and Unsworth, three-quarter backs ; White and Watton half backs: Padbury, Walker, Kennedy, Wood, Smith and McDonald. forwArds. Oldham—Wood; hack ; Tyson, Dean, Llewellyn, and Cash, three-quarter backs: Anlezark and White, half back; Avery, Jardine,A. Smith, Helm, Owens and Ferguson, forwards. Referee, Mr. F. Renton. Hunslet. Touch judges. Messrs. A. E. Cregson, Salford, and E.Tonge, Swinton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>NOTES BY " OVALIST."</head>
        <p>NOTES BY " OVALIST."</p>
        <p>The" Wirepullers " still bead the League, but there are clouds on the near horizon which indicate the approach of trouble. I might venture on another prophecy this week, and say thatby to-night Warring- ton will have been deposed from the leadership, but with every hope of regaining it before the New Year. To-day's engagement is at Oldham—ever a grave for Warrington's brightest hopes. Only once, I think, in their history have Warrington won at Watersheddings, and they.have frequently lost heavily. List season they went down 18-0 at Oldham, after having beaten the Spinners 27-0 in that memorable Monday evening match at Warrington. All I expect to-day is that Warrington will improve on their last figures at Oldham. The team should be capable of getting a point or two, and given any luck they may shake the- homesters up considerably. Oldham have the faster back division aud that may turn the scale.</p>
        <p>The League table up to date is as follows For. Agst. -</p>
        <p>Per-</p>
        <p>P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. . P. centage Warrington 9 7 1 1...12 28 108...10 12 56... 83-33 Runcorn 8 6 1 15 67...11 12' 58... 8125 Hull KR 11 9 2 0...37 31 167...17 24 106... 8181 Oldham 8 6 2 0...15 33 129... 9 7 39... 7500 Wakefi'd T 7 5 2 0...12 17 75... 5 9 37... 7137 7 5 2 0...16 17 83...11 11 55... 7142 Leeds Leigh Buddersfrd 9 6 3 0...15 29 117...13 15 71... 66.66 St. Helens 8 4 2 2...21 .29 129...13 23 95... 6250 Keig hley 8 5 3 0...18 24'108...13 13 65... 62.50 Hunslet Salford Swinton huh lO 6 4 0...21 29 129...14 23 97... 60.00 Batley 9 5 4 0...23 18 100...12 10 51... 5555 Wigan 7 3 4 0...13 17 77... 9 13 57... 42135 Barrow Bradford N 9 3 0 6... 9 8 42...13 22 92... 3333 Widnes Broughton 9 3 6 0...19 21 101.. 25 35 155... 3333 Dewsbury 8 2 5 1... 8 10 46... 9 22 84... 31'25 Ebbw Vale 9 2 6 1... 7 14 56...17 17 85... 2717 York Rochdale 7 1 6 0.. 3 13 45...15 25 105... 1428 Merthyr Bramley Treherbert 6 0 6 0... 2 9 31...19 28 123...100'00</p>
        <p>'Runcorn are pretty sure to go to the top again, seeing that they have only Swinton as opponents at Runcorn. Hull Kingston Rovers go to Leeds, so that their chances are not particularly rosy. Oldham are likely to jump into second place.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire Cup second round provided some rare games last Saturday, and the greatest of all was at Runcorn, where the chirpy Linnets once more upset the pretensions of Oldham. The latter were a much fancied side I tipped them myself—but Runoorn played just as well in the dry as they had done in the wet the week before, and they beat Oldham at all points. We all know the worrying game Runcorn can play on their own peculiar patch. Well, they worried Oldham into impotency and showed considerable trickiness in the bargain. Seeing that Jolley and Harrison were both absentees, I think Runcorn's performance is the most meritorious of the season.</p>
        <p>One result of the Runcorn cup-tie victory is that Warrington will be deprived of their attractive fixture next Saturday when the Linnets were due at Wilderspool. Instead, they will be engaged with Leigh in the semi-final at Mather-lane. Warrington's only solatium will be one-third of Runcorn's share of the Leigh gate.</p>
        <p>In place of the Runcorn match, Warrington have reversed dates with Merthyr, and the, Welshmen will be the visitors to Wilderspool next Saturday.</p>
        <p>Warrington will have their strongest force in the field to-day and do not mean to give up the ghost at Oldham without a struggle. As there are some doubts about Frawley having completely recovered from his injury, he has been placed upon the" A "team, and Taylor and Bradshaw will form the right wing.</p>
        <p>The team is : Tilley, full back ; Fish, Dickenson, Taylor, and Bradshaw, threequarters ; Brooks and Jenkins, half backs; Boardman, O'Malley, O'Neill, Thomas, Shugars and Belton, forwards.</p>
        <p>Oldham will also be at full strength, G. W. Smith's suspension having expired. The New Zealander, notwithstanding his 37 years undoubtedly strengthens the back division.</p>
        <p>The League matches for to-day with the results of corresponding games last season, are as follow :- 29 2 Salford v. Barrow. 14— 8 Huddersfield v. Batley. 8— 5 Bradford v. Hunslet. 67-10 Wakefield Trinity v. Bramley. 15-10 Broughton Rangers v. Wigan. -- Dewsbury v. Merthyr Tydvil. 2 3 Treherbert v. Ebb Vale. 7-41 Keighley v. Halifax. 25-10 Hull v. York. 8— 5 Leeds v. Hull Kingston Rovers. 12— 3 Runcorn v. Swinton. 14— 3 Leigh v. St. Helens. _ 18— 0 Oldham v. Warrington.</p>
        <p>There will be a battle royal at Wilderspool between the Warrington and Oldham "A " teams. The Oldham men fairly thrashed Warrington " A " on their own ground last season, and were the only team to do so. I have no doubt they will make the locals go all the way to-day and much will depend on the way the forwards do their work. The Oldham backs -will be as good as our own. Last season they were better. The "A" team seem to have the same fault as the first—lack of speed in the inside positions.</p>
        <p>The " A " team will be itantyre, full back; Smith, Cartwright; Landor and Frawley, three-quarters ; Lloyd and Daintith, half backs; Harmer, Chesters, Naylor, Boardman, Bradshaw and. Gibbs, forwards.</p>
        <p>It has been reported in the Manchester papers that Stuntz had left Marseilles, and was due here this week end in company with another Australian three-quarter named Brown, who was going to Bartow. The Warrington officials place no credit in either rumour and do not expect Stuntz for nearly another fortnight.</p>
        <p>There was rather a shock at Wilderspool on Satur- day last when the half-time message from Barrow save 5-3 in favour of the Furness men. The War-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>lib</head>
        <p>lib</p>
        <p>Northern Union County Championship. YORKSHIRE BEAT LANCASHIRE.</p>
        <p>If the attendance at this re.atch at Hull on Thursday was anything to go by little interest was taken in the game, as only a very small number of people were present. Yorkshire were the first to ECOTO through a place kick by Devereux. This early reverse seemed to inspire Lancashire to force the game and Kennedy scored an unconverted try. Lancashire appeared now to be the masters of the situation, for Miller scored another unconverted try for them. This was soon followed by another try by Define which Clarkson goaled. This quick scoring roused the Yorkshirernea, and they were not long in passing the Lancashire lead. Eccles scorcd a try and Devereux placed a goal for Yorkshire. Just before the interval Eccles scored an unconverted try. At the interval Yorkshire led by sixteen points to eleven. In the secend half Yorkshire were seen to great advantage, and ,put on eleven points to Lancashire's three. Early on Eccles wound up a fine bout of passing by scoring at the corner for 'Yorkshire. Then Smith added a try, which Eccles converted. Gallop, after a splendid run, scored for Lancashire, the goal kick failing. In the closing stages Boylen added a try, and though Davis made a good attempt at the goal kick, he just failed,</p>
        <p>- Yorkshire Lancashire.. Fourteen points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE COMBINATION</p>
        <p>Oldham "A " — 10 Wigan Highlield 2 0 '1 Wigan "A " - R'dale Hor. "A " 0 0 0 Runcorn "A " --: —l5 Leigh Shamrocks —— 11 Warrington "A " —— 36 Pemberton WARRINGTON "A" V. PEMBERTON</p>
        <p>There was a crowd of quite 4,000 people to witness the " A " team match with Pemberton at Wilderspool, and they wore rewarded with a very bright display by the Champions. On the visiting side were Treharne (late of Warrington) and Green (late of Wigan), but they were no match for the local Juniors. Pine passing by Warrington soon broke down the defence, and Landor ran round the posts, for Mclntyre to goal. Warrington continued to show superior tactics, and after Treharne had been bowled over near the quarter flog Cartwright made a commendable effort. Splendid exchange kicking went in favour of Warrington, and after Green hadshown .up for the visitors, Smith placed Warrington on the aggressive. The ball was worked across to Smith who put on the finishing touch to a series of clever exchanges, Mclntyre placing a goal from near the touch-line. Cartwright next got over for Warrington, Mclntyre gosling, and Smith again broke through the defence in clever fashion. Half-time : Warrington, 18 points; Pemberton, nil. Turning round, Warrington main- tained the advantage throughout, and after Mclntyre had placed a goal the Wirepullers scored at their leisure. The passing of the winners was exceptionally flue and tries were scored by Cartwright, Smith,Chesters and Jordan, Mclntyre placing two goals. Final: Warrington, six goals eight trios (36 points) ; Pemberton, nil.</p>
        <p>OTHER MATCHES. RYLANDS' RECS V. THATTO HEATH. Rylands' Rocs entertained Thatto Heath in a friendly match at Gersey-laue on Saturday last, and a very good game ensued, both teams being well represented. After the ball bad been knocked about for 10 minutes. Leah fur Rylands' received the ball from Derbyshire, and by passing and re-passing with Gallop for fully three-quarters of the length of the field secured a try. The visitors now attacked and from a bit of forward work Leah again got the ball and made good use by registering another try. A few minutes later Thatto Heath with a forward rush crossed the line and a goal was added to the try thus secured. Wright was successful in adding another try for Rylands' which McGowan converted. Just before half-time the home team were penalised and Thatto Heath easily got a goal. Half-time result:— Rylands' Rees, 11 points ; Thatto Heath, 7 points. On turning round the home team again had the best of the game, Horracks, Broadhurst and Gallop each scoring tries but McGowan was only able to convert one of them. Thatto Heath were unable to add to the interval score and the results of the game was :—Rylands' Rees, 22 points; Thatto Heath, 7 points. RYLANDS' RECS. No. 2 v. WIDNES WHITE STAR. lands' Rees No. 2 played Widnes White Star away on Saturday last, and although the out standing feature of the game was the brilliant play on the part of Blong for the home team, were succes3ful in orrying off the honour of the day by 7 points to nil.</p>
        <p>PEARSON AND KNoWLES V. STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Pearson and Knowles' visited Stockton Heath on Saturday last and a keenly fought game rt suited in a win for the home team by 6 points to 3.</p>
        <p>MATCUES FOR TO-DAY (SATURDAY).</p>
        <p>Rylacds' Rees v. Denton Green, at Gorsey-lane• Rylands' Rees team is as follows :—McGowan, full back ; Wright, liorncks, Massey and Furness, three- quarters ; Floyd (captain) and Gallop, half backs ; Bwadhurst, Mason, Fillington, Stretford, Robertson and Britten, forwarde.</p>
      </div>
