        <p>LIVERPOOL AND DISTRICT LEAGUE. WARRINGTON ALBIONS V. SCTTON S. ANNE'S.</p>
        <p>Warrington Albions entertained Sutton St. Anne's last Saturday on the Liverpool-road ground and defeated them by a margin of 3 golls, the score being 5 to 2. The teams were as follows: Warrington Albions :—Makin, goal; Serge int and Short,full backs; Whitfield, Dickenson and Dilworth, half backs; Cheaters, Whitfield, Thomas, Hunter and Ritchie, forward. Sutton St. Anne's, Horrocks, goal; Carlin and Tetlow, full backs; Padiliand, J. Ann, Sherlock, half backs; T. Waters, amace, Cross, Sneyd and Liptrot, forwards. The match %iris very keenly fought until within a few minutes of the close of the game. At the outset the visiting tour] pressed and within three minutes of the start Caunce with a well directed shot. from about 20 yards range defeated Makin, the Albions' custodian. The home team roused by this reversa made a splendid effort to get through but the St. Anne's backs managed to elms, only to be tried half a minute later when Tetlow succeeded in stopping with his chest a powerful shot from Ritchie, much to his discomfiture. There was some give and take play and then the Anions got in the visitors' goal, but Tetlow cleared,the ball going in the direction of Chesters who seizing tbe opportunity rushed into the goal mouth but Horrocks cleared, hurting himself in the attempt. The ball was soon at the other end of the field and Makin saved two shots in quick succession. ' Shortly afterwards Cheaters made an attempt for goal but the ball glanced off the posts and was soon in neutral territory. Liptrot managed to get past the Albions' backs and it seemed inevitable that another goal would be recorded for St. Anne's, but Short with a sprint intercepted and the ball was soon in the centre of the field. Not to be denied the visitors pressed and even beat all the home team except the goal keeper who ran out, grasped the ball, punched it for all he was worth and play was again in the enemy's quarter. The Albions' forwards displayed fine combination, which Ritchie marred when he tried for goal owing to the kick being so weak. No sooner was the ball in the field again than the home forwards made their way to the Saints' goal and Thomas sent in a fine shot, the ball passing lortween Horrocks' legs. Just before half time Thomas added another goal and the scoring up to the interval was :—Warrington Albions 2 goals; Sutton St. Annes 1 goal. Resuming the Albions got the ball practically to themselves and Ritchie swung it round to Cheaters who made good use of it, ridding another goal for the Albions to the delight of the spectators. With the advantage of a penalty, Waters very easily secured the necessary. A few minutes later, after hard work on the part of both teams, Ritchie sent in a good slrot and Horrocks in making an attempt to save, fell. Cheaters took up the attack and the n. stopped a tinging shot from him. Two minutes off time, Whitfield added another goal, the result being : Warrington Albions 5; Sutton St. Annes 2.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>THE LANCASHIRE AND CHESHIRE ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>The half•yearly meeting and the annual dinner and distribution of prizes of the Lancashire and Cheshire Amateur Bowling Association were held on Saturday at the Albion Hotel, Manchester, Mr. Coombes presiding in the unavoidable absence of Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P. Some very important business was transacted at the afternoon meeting, the chief item being an application on behalf of the existing Cheshire Association to be affliated as a distinct oonnty in the British Crown Green Association. Messrs. J. Darbyshire, E. Knowles, and the hon. secretary, Mr. T. W. Cowley, attending to expound tbe views of the Cheshire bowlers. . . .</p>
        <p>These were well received, and after further consider- ation the resolution proposedcby Mr. James Knowles, and seconded by Mr. J. Lewehwaite, that the proposed new County Association of Cheshire be acceded to, and that the bye-laws be altered accordingly, was unanimously agreed to, and the wish expressed that Cheshire would prove itself worthy of honour. The prizes won during the season were as follows : Individual Merit Competition: Tom Glover, 1 ; H. Arnold, 2 • J. Brooks. 3 ; J. Weave, 4; J. Antwiss, 5; E. Knowles, 6; Bedforth, 7; T. B. Burgess, 8. Championship Cup and gold medals: Warrington Cemetery, 1; Leigh Railway, 2. Silver Medals, Association Competition : Pendleton Woolpack, 1; Irlarns-o'-th'-lleight, 2. Second Team Competition: Whitefleld Subscription, 1; Warrington Cemetery, 2. Gold medals, hest average : Yorkshire match, Alfred Scott; Staffordshire match, Tom Ellison. Special gold medals for best wins in deciding county tie with Yorkshire—at Yorkshire, J. Brooks, 21-5 ; at Flixton G. VV. Corner, 21-12.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>FIFTY GUINEA TROPIIY FOR BAKERS.</head>
        <p>FIFTY GUINEA TROPIIY FOR BAKERS.</p>
        <p>It has been said quite truly that competition develops trade. Incidentally it was remarked that it tends towards perfection. It will interest eaters of eholemeal bread to learn that the Natural Food Co., are offering monthly prizes to bakers of Allinson bread and also a fifty guinea silver trophy for competition among the monthly prize-winners. A prize of five guineas and a handsome framed Diploma for exhibition will be awarded every month until further notice to the baker Kilo sends the cost specimen of Allison loaf of his own baking. A prize of one guinea also will be awarded to the prize-winner's foreman. In addition to the above monthly prize there will be a ipecial com- petition every January open to the prize-winners at the monthly competitions during the previous year, the awbrd consisting of a handsome silver challenge trophy value fifty guineas. This trophy will be awarded for the best loaf sent in. The names and addresses of the prize-winners will be published in the chief trade papers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>FOOTBALL GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>BY B. BENNISON.</p>
        <p>ILLUSTRATED BY JACK DODWORTH,</p>
        <p>One cannot be absolutely certain what any team will do at the end of the second month of the season; but in these days, when it is the fashion to strike comparisons with the idea of finding, if possible, whether football to-day is as good as it was, say, ten years ago, it is inte- resting to turn to and study the perform- ances of Glossop, though they be only members of the Second Division of the League. I will tell you why I think Glossop attracts the attention of the student of the game; they have so fat proved beyond question, I think, that the team that will do well is one that Is built up by ama- teurs and professionals. If one takes the Corinthians of to-day—and by Corinthians I speak of the public school boys generally—l do not think they play so well as their predecessors; but at the present time—and Glossop proves this to be true—if you can get a team which includes a fair sprinkling of amateurs you are sure to reap success. The amateur style is distinctive and individualistic; it makes for personal specu- lation; that is, the amateur who has learnt his football lessons well takes the shortest cut for goals, and he is imbued with much confidence in his own ability. With the help of the profes- sional, his game, though always characteristic of himself, becomes polished, and the two make a desirable combination. Now, in the Glossop- team there are three amateur forwards—there</p>
        <p>were four until T. T. Fitchie left with the PH- grims for America—Raine, Stapley, and Sharpe. These three men are, to all intents and purposes, the Glossop front rank, and that they have done and are doing extremely well is shown by the exalted position which the Derbyshire club oc- cupy. But Ido not think they would have, been so effective if they were not backed .up by pro- fessional half-backs. My ideal team is one that has good, tear-away amateurs, fed and cajoled by a professional middle division. I have seen this season that if the Corinthians had a stronger intermediate lino they would take a tremendous amount of beating. The presence of amateurs in League football is good for the game. I wish there were more of Ahem. Stoke have never been so strong as when they included in their ranks Len Hales. It was a pity that Hales met with an accident when he was just beginning his career; he would have surely become one of our most famous forwards. And by the way, as showing how the amateur fits in with the methods of the League player, there is a striking demonstration in the doings of W. C. Jordan. The Dark Blue, when assisting West 13romwich, was a real, live, desirable centre-for- ward. At the 'Varsity he did not always distin- guish himeelf. -</p>
        <p>The Emergency Man. You often come across a very fine footballer who, from the outset of his career, is obliged to play the part of an emergency man. Such a footballer is Bannister, who has just been trans- ferred from Manchester United to Preston</p>
        <p>North End. Bannister has been a very good for. ward, distinctly above the average for many seasons. Before the Manchester City scandals which led to an epidemic of fines and suspen- sions he was a handy man; then ho became accepted as the ideal partner for Meredith, and as such was transferred from the City club along with Burgeees, Sandy Turnbull, and the great Meredith himself to the United. It came about that the Clayton officials- discovered 11-also at Southend, and Bannister again dropped into the ieserves. Now he has joined Preston" North End, who, I imagine, could have had him many seasons ago, for 'Bannister belongs to Leyland, which is wily a few miles from Deerdale, and I believe that all through his playing life he has lived in his native town. I hope Bannister vill bring better days to the old Lancashire club. '</p>
        <p>TheELondon Clubs. I expect that much recruiting will be done by the managers of London's First-League clubs if tho opportunity of securing good men occurs. There is promise of an improvement at Woolwich Arsenal; but one is afraid that days of prosperity at Plumstead will be few, and as for Tottenham and Chelsea, there can be no</p>
        <p>doubt whatever that there is need of vigorous recruits. The Tottenham team as at preoent constituted will not do at all, and one can place no sort of reliance upon Chelsea until there is greater strength at half-back.</p>
        <p>Great Times at Blackburn. It is a very long time since there were such great days at Blackburn. The improve- ment is wonderful. One wonders whether the Rovers will again become the great Cup fighters they were in the time of Brandon, Forbes, Dewar, Forrest, and. Joe Lofthouse. When one thinks of the old days one would like to know what has become of the men who helped to make 'the game what it is to-day. One feels that there is not such a touch of romance in the game as there used to be. Bradford City are coming on again, but I am afraid that Sheffield United are inclined to wobble. Liverpool are still going great guns, i but there is a marked falling off n the -play of a club like Bristol City, while Bolton Wan- derers, who, by the way, have juset-returned from Scotland with a new goalkeeper, are ,quite un- certain. And is there another team so incon- sistent as Middlesbrough? The Second Division remains the delightful puzzle it over was. It is not long odds againa; any of the first half- dozen clubs getting into the charmed circle. "Soccer" in South Wales. I was speaking the other day to a gentleman who did much to create the Southern League, and he assured me that, notwithstanding the altered condition of London football, there is every likelihood of a revival; at any rate, most clubs in this competition, hard hit though they have been by the running away of first Fulham and then Tottenham, are quite certain that they will have a good season financially. It is thought that much good will follow the missionary work that has been done by Mr. Harry Bradshaw in South Wales. It is believed that in this part of the Principality tho Association game is going to boom. Ido not want to discourage the South League gentlemen, but I am afraid that it will be a long time before Rugby takes a back seat to "Soccer ", in places like Swansea, Cardiff, and Newport, for instance. Still, one has seen a great change come over football life in recent years. Who would have thought that the round ball would be chased with such ansto in centros like Leeds, .boraotoro, tlldnam, and nun, tor in- stance? And so the Southern League may fairly hope to strengthen their position by coquetting With the people of south Wales,</p>
