      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>FROM WARRINGTON TO WIEN AND BACK.</head>
        <p>FROM WARRINGTON TO WIEN AND BACK.</p>
        <p>WITH SOME IMPRESSIONS BY THE WAY. (SPECIALLY WRITTEN FOR " TIIE EXAMINER" BY ALDERMAN BENNETT)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>ARTICLE 111.</head>
        <p>ARTICLE 111.</p>
        <p>We sped on, now, into the Vosges Moun- tains, passing Saarburg, whence a line runs on to Metz, the scene of the historical capitu- lation, and Lutzelburg, four miles from which, at Pfalzburg, Erckmann and Lobau were born. On through the valley of the Zorn and into Alsace, through those." blue Alsatian mountains" of which my good friend Mr.W. H. J.Jenkins used to sing. Then through Zabern, the Roman Tabernae, and presently I *got my first great thrill, for gAzing always through the windows at the moving shadow-show, there, clearly visible across the level plain, I saw the lofty spire of an immense Cathedral. Instinctively I knew that it was Strassburg, and, as I looked at it, I recollected how, not forty years before, that towering edifice had been the target for the German guns, and how, for many weeks, the folks who dwelt beneath it had been doomed to live on rats and mice. And I remembered how, as a boy, I vaguely realized the horror of it all in reading Hesba Stretton's moving tale, "Max Kromer." It had been originally intende'd to break the journey here, and I was very sorry that this was not possible, for Strassburg has always been a magic word for me, although, to tell the truth, its low, flat site, its wide- spread buildings, often hidden in trees, and the great busy Sta Von, were as much unlike</p>
        <p>a little dismal—triste, the citizens of Paris would have called it. The street where I was staying was about as gay as Portland- Street by gas-light; and, though I came across an occasional Beer flail, and rambled on reflectively for some distance, the place seemed singularly silent and deserted, and I did not find the task exhilarating, and was glad enough to go to bed. _ .</p>
        <p>Sometime between the hours of six and seven, a strident bell within my room whirred suddenly and with a strange insis- ence, but, as I had been told that we should not start out from the Hotel till nearly nine o'clock, I let it ring and slumbered on for perhaps another hour. And then a sudden knocking came, and, going to the door, I almost ran into the arms of Mr. Shawcross ; found that practically everyia9dy had already breakfasted; and learnt that, as the outcome of the last night's interview, it was arranged that we should start at eight o'clock instead of nine, and thg the electric bell in the bed- room had been rung at that unconscionably early hour to warn me of the fact. I dressed and had my rolls and coffee with such speed as I was able to command, and followed Mr. Shawcross to the Hotel Marquart ; but we were both in ample time, and it was some minutes before we set out, in the care of flit Vice-Consul Herr B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>TOWN HALL AND MARKET PLACE, STUTTGART.</head>
        <p>TOWN HALL AND MARKET PLACE, STUTTGART.</p>
        <p>my preconceived ideas of the place as one can well imagine. We only stopped for a. few minutes, and I was not everi able to obtain a picture post-card; but, as we glided out again, I caught a glimpse of the fortifi- cations (what tales of horror they could tell!) and watchdd the tall towers disappearing gradually until my neck began to ache. And then I had my second thrill. Suddenly I caught the sheen of water. I saw that it was broad, and of a lovely emerald, and that it rolled away in majesty towards the North, and, with a strange enthusiasm, I cried "The Rhine." And such, indeed,it was. The first sight of a great river is always impres- sive, and no river in the world, perhaps, has woven itself so closely-into all men's hearts as this.</p>
        <p>As twilight gradually descended, Mr. Aldridge passed along the corridors of our train (I never travelled in a train upon this journey which was not built upon the corridor principle) to call our attention to the first glimpse of the beautiful Black Forest. Most of us were vaguely moved again, but, alas! the Secretary's tidings left the Bridge-ites cold. All day they had bent over their absorbing cards, and everything that France and Germany could show seemed less import- ant than those Kings and Queens and Jacks and Aces. "It is a mad world, my masters ! " As long as we could see, we gazed upon that sombre Forest on the hills, the gloom of which so amply justifies the name, and brings a hundred eerie legends to one's mind ; and presently we stopped at Baden-Baden, a name which had a pleasant and familiar sound, and, just eie darkness fell, rode slowly into proud Carlsruhe. "'What is the matter here ? " said Coun- cillor Walker, a pleasant little man from Walsall. Suddenly I looked outside and caught a fleeting glimpse of a street bedecked from end to end with flags, and then we pulled up at a handsome Station, and,over its main entrance, caught a vision of imperial eagles and black and yellow flags; and we learnt, with yet another thrill,that the great War-Lord of Europe was at present here attending the manoeuvres. The Bridge-ites left their cards,at last, and all of us sat down to our first German dinner. It did not greatly differ from the meal which we had in the French train, and, for the benefit of those who think that Germans live on sausages, and horse-flesh and black bread', I may at once observe that I do not think I tasted sausage during the whole of my journey, and though the beef was occasionally .a little like horse-flesh, it was quite eatable. As for the bread, several of our party always asked for it, because they liked it better than the white. Still on we rode in the black night through the Black Forest, and, the dinner ended, and the Bridge-ites at their game again (with some excuse at last), we peered into the darkness vainly ; passed an unknown town or two (there was, I found, a strange hiatus in my Baedekers at this part of the journey); and at half past eight, or thereabouts, we slowed down once again at Stuttgart. It was raining slightly when we arrived, and my first impressions were not over-favourable. AS I left the big and busy Central Station; with its endless. long low, platforms and its lines that led all over Europe, I came out into a dark and silent thoroughfare—the Friedrichs Strasse —which curiously suggested Manchester; went across to the Victoria Hotel, which was directly opposite; saw that my. two bags had landed safely; put them in my comfortable bed room ' • and then went on to the Hotel Marquart,where most of the party were staying, to buy some Post cards, write some letters, and to find the" Examiner" and my other local newspapers awaiting me. Mr. Thompson, Mr. Aldridge and Mr. Shaw— cross were already deep in the arrange- ments for to-morrow, and the rest of the company had scarcely settled down; and so I sallied forth alone into the Schloss-Flatz, a great leafy square immediately below the Hotel. But the vicinity looked dark and c'aeerless, and I was glad to pass into a spacious Beer Hall, and to scan the latest news from Warrington to the sound of moving music and the not unwelcome solace 'of a mighty lidded mug of lager beer. After resting here awhile, I sallied forth again, but Stuttgart seemed, to tell the truth,</p>
        <p>Ehrenbacher,for the Rathaus, or Town Hall, as we should call it. It was raining heavily, and the Schloss-Flatz was distinctly sloppy; but we noticed, as we passed, the tall Jubilee , Column erected in honour of William the First. and several other statues; and, walking on beneath the trees, we saw the golden monu- ment of the same king on horseback—" The Unity Emperor,"as I ventured to describe him; and, sauntering through several quaint old streets, we reached the Market Place, extremely picturesque and ancient, and, on -the other side the square, directly facing us, came right upon the Rathaus. It was a dignified and solid-looking building with a central tower, its inside passages of great solidity and cloistral gloom; but, passing through some intermediate rooms, we were conducted to a handsome hall which I took to be the Council Chamber; and here we spent an hour or two in listening to the full and clear accounts of Stuttgart and its civic plans and policy which were con- tributed, with tireless courtesy, by some of the officials of the city. Many of us were fain to pester them with questions, hut, as time was passing, these were strictly limited in number, and, well armed with maps and documentary informa- tion, we emerged to find a string of carriages awaiting- us, and, each of us in charge of some well-mannered and enlightened German, to drive through many spaciout streets, alive with business, to..a fine new Elementary School upon the pleasant hills which gird the city,; to note how carefully its growth was being regu- lated •, to ponder over all the lessons we had learnt that morning in the Rathaus ; and, through multitudes of big block dwellings, to ride back amid the pouring rain for lunch. This was provided at the Rathaus in the Municipal Restaurant and Wine Cellar,which is a regular institution in Germany. It was our earliest experience of German hospitality, and our host was the Oberburgomaster (Herr 'von Gantz) himself. The Menu was not large, but each item on the list VMS excellent. It commenced with the loveliest omelette. which I have ever tasted. The Germans seem to*specially excel in omelettes. The meat we usually had was beef, and it was always served in small, thick slices, and accompanied by a great dish which contained a number of tiny mountains, sometimes close upon a dozen, each composed of a different vegetable. We liked the vegetables better than the beef ; but the chicken was a revelation. This was generally served with salad and stewed fruit. At Stuttgart it was accompanied by a dish of yellow plums. And to the British house- wife who is not able to conceive the possibilities of such a combination I content myself with saying: Try it! And what of the Oberburgomaster ? A little, grave, short-whiskered, thoughtful, shrewd and unpretentious-looking man, he might have been the President of the Manchester Chamber of Commerce say. He seemed to be immersed in business, and looked as if he had not any time for levity. He seldom smiled and was entirely homely and unostentatious; but Herr von Gantz is one of the ablest men in Germany, and is the maim cog in the wonderful municipal machinery which has gained for Stuttgart much of its success, its beauty, and its pre- eminence as a well-governed city; and it was with no perfunctory homage that, between the courses (in -the German way), we all stood up to drink his health in one of the excellent'wines of the country, and, each of us the proud possessor of a well-filled cigar case(a little present from our hospitable hosts) departed to resume our drive. The rain still pitilessly fell, but, nothing daunted, we set forth once more, and, leaving the old Market Place, rode out to the green hills which gird the town, and noticed, as we gradually rose, how careful the builders of the road had been in planning it; and how, at twenty points, we gazed across this " town of gardens," which lay, in its deep valley, dimly visible amid the rain, with nothing- to obstruct the view. We lost it for awhile, and passed into the pleasant woods which had been lately bought by the municipality, and then, returning, visited a charming colony of Model Dwellings, which had been built by a great Society for the Welfare af the Working People; and, con-</p>
        <p>. _ _, -,._...... ... . . . . ••. ' • .. . . - . .. . _ : .`,kiiii,4,,li': . .. • , I • . , . . . . . - , . . • 'O, • , • . . , , • ; 07 ,317,•;;•-•`q.k. ..,-..• „•, .. ...: -• '•.- • - -,,.. , •74„,„ '4 1.;,' ~..4",,, ~ -,,t :', . •t-:. • ..'O4: A.,'" ,f''',•,',:s•;. •,,zek ••„ •.• , •-., • ..,,:,/,:i„...,,,,,, :41T-i.,1, •t,,,,,,, ••- , • 1 .6 .11 ...:...•.',,'.•••i-::t.:41,• '',•••'7-t.,,,e'•t774;'.••••:,';',,,, .` ' x••,°4Q''''' '-•,, • ' , ~.,'"..':',7›,f-:'• ei,...14„.t....„47,..11.,.,1ft., rictoi . .4-.,,,...,,•,:::,,•#,;m•itif....:„;;,•,. . , t.•-r„,..c... ~,..,tr,,, ~,.. ---•:„-/ I .. ••:, :-.i,,,; • 1•Q-7,1t.t.:wr,,, jr,...,r,. f... -..- ',- ..'•''..2' '''' - '•• ', 1.•.•;""*", -•. . ~.r :. 'PA ; '..r ::7.4'l;.:''''''''''-'lC.'"':j : o4f-:.'Z'f''''Ar,,,rk.„.4. ' '1 ''' :', eli'..s.'' . r,;;:.:e. '" 's ' .:','•;:t ' 1141 r: ~,,, '''. ..t..*: tr,,r4v.,.1%,,.::,.. 0 iii. 4 . ......2.1 :. , . ~, s- ' : .-7 ' ' ' ' ; , ~„ ,„ ,- e ' ~. :" /a*, 411-7-::1- -A: ,:; Z-1:.:7-',••;?;,04.1..... _Rift: • ~-•:. ': . - - ,-, voi , -.. •,„,-, ~,-. „..,;,,,,,,,,, ~,-,-..., ~,' :-,', 4 ...:. ‘ ..4,...:42. ,;.-€:.:,., -.;.-. 4 %trmr..4 ....::-,V,l'''. ''''', '''''.r"--: :.;, ~,-:',. , k-.7* . : ''...!...i...i. •• , " ~ i,,.'... ;,..-L '' -- ' " •,-.. -,' ::' ' , . ~... ~ , .. . .. ~ ... falra,.. 'f. .. .i , -,,..,,,• ' , , -,,,i,:f,-• - ' - • ~.,--t.....• ~, - , ~,a,...,..il -,tti. • .€,..„1.,,K _ ~ . - ‘..4,...,..5 '•'• '''' ' '': ' V. ••• - -...1-z7.4-; ‘. '"' . .". ~.. ''..• e, ' , ... ', ' . .-.- '!..:':,- '.'...,.,:-:.1 ' ,7,,-, • 1, t, _ .. . air, ••••P '1 ~..',,: , ' . . . ''•':-'"'-'!.l.7=Zl.4re'' . .." :. :. ,- .:: ..',.:..i5;;;;,..;,..;,:a.ii-,.igiigi.. ...</p>
