      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, November 6, 1909</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, November 6, 1909</p>
        <p>ducted by its venerable founder, went into several of the houses, chatted with the occupants, saw their clean and well-- kept rooms, and blessed the brain which had benevolently organized so great a scheme and made it so successful.</p>
        <p>Then, gradually descending, we drove right through the monster civic Slaughter- houses, much lamenting that there was not time to actually enter them, and so came to the north end of the town, and to the river Neckar and the noble Public Spa. We visited the Recreation Halls, and tried the water, which was drinkable enough, and glanced into the woods behind it, and we rode' home through the great Schloss- Garten, a park of some two hundred acres, laid out in the English style, with, lakes and flowers, and lovely trees, and many marble statues, and a big Botanical Garden, and, at the southern end of it, the Royal Palace, and the Schloss-Platz, and, a little fiirther on, the hospitable doors of our Hotels. It was only a bird's-eye view of Stuttart, but the impressions which it left on us, in spite of the continued rain and the dull leaden sky, were altogether favourable, and there was not 4pne of us who was not loth to leave it and who did not envy its good citizens the statesmanlike and comprehensive policy which is moulding all its future on such sensible and comprehensive lines. I might easily fill the whole of this week's article with an account of its municipal achieve- ments, but I have endeavoured to set forth their salient features in my Report to the Town Council, and I must not traverse the same ground again. My good friend,- Councillor Pemberton, at a recent Council meeting, discounted the views of Councillor Roberts and myself con- cerning the superiority of German methods by declaring thst the towns which we had seen were modern, and that their methods had not any -relevance in an old town like Warrington But the name of Stuttgart first occurs in 1229; from 1265 it was the favourite residence of the Counts of Wurtem- burg ; it became the capital in 1482; and it began to attain its present dimensions and appearance quite a hundred years ago. Its population is, to-day, above a quarter of a million, and in, business enterprize and civic progress and prosperity, it need not fear comparison with any town in Germany. At night, the Oberburgomaster, the Vice- Consul, and the editors of the three chief Stuttgart papers honoured us by dining with our party at the Hotel Marquart. Of course, our spokesmen paid due tribute to their famous city, and of course we did our best to emphasize the friendliness which should exist between the two great sister nations. I sat directly opposite to the attractive wife of the editor of the great Social Democratic "daily." The editor hiriiself, despite his politics, was quite absurdly like the lordly Wilhelm; but he told me that the people did not specially admire the Kaiser; that there was too much Kaiser and too little people about him; and he added that he (the editor) was always on the side of the brave Oberburgomaster in his endless battle .for the public weal. So far as I could judge; indeed, the Emperor is not pver popular in Southern Germany. My tete-a-tete had lately come from Bremen, and I did my best to be agreeable, but I hardly knew a word of German and she only knew a little English; and dinner over, nOt a few of us were glad to gather round the charming daughter of o,ne of our companions in the day's excursion who had been to an English School, spoke English perfectly, and puffed her after dinner cigarette' in a perfectly bewitching way. The beautiful wife of the Vice-Consul, who was a famous singer, had meanwhile charmed us by her delightful rendering of "Home, sweet home," and " Comin' thro' the rye " ; and, after that, in sweet response to our renewed solicitations, she sang two lovely German songs; and, last of all, just ere we parted, Tosti's beautiful "Good-bye." But we would not have it. There seemed to be only two words to fit the situation. They were the German words, " Auf Wiedersehen "</p>
        <p>(To be continued).</p>
        <p>t. Quintin so much after all," he said to himief as he exchanged his boots for slippers and put on a comfortable old coat. Mr. Sinclair came for -a week, but time went, and though he frequently talked of returning to town, he still stayed. He had Mrs. Denis's little parlour with its green wall-paper covered with roses, .ts cupboard of china and shelf of old-fashioned books and its jars of pot-pourri, which waged an incessant struggle against the ,odour of damp that intruded itself when a fire was not constantly kept going. At his table, drawn under the low window, Mr. Sinclair seemed to do a deal of writing. At first the books lie had brought in his bag served him. Then he made an excursion up to town 'and brought back a great box of them. They lay on the floor and the sofa, and everywhere, and were an eyesore to Mrs. Denis with her housewifely instincts, till at last she got the carpenter to run up a few shelves around the walls of Mr. Sinclair's bedroom: , _ _ _</p>
        <p>, When the books were established that gentleman viewed them with satisfaction. _ _</p>
        <p>"Better than Bloomsbury," he said to himself, as he looked out of one little window to the sea, and saw from the other the little garden where everylhing began to grow grey and indistinct with buds.</p>
        <p>He went to dine two or three evenings in the week with Mr. St. Quintin, who had returned, and sometimes the rector dined with him and enjoyed much Mrs. Penis's excellent cookery. He was rejoiced to have his friend so near. Both were bachelors, and their tastes were alike, though the rector dabbled with science in a gentle, amateuri. h way, while Mr. Sinclair was to some extent a famous man.</p>
        <p>The year turned round to spring, and then to summer. Mrs. Denis's summer visitors had sought her Out and been refused. Mr. Sinclair seemed in permanent occupation of her rooms. :lie did not appear anxious to lave anyone else, for there was a room in the gable still unoccupiesl. At last she had to reckon with an insistent application. This was a Mi ,Doyney a literary lady who had Veen sent by a former inhabitant of the gable-room. She would not take "No" for an answer.</p>
        <p>"Though I'm ample," she said, "you'd be surprised at how I can squeeze in. Please don't deny a weary traveller. I shall rot give very much trouble."</p>
        <p>She was as good as her word. The little maid, whom Mrs. Danis had added to her establishment since Mr. Sinclair had become an inmate, fomd the new lady very unexacting. She and Mr. Sinclair were so quiet in the house, "that they triOit as well 13 out of it," Jinny remarked. Miss Doyne took long walks over the sand cliffs into the interior all alone, or went off on her bicycle, which is a rermlnent wonder to the, inhabitants of Sandy Bay, and remained out. all day. _ _</p>
        <p>She had been a nurse at one tinte, she told Mrs. Denis, and the latter often thought what a brisk and reas3urin4 presence in a sick-room hers would be. While she talked, Mrs. Denis would sit and regard her with eyes of admiration, which perhap pleased the elder woman,' for she became quite affectionate in her manner to her little landlady. She was a big woman, and very handsome, with splendid coils of grey hair low on hee stately neck. She had-blue eyes, and very rosy cheeks, and her face sparkled so that she suggested to Susan Dvnis a picture of a lady in frost. She :eo ned alive and tingling down to her finger-tips. . Aft« r a time, she and Mr. Sinclair got to know each other, and then she did not so (lien go hing walls alone. Mr. Sinclair accompanied her pretty constantly, and Mr. St. Quintin sometimes made one of the party. An odd kind of friendship hid sprung up between the fellow-lodgers. They seennd to be always wrangling whenever Mrs. Denis heard them. Mr. Sinclair had old-fashioned ideas about wcanen. Miss Do no advcented every- Jiing that was most advanced. They liked bet;er to disagree than to agree. Miss Doyne's qu'ck, clear voice was always in a high wind of argument, and when she had said her say, Mr. Sinclair would come in with a sharp jest, which Susan Denis thongiit, quite demolished his opponent's premises. But if the speeches had a imie of sub-acidity about them, in the minds of the two was nothing but good will towards each other. Susan now presided over meals, looking so dainty and fresh that no ore could believe she had done the cooking before taking her place at the head of the table.</p>
        <p>She felt happy generally in those days, though once a month when she paid her visit to the asylum; she came back with an obi ions depression that Mr. Sinclair dimly saw and IVIiS vexed at.. Neither he nor I‘..liss Boyne had the least idea of Susan's great trouble. Neitherercw she had a husband living. But with masculine selfishness . the man was grieved when the bright, quiet little presence at the head of the table became sad and pale.</p>
        <p>He was soon to learn the reason. One morning there came a letter from the asylum to Mrs. Denis to tell her that her husband's mad fit was over for the time being, and she might remove him. Fur the first time, the news was a blow to her, though she hardly knew why. For the .first time, during many hard years, she had been taking a nue pleasure in life, and now-- She gave a great sigh a.s she folded the letter and put it away. "Oh, roor, poor Jack I" she said, with a sudden revulsion of feeling. "Your own wife loves you, and will take care of you before all the world." It was this morning of all mornings that Jinny, a precocious imp of fifteen, with a ca-t in her eye that gave her a certain wild look, took it upon herself to prophesy. "What a beautiful couple they'll make, to be sure, !" she burst out, with an enthusiasm that made her stop half-way in sand-papering the poker. "Who ?" said Mrs. Denis, in amazement. "o'6 Wy, Mr. Sinclair an' Miss Doyne, 'm. Everycne says as 'ow they'rz?, made for each ,other, thouglonother do say Miss Doyne's a sight older than Alr.-Sinclalr, for all !is grey 'air." "Jinny," said Susan Denis, a little sharply, "1 wish you wouldn't gossip about the people in this house ; and, above all, I wish you wouldn't repeat village go.ssie ti!!%" .</p>
        <p>"for: said the algrieved Jinny, returning to I; neglected duties. "ism sure I didn'i mean no liarni."_</p>
        <p>But Mrs. Denis had gone •upslnlr3 to her own rooli•, where ?lie began to think out _what wiN lasi to be done about her lodgers._ As she was thinking, she got out the sober hat and chid: in which sln alwafs paid her visits to the as, lem. the thin: she was sure of, they would h:r.c to go. It mas thonfand pilics that Mr. Sinclair :should established himself as though for a long time ; bu it. couldn't be helped now. She couldu'i ‘l,ii as! them to leave that very day, to be sure, and Foci Jack mustn't- remain in that Jul rim an hour long(, than she could help it ; but they must go nex. week.</p>
        <p>She put on her bonnet and cloak, and went dowt to the parlour. Mr. Sinclair was at his table writing when she went. in. "Well," he said, as he stood op and gave her chair, "are you off to the town ? Come to see if I have any commissions, oh ? " lie was mending a pen, and had not noticed th, gravity of her face. "If you please, Mr. Sinclair," she said, in low voice, "I shall want my rooms as soon :I: you can find it convenient to give them -tip." "Give them up!" shouted her lodger. "Why. I don't mean to give them up. I never was s, comfortable in my life!" "I shall want the rooms," she answered. "lan very sorry, but I slull want them, indeed." _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>fie turned and looked at her. She was pale, and there were traces of tears about, her eyes. Ther he cArne over to her and put his hand impulsive]) on her shoulder.</p>
        <p>"My little woman," he said, with a quiet affectionateness, "what is the -it:atter, and by d( you want to turn me out ? You might know ] wouldn't go. lam too happy." "Miss Doyne will be going too," blurted Susan. He looked at her curiously. Then he gave n little laugh..</p>
        <p>"I shl lose a good comrade, but not for long. I think she will soon be back at the Rectory, Ps MIS. St. Quintin. Is that news to you? I only heard it myself last night." Susan's face cleared for an instant, then it fell again. "You don•t know," she said, "that I have a husband in the asylum." "A husband!" echoed Mr. Sinclair, incredu- lously. "Good God, no!' You are playing with rue !"</p>
        <p>He had turned red and pale. As he spoke his fingers unconsciously clutched Scsan's shoulder till she could have cried out. She went on in a sad voice:</p>
        <p>"lie has been mad at intervals elmest since our marriage. When he is sane, I take Lim home till the madness conies back again. But I must be alone to take care of him, for I never know when he will break Out."</p>
        <p>"You mean to say that you shut yourself up here alone with a man liable to sudden madness ? " She nodded her head. She had the air of a little marlyr. lie thought, with a shudder, that the knew her liceband would kill her dome day.</p>
        <p>"And you want me to go away and leave you in these circumstances? "</p>
        <p>She nodded her head again. "Well, then, I shall not go," he said. "There is a thousand times more reason now why I should remain." She looked at him half with admiration, half with appeal. lie understood that she shrank from letting him see her poor wreck of a man. "My dear," he said, his manner changing to that of a kind doctor, "do not mind me. I have been studying madness for years. I have been seeing sad and painful-things all my life. I shall help you with him, and shall have only: prefouna pity for him. Let me stay !" "If I do not, you will say that you will2not go," said Mrs. Denis, naively. "I believe I should," he ansWered, holding the door open for her as if the whole question wcre settled.</p>
        <p>As for Miss Doyne, when she had heard Susan's story, she just took her in her aims and cried over her. But for going she was as obstinate as Mr. Sinclair. "You won't, separate a pair of old lovers," she said, with a light in her eyts that softened her face wonderfully. "I've promised Everard—Mr. St. Quintin—to marry him next month. We are too old to endure the chances of life and death that are involved in delay and absence front each other. Till then, let. me, stay, my dear." "I thought," said Susan Denis, with sudden boldness, "tbat it was Mr. Sinclair." "You foolish woiran ! lie is fifteen years younger than I am, for all his grey hairs. Have they deceived you ? Ile hats never thought of me." She stopped as if she had been on the point of saying more, and glanced sharply at the other woman's face, but the unconscious innocence of it reassured her. "Dear soul," she said in her own mind, "she would never imagine that anybody could be in love with her because she is married. As if that always prevented it!" After that began the faithful seven years in which Godfrey Sinclair served for his Rachel with no hope o! ultimate rett arch By patience and gentle- ness he won poor Jack Denis s affection. When Miss Dovne had gone to the Rectory as Mrs. St. Quintin, I.lr. Sinclair remained, Susan Denis's one lodger. Ihirin‘r the intervals when Jack was at home he was his constant companion. When he was not he fell lack behind the little barrier of reserve that Sesan t bought it well to set up between them when there was no longer her hus'iand's presence. Once it happened in those years that Mr. Sinclair took it into his head to shave off his beard. Without it he was a young man, and an uncommonly handsome one. The grey hair but made the young face more distinguished. Mr. tt,. Quintin was right when he had told his wife that before Sinclair buried himself in study ht was one of the best-lcoking men in town. "Po you think, Everard," Mrs. St. Quintin sometimes asked her husband, "that she guesses at all at what the man is doing for her ? Dear as she is, it. veins too great a sacrifice for any weman." "Sinclair, like mys'elf, my dear," replied Mr. St. Quintin, with tender gallantry, "has never loved but once.", • Then 11 hen the seven years were done Jack Denis died. Ile was lad in the little churchyard on the cliffs at Sandy Bay, and his widow, though she was lonely for hint as women always are when they lose a .burden and a dependent, could not but be glad that his long suffering was over. The first. weeks after his death passed much as usual. Then' one day Mr. Sinclair spoke. lie had prevailed on Mrs. Denis to walk with him to the Rectory. "Susan," he said, suddenly, "are things to go on for ever in this way ? lam a young man and you arc a young woman, no longer protected by (veil such a marriage its yours was. It doesn't scent to me that the thing will work." "Will people talk ?" she said, looking at him with quick alarm. "They might, .but it is not so much that as that my endurance is nearly at an end. I have served seven years for you, my .Rachel I You do not want me to wait seven more ?" - "No," she said, lifting her bead bravely. "I love you, though it is only now I know that I have always loved you. But we must not forget that 6 my hu,band is dead so short a time." "My dearest "—and he kissed her. "Come, and we will talk to Mrs. St. Quintin about it. She is a clear-beaded woman, and she will tell you what I tell you, that I have served long enough. Your marria4e was net like other marriages." A few days later Mr. Sinclair left Sandy Bay. It was said- that he was going to Berlin for, two years to do some sci work. A week or so later Mrs. Denis shut up her house and went off to London with Mr. and Mrs. St. Quintin. They returned without her a few weeks later, and replied to inquiries for her that she was with friends having a long rest and change after all her years of trouble. But what a sensation it caused when Mrs. Denis came back as Mrs. Sinclair, after two years were up, and with a little daughter of a year old, too! You would hardly know the cottage now. Mr. Sinclair has added so much to it that it straggles over where the little garden once was, and there is a part of it reaching out towards the sea, and an odd room built on top at the back of the gable- room, of which you can't see the wall for books. There never was such a happy couple as Mr. and Mrs. Sinclair, unless, perhaps, it is Mr. and Mrs. St. Quintin. [THE END.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
