      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>NEWS FROM THE OTHER SIDE.</head>
        <p>NEWS FROM THE OTHER SIDE.</p>
        <p>It would be interesting to know the opinioh of the late Professor Lombroso, if it were possible, through the intermediary of Julia's Bureau at Mr. Stead's, upon the extraordinary appearance of a ghost there has been lately upon the hoardings!</p>
        <p>The new Bovril poster, of the Toreador looking down upon the small bottle, which contains all the Power of the Beef, has been pasted over the poster it has succeeded—that of a very living ox looking at his wooden model, and with a sigh, saying, 'Lucky beggar, you can't be made into Bovril,"</p>
        <p>When the poster is wet the head of this bull comes through the new poster, showing in a grey, green, shadowy colour, making it appear as though a ghostly ox head were looking down upon the Toreador and the Bovril bottle. There is not much doubt about it, if bill- posting had been in existence, as a method of public advertising, in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries, someone from Bovril, Limited, would have had to walk the streets in aflame-coloured garment to expiate the tnagic. which would have been laid to his charge over this poster. Messrs. Bovril, Limited, are preparing a photograph of one of these ghostly posters, in the form of a picture posscard, and anybody sending a penny stamp for postage to Old Street, City Road, London, E. 0., can have a packet of six of them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>(apyright.)</head>
        <p>(apyright.)</p>
        <p>THE ROMANCE OF A LANDLADY.</p>
        <p>Br KATHARINE TYNAN,</p>
        <p>Author of "A Daughter of the Fields," "The Way of a Maid," "Oh, What a Plague is Lcve," "She Walks in Beauty," &amp;e.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Denis had come in, tired in body and heart. She had been to see her husband at the county asylum, and it was always a melancholy visit. She felt this evening, a mood unusual to her, a profound discouragement. Only for poor Jack, whose fees as a paying patient must be earned, and whomohe must be ready to receive when his lucid intervals occurred, she would have liked to lie down on the sands outside and let the encroaching tide cover her. Not that she thought of suicide. Susan Denis was a devout woman, with n strong faith in an unseen world, and she never thought of sinning against her faith. But she had felt to-night, as she dragged her tired limbs from the station, that the best lot in all he world would have been just to have been born, to have been made a child of God, and then to have died. She was a little woman, black-eye 4 and petty, and usually full of energy. The little kitchen in which she sat, to) tired to take off her bonnet, lac wiliness to her housewifely qualities, for though she was a lady she did her own work. The tiled floor freshly .ochred was bright and pleasant. The plates shone on the dresser, and the bright tins winked from the wall as the firelight rose and fell. Mrs. Sharp's Me7gy from next, door had been in to light the fire before Mrs. Denis should come home, and the kettle was singing a cheerful song a 3 th€ mistress of the little house sat there too tirod to go up..trOrs yet awhile. She felt so lonely and so helplesv th,..t she could not have t,orna Meggy's obse.-.att eyes ; although they would have expressed n.:dring .but sympathy, for the neighbouis, young and old and of all classes. ,were sorry for Mrs. Denis and good to he;-. The only sympathy she could bear was the constant eympathy of Trust, her old terrier, wino sat now with his head on her knee looking up at her with deep eyes that seemed to understand. She and Trust had suffered so much together. Through (he dull trouble of remembering Jack's alienated gaze as she had tried to talk to him there eame the quest ion, how was she to pay for him in the alyium and keep herself during these winter mon t lis ?</p>
        <p>In summer she usually netted a good deal from letting her upper floor to London ladies or gentle- men. Her how() was as neat as a doll's house, someone had said, ard nearly as small. But what joy for Londoners to rise out of the Lyon scented sheets, having left London only the day befo:e. and, drawing the chintz curtains, to look upon time grey sea turning aquamarine and sapphire in the distance 1 sandy Bay was still quite unspoilt, though the railway pa=sed within a couple of miles of it. The place belonged to a very conservative lord of the soil, At ho would have no Casino,' no promenade pier, no bands, no Christy Minstrels, no amateur photographers, none of the orthodox seaside delights. So only very quiet people, anxious for a rest and change, came there season after season.</p>
        <p>But this summer Mrs. Denis had had to say "no" to her faithful patrons. :lack Denis's was a recurring madness. For months at a time he would be quite sane. As soon as these lucid intervals set in his true-hearted wife would come to the asylum and fetch hint home. Poor fellow, at the best he was melancholy iVith the knowledge of his hard doom ! It was his wife's task of Sisyphus to lead him out of himself, and cheer hint with her talk, •innocent and merry, or to lift him up with the certainty of her undying devotion. But the minute she relaxed her efforts, down he tumbled once more into the nethermost pit of dejection. ;lack had b,rn at home six months the last time, and the strain shewed itself still in her white clreeks and the air of weariness, which, now that he could not see her, she was at no pains to conceal. She never knew when the madness would return, and she had not dared do anything but refuse her lodgers. As she sat she mechanically turned up her sleeve and revealed an arm, white and round, but marred by a bitter black bruise. Poor Jack! Her heart ached , for him as she remembered his shame and agony when he had discovered the scar on her temple, which she always concealed so carefully under a little wa-va of hair. He had turned away from her, and covered his face with his hands, and cried like a child.</p>
        <p>These things had gone on neArly from the threshold of her married life. Jack had had an injury to his head. His horse had thrown him and had kicked him as he lay, and soon after-. wards, when the wound was healed and he was about again, the injury to his brain had revealed itself. . If you want'ed an opinion of Mrs. Denis you should ask Dr. Walton at the asylum. That dis- tinguished physic:an was moved to an emotion foreign to him when he spoke of her. "Talk of heroines," he would say, "why, there's a little woman not much bigger than a child, and she takes her life in her hands to comfort that poor beggar when he has a sane interval, and realises the danger, mind you. No one can say when and how his attacks will recur, and though she knows the symptoms she can't always get him off in time. Violent ! I should think he is violent when the fit seizes him, and -he's more likely to hurt her than anyone else. Poor beggar, it would be a lot more merciful for Loth of them if he were mad all the time !" But even the doctor did not suspect thit Mrs. Penis had not given her husband the perfect love which would have made the poor shell of the man infinitely dear, and all sacrifice sweet. She had married him soon after a young cousin had been drowned at sea to ii horn she had given her girlish love. She had married him because -be so greatly desired it, And because she had bcen too low and dispirited at the time to resist him. The love might have conic if Jack had had time, for he w,k; a good fellow, and fond of her. But now, though she gave him infinite compassion and. nnwearying tenderress, she would never .give him the love which would have lifted her care of him to -something of exaltation and rapture. Look at her as slip sits mechanically smoothing a fold of her bonnet-string! She is pretty and relined, with son:ailing about her pose "sight and glancing. But there is just such a cloud on her as might lie on a sick bird. Her bright eye is dim, her soft hair rough, lur attitude one of dejection. She stood up at last, and made her tea. Then she went to her room and put away her bonnet and cloAk with anxious tidiness. • Trust had ,followed her upstairs, and lay blinking at her with his nose on his paws. As she turned from smoothing her hair at the glass her eye fell on him. "Wlat are we to do, Trust ?" she asked—she had got into a way of talking to her dog. "What are we to do for money till the summer comes round ? The pension will nearly all go for your master, old fellow, and if we can't eke out what's left somehow there'll be short commons for you and me."</p>
        <p>• The dog looked, at her intelligently. Then he 'pricked up his ears and listened. Yes, there was a knock at the door, which, without any inter- vening hall, opened straight on the little kitchen from the seashore. Sonic of the neighbours no doubt, bail come to ace if she wanted for anything. When she opened the door the wind rushed in and roughened her smooth hair. There was a tall, grey-haired and grey-bearded gentleman standing in the doonway with a bag in his hand. As he lifted his hat his eyes took in the red-tiled kitchen with its leaping fire, and the tea spread on a snowy napkin on the scaecely less snowy table, and he looked pleased. "Are you .Mrs. Denis ?" he asked, and before she could answer him he had put one fo.)t over her threshold. Outside a cold rain bad set in, and the black sea was troubled. "Bemuse," he went on when she had answered him, "Mrs. Rooke at the Rectory sent me here to ask for a lodging. 1 came down to see my friend, Mr. St. Quintin, for a week, and find he is away and the house occupied by strangers. I hope you can put me up for a nlght or two." "Certainly," Mrs. Denis answered, with a soft intonation auit pleased him. "Won't you come in and sit down here while I put your room a little to rights " s'epped in bri,kly, as though glad to exchange the night outside for the comfort within, and dr. 41,0 ed into Mrs. Denis's armchair with a long sigh of content.</p>
        <p>When she bad conic down again be shouldered his bag and trouped upstairs. Ire looked about him with approval. The bare floor with its coupl6 of rugs was spotlessly clean. Lot,so chintz window- curtains were drawn across fht. liltle casement. I:he sheets and blankets airing by the newly-maae fire sent out a sweet country smell.</p>
        <p>"If she has a room where I can write, and ip at can gin me aometbing !It to eat, 5441 min.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>SECRET OF SKIN-HEALING.</head>
        <p>THE SECRET OF SKIN-HEALING.</p>
        <p>Latest Revelation of Science.</p>
        <p>The mystery of the constant growth of new skin to replace that which is daily washed off has always been of absorbing interest. Probe that secret, and the cause might be discovered for those many skin diseases which, however much they may vary in intensity of irritation and disfigurement, all have one common peril of stopping Nature's function of grow- ing new skin. _ _ _</p>
        <p>it was a true understanding of the skin that gave ise to the early Roman custom of regularly ' anointing the human body with certain herbal balms whose skin-reviving powers were ds remarkable as their aroma was rich and pleasant. The Ancient Gladiators, too, regularly dressed their skins with precious balms. Unfortunately, in recent years there have crept inte use cheap ointments and salves, which nearly always contain rancid animal fats and mineral poisons, too coarse to be absorbed by the minute pores or " breathing-holes " in the skin. Costly researches have, however, been made along new lines with a view to finding other natural means for keeping up that constant supply of new healthy tissue which is absent when ulcers, pimples, or fester- ing sores break out. Attention was drawn .to the peculiar healing properties possessed by certain rare herbs. When these herbs were scientifically treated they yielded rich juices which accounted for those wonderful powers. Steps were consequently taken to' bring these juices to a high point of refinement and] perfection, and to secure the right combination of them for human use.</p>
        <p>Eventually there was produeed an entirely new substance called Zam-buk, which on severe practical test was seen to be capable of both destroying germs and poisons lying beneath the skin-surface, and pro- moting the growth of new healthy skin on Nature's own lines. So powerful is this new preparation that long-standing cases of eczema, ulcers, bad legs, and poisoned wounds readily yield to its strange influence. This demanded the invention of delicate machinery whereby the balm could be produced in large quan- tities and at a cost within the reach of the most modest. purse. Zam-buk then quickly came to the forefront. as the world's greatest healer and skin-cure—the ideal first-aid for which the mother in the home, the athlete in the field, and the breadwinner at his work had long been in real need. Thus this new balm, embodying the great secret of skin healing and containing no rancid animal fat, was given to the public, who acclaimed it as an urgent necessity in eveiy home. •</p>
        <p>Warrington Soldier Who Died to Save 'Others. .The coroner's inquiry into, circumstances connected with the deaths of Lance-Bergeant Fitzpatrick and Private Whelan, of the Ist Cheshire Regiment, who were drowned in the river Lagan, Belfast, last week, through the carsizing of a pontoon, concluded on Monday. Fitzpatrick was a Warrington man and stayed with his sister, Mrs. Tracey, of Naylor-street, before leaving for Belfast. Witnesses stated that while the raft, on which were seated thirty-two soldiers, was being taken across the river it suddenly turned turtle, throwing all the men in the water. The deceased men, who were experienced swimmers, lost their lives in endeavouring to save their comrades. The raft was properly constructed, and its overturn- ing was probably due to a strong under current, The jury returned a verdict of" Accidentally drowned,</p>
      </div>
