      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>LOCAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>FRANCHISE BILL PASSED.</p>
        <p>Niter three night's discussion and with certain Amendments, the most important of which abolished •Plural voting, the Franchise Bill introduced by Mr. F. G. Taylor (Attorney-General); was passed at the meat- ing of the Warrington Parliamentary Debating Society 'ch Tuesday evening, Mr. FELix AlaciNS (West Clare, L.) moved an •atnendment that the clauses with reference to a redistribution of seats be deleted and that a clause 1,..he substituted to the effect that each member of the Rouse of Commons, on a division being ta'ten in thit assembly, should have voting power in proportion to the number of electors in his constituency and the votes recorded for him at the election. He proposed that a Member should be allowed one vote for each 100 'electors and one for each 100 votes be received in excess Of his opponent. He contended that the time for a 4edistribution of voting po ver had arrived and if unfortunately such a measure adversely affected the "Presentation of Ireland it would be one more proof that the much-vaunted Act of Union had failed in its Purpose. The best way out of the difficulty would be 'a scheme of Home Rule for Ireland and simultane- ?nab, with its adoption the representation of Ireland in the House of Commons could be reduced. Redistri- bution of voting power was long over-due in this 'country. If the City of London were to be represented nh a basis of resident population it would not return 'O members; be questioned whether it would return 'nue. Redistribution of seats would be only a temporary Illeasure, as in a few years the conditions would lapse into the old state of inequality. Under the ..ecbeme he proposed the Member for Wal rington in the Present Parliament would be entitled to 118 votes. Another advantage was that it would be regarded as an 'offence to civic pride to keep voters off the register, and every legitimate effort would be made to augment the number of voters. The number of unopposed elec- Naas would also be reduced, as there would be greater encouragement to the minority to fight. , Mr. E. S. RIMMER (L. Kilkenny, N.) seconded. Mr. H. HOPE (First Commissioner of Works) opposed the amendment, as it would be unworkable, although Pe congratulated the hon. member on 'the ingenuity of Tlis suggestion. It would be workable in trades union Congresses and limited liability companies, but it would hot he workable in Parliament. It would produce many „,_Gilbertian situations, and lead to chaos and disorder. ;That would be the lot of the party Whips when they nad in the early hours of the morning to tot up columns e,f figures? (Laughter). He contended tbat it would ')o, far simpler and fairer to have a redistribution of 'eats. _</p>
        <p>di; a division, 16 voted for and 16 against the amend- 1e nt. The SPEAKER thought that the scheme had not "wen sufficiently worked out to form a definite opinion upon, so he gave his casting vote for the Government. Mr. H. RIGBY (Scotland Division of Liverpool, N.) %loved an amendment providing for tbe repeal of the let of Union. He dwelt upon the "utter wickedness" °f the Act and the corruption by which it was consum- Plated, and waxed sarcastic about the terror which tbe l'eating of the orange drum inspired in the Conservative tarty. The Irish members of Parliament were simply fl that assembly as a continual protest against the itreseat conditions of government. They had ally no desire to interfere in English questions but, ‘effering and struggling under an unjust system, they vete better able to appreciate the struggles of the Z?orking classes of this country unier a system which ,?ed much in common with their own. He was sure if the people of this country knew how the Act of ;Ilion was carried, they would not hesitate to wipe 'lvay that blot on the fair name of Albion. Mr. J. Maritsa (Mid Tyrone, N.) seconded the 7,111, Pndment. He said a more disgraceful Act was never vlaced upon the Statute Book. Mr. J. McDERat ID (President of the Board of Trade) =fiztid they had beard a great deal of the Act of Union =41,1 its injustices;but he would point out that the Act le no way governed the present representation of lleland in the House of Commons. That was fixed by ‘443 Reform Act of 1832. The Government were pre- :ftred at all times to give Ireland the same treatment 03 they gave to England and they were willing to grant "et any extension of local self-government which her ,(3rtdltion meried. For seven hundred years before the I.et of Union Ireland had been in a chronic state of qlseffection. After the passing of the Act she pros- Tered in such a way that she had never done previously, 11 population, wealth and industries. The Bill intro- read by the Government was absolutely fair and was lr ahead of anything hitherto proposed by any respon- party. The amendment showed that the real aim at the Nationalists was separation. The amendment was defeated by 21 votes to 15. Mr. E. ENGLAND (Warrington, L) then resumed the debate on the Bill as a whole. He said the present ').stem of enfranchisement was a discredit to their 9vilizatiun and intelligence. The Bill was, however, Complete as it did not include female franchise. At re same time, it was not altogether bodes it did help and extend the franchise. He approvbd of votes given to soldiers and sailors. Those who opposed v:.at part of the measure were not true Liberals and ,a,**3 not acting up to democratic ideas. He "aracterized the granting of the franchise to graduates •4-,.the Universities as having a touch of" snobocracy." ,ee Bill had been amended by the abolition of plural Iv'oting, and under the circumstances the Liberals °._uld not oppose it. ; mr. F. A. JENKINS (Postmaster General), said he was at favour of the enfranckisement of all women who had th'e qualification. He was of the opinion that there were nley women in Warrington who were more capable using the vote than most men voters. He strongly oll,Pported the proposed abolition of canvassing but .iected to the closing of public houses on election "ItY. The voter nowadays had a little commonsense led there was no reason why he should be debarred 'Om having refreshments. t Mr. P. O'BRIEN (North St. Pancras, L.) said he did not b!_ilard the Bill as perfect but it was sufficiently good to „warrant his heartily supporting it. The Tories were rrogresaing wonderfully. (Laughter.) He did not 'llree with the proposed reduction of Ireland's repre- , litentation •' it would be going back on a bargain. The Nisattire of Ireland was in the bands of the English Pais owe and he believed the question would eventually "e settled to the satisfaction of Ireland and to the °redit of England. Mr. W. J. WALKER (Bermondsey, I.) said it was not 11jten that he found himself in agreement with a Tory uvernment, but be heartily supported the Bill. He .eeotamended the Minister of Education to educate `olne of the Liberal members as to what was meant by [tic graduate of a University." (Laughter.) The pro- 're, al to give a vote to income tax payers would remove tong-felt grievance. Many young men in responsible reitions who were assessed for income tax never had vote owing to their living in lodgings. He strongly heDnorted, as a temperance reformer, the closing of "tliblie-housAs during the hours of poll. Those Zeo knew the squalor of districts like Bermondsey also lt,,ew what an influence the liquor bad in elections. file ATTORNEY-GENERAL in his reply to tbe debate nid the Irish members were inconsistent in maintaining at there was no moral obligation on their part to the Act of Union, and yet in objecting ta its being Pered with. He was surprised that not one member '°, the opposition had had the courage to discuss the question of the House of Lords which loomed sa large °Utsiae. He replied to the National members on the Itlestion of the depopulation of Ireland, and still main- lit!ned that that was largely due to the agitation of the rlah Land League and similar organizations. b When it was announced that the amendment that the :All be read three months hence had been abandoned ere were hisses from the Nationalists. The Bill as amended was passed by 47 votes to 13, tile Liberals supporting it.</p>
