      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>POOR LAW REFORM.</head>
        <p>POOR LAW REFORM.</p>
        <p>week the subject of debate will be a bill" to ovide for the bettor working of the laws relating to relief of poor persons in England and Wales." Its ard as follows :</p>
        <p>he it enacted &amp;c. (I). That the status and salary of the head of the ;acal Government Board be that of a Secretary of 2state, and that the division of the Local Government 011 rd which has hitherto dealt with the relief of the .1.(1.20r he known as the public assistance division, and .oat the division shall print and present its annual to Parliament separate front the rest of the sePott of the Local Government Board. (2). That the area for administrative purposes of 1)11blie assistance shall in future be that of the County °I. County Borough with the exception of London, ‘vhich shall be that of the London County Council. _,.fltat the area charge shall coincide with the area of ministration. That the duties at present performed by Boards Guardians shall in future be discharged by a Local lathority in the enlarged area to be called the Public 'stance Authority, which shall be a Statutory Corn- ittee of the County or County Borough Council to be 'c'llastituted as follows : (4). One-half of the Members to be appointed by the Council 'of the County or County Borough, and the persons so appointed may be persons who are members of the CounolL (b). The other half to be co-opted for a term of three years by the Council from outside their num- ber, and to consist of persons experienced in the local administration of Public Assistance or other cognate work. One-third of the co-opted members to retire each ycar, but shall be eligible for re-election. (c). Women to he eligible for appointment under both subsections. •ul4). That the Public Assistances Authorities shall 'luP local Public Assistance Committees, for dealing applications for assistance. supervising cases and other duties as may be delegated to them. heße committees to consist of persons nominated by</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <p>•</p>
        <p>WORTH FRAMING!</p>
        <p>SO WE HAVE FRAMED IT.</p>
        <p>MR. HE'S VINDICATION.</p>
        <p>A BAD QUARTER-OF-AN-HOUR</p>
        <p>FOR MR. BALFOUR.</p>
        <p>Historic Scene in the Commons.</p>
        <p>the Urban and District Councils and Voluntary Aid 1 Committees (where established), to have power to I co-opt representatives of local hospitals. nursing associations, registered friendly societies, , and to include a proportion of women ordinarily not less than one-third. No voting member of any committee, or authority, to receive any payment whatever. (5). That a system of Organized Voluntary Aid Authorities be estsblished throughout the Country, to work in close Co-operation with the new Public Assist- ance Authorities. (6). That the Charities Commission be affiliated to the Local Government Beard, and its duties enlarged so as to include the organization, registration, and general supervision of all Endowed and Voluntary Aid Societies. (7). That general mixed Workhouses be abolished, and indoor assistance be given in separate institutions, appropriate to the classes of applicants. Children in no case to be maintained in workhouses, but by a system of boarding out or Cottage Homes. The Aged and Infirm to be classified according to the record of their lives and conduct, respectable aged persons being accommodated in small homes, and Sick, Able-bodied men, Able-bodied women, Vagrants. Feeble-minded and epileptics being divided and treated with a view to cure and restoration. (8). That there shall be one uniform regulation governing "Home Assistance." which shall (except in urgent cases) be givin only after thorough enquiry and under proper local supervision, and shall in all cases be adequate to meet the needs of the recipient. (9). That assistance to able-bodied persons shall only be granted in co-operation with the Authorities dealing witd Unemployment'whose agencies and works shall be made available for labour, tests and other similar purposes. (10). That on the application of the Public Assist- ance Authority, the local Justices of the Peace shall have power to commit to a" Detention Colony" under the Home Office persons guilty of persistent indulgence in vicious or pernicious habits. (11). That no person shall be disfranchised on account of the receipt of Public Assistance except those who have received such assistance (other than Medical Relief) for three months or more in the qualifying year. (12). That in all cases of indirect payment of rates hy those who elect Local Authorities the landlord or his agent shall shew as a separate item on the receipt for rent the amount paid on account of rates.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Wednesday, the Lord Advocate vindicated himself in the completest possible manner against the charges of Mr. Balfour, and moreover gave the Leader of the Opposition a tu quoque which he is not likely to forget as long as he lives. Mr. Ure rose at five minutes to ten, and his rising was the signal for one of the most remarkable demonstrations that has taken place in the whole course of this Parliament. The ringing cheers from crowded Ministerial and Labour benches were prolonged for several minutes. Mr. Balfour was absent, but came in shortly afterwards, and was given an ovation by his friends. "The fact that I stand here," said Mr. Ure, "is evidence that I am not unworthy to hold office."</p>
        <p>Again the cheers broke out. "My advocacy of this Bill," he went on, " has not been characterised by any argu- ment calculated to bring disgrace upon my upbringing, upon my profession, upon my honour or upon my country." Once more the cheers. "I believe I shall be able to satisfy this House that I am not guilty of the grave and serious charge against my personal honour." Mr. Balfour made his appearance with a, scornful smile on his face. Speaking with- out the assistance of a single note the Lord- Advocate repudiated Mr. Balfour's wicked slanders amid vehement demonstrations of approval from the House. The speech which followed was surpassingly brilliant. "Taxes," cried Mr. Ure, " cannot exist side by side with Socialism, since taxes are raised upon private property which Socialism would abolish." The drama deepened when Mr. tire turned to his error in stating the Buccleuch case. He admitted the slip, but denied that in giving such instances he alleged rapacity. With exquisite skill he separated Mr. Long from Mr. Balfour, paying a high tribute to Mr. Long's fairness. A STARTLING REVELATION.</p>
        <p>Having- isolated his slanderous opponent he then produced, amid indescribable enthusiasm, a telegram from Mr. Balfour, sent by him to the electors of Worcestershire, for the purpose of a by-election, in which he had told the old people in effect that they would not get their pensions unless they voted against Free Trade. "A cold and calculated lie" cried Mr. Dillon. "The hon. member must withdraw," ruled the Speaker with stern promptitude, and amid a scene of historic excitement Mr. Dillon said "I was, Mr. Speaker, only following the language used by the Leader of the Opposition, but I apologise!" Mr. Speaker, again rose and delivered a thrust which made Mr. Balfour's smile wither in dismay. "Language may be used outside this House which is not per- mitted across this floor." The condemnation was followed by volleys of cheering. "And, that is why," said Mr. Dillon," he used it outside this House." "That telegram," said Mr. Ure, with deadly quietness, "was intended to cause apprehension in the minds of poor old folk." No statesman for a genera- tion has received such a shattering castigation.</p>
        <p>DRAMATIC SCENE. Then foljipwed what was perhaps the most dramatic ssage of fife speech. Leaning over the dispatch box, and looking straight at Mr. Balfour, who was stretched lazily on the bench opposite, Mr. Ure said: "Sir, is that licensed calumny? Is that a</p>
        <p>double crime? Is that 'a cold and calculated lie? Did the man who said that bring dis- grace upon his upbringing or his high position and upon his country ? " The last words were almost inaudible, for Mr. Tire had dropped his ringing tones under the stress of emotion. _ .</p>
        <p>Mr. Ure's finishing, words electrified his friends. "I hate to have to make such a speech. but I am compelled to do so, as it affects my personal honour. Accusations couched in language such as this happily find no parallel in the history of the House since the days when it was open to a man to defend an attack with his own right hand." Loud cheers, renewed again •and again, marked the finish of the speech. Some members, carried away by their enthusiasm, clapped their hands vigorously. Mr. Balfour's reply was in no sense a with- drawal of the slanderous accusations he had been guilty of. He went on to say there was only one part, one point, on which all were agreed, namely, that national obliga- tions should be sacred. The only meaning which could be put on the Lord Advocate's speeches in the country when he referred to the question of old-age pensions was that there were certain obligations which were not sacred in the eyes of his political oppo- nents—(loud cheers)—and that those obliga- tions were in danger of repudiation by those political opponents when they came into office. The Lord Advocate in this matter was 'absolutely without excuse. (Loud Opposition cheers.) He told the House solemnly on his word of honour that he had no feeling against the Lord Advocate. (Ministerial cries of" Oh ! Oh !") "Never," added Mr. Balfour, with warmth "but I honestly admit I was moved with indignation." THE PREMIERS DENUNCIATION OF MR. BALFOUR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Asquith rose amid loud Ministerial cheers and said he confessed that he had hoped the 'right hon. gentleman had got up to fulfil what was the almost 'universal expectation from every quarter of the House after the dignified and simple and con- vincing statement of the Lord Advocate, namely to express his regret that in a moment of heat and unusual excitement he had been betrayed into making charges -Which it was absolutely impossible to sub- stantiate. (Loud Ministerial cheers and Opposition cries of "Oh, oh.") What was the charge which had been made against the Lord Advocate? It was that he had deliberately put forward a frigid and calculated lie. This was a departure from the best traditions of British public life, and in view of the esteem in which Mr. Ure was held it was an outrage upon our public life. (Loud Ministerial cheers). There was never a more discreditable chapter in the whole history of British politics than that of the: Unionists in regard to old age pensions. For ten years they had promised them, and had dangled them before the electorate. They had been used to catch votes, and yet they had never raised a finger to carry out their pledges. In conclusion, Mr. Asquith said, referring to Mr. Ure</p>
        <p>"The right hon. gentleman has made a speech which will live in our annals. It is one of the finest and not dignified vindica- tions of personal honour and political con- sistency. He has repudiated and disproved the discredited and outrageous suggestion made against his personal honour. "I venture to say to him, in the name of the vast majority of this House to-night, that he leaves its precincts with the honour and confidence of his colleagues and his fellow members." _ _</p>
        <p>Loud and prolonged Ministerial cheers followed, and then, on the motion of Mr. Chaplin, the debate was adjourned.</p>
        <p>was mostly possessed by the people who were young and pretty. She asked the audience if they thought. that Mr. John Burns was a friend of women, to which she received a prompt answer in the negative. She said that Mr. thiros had proposed to deny married women the right to work in factories and had actually brought in a bill for that. If women were married and went into factories they had a very good reason for doing so. If they were deprived of that it meant that they were then driving them back upon underpaid work. It was possible that the women had invalid husbands to support and what would they do if their support were taken away from them ? Of course, they said that women could go on the rateri,hut it' they went on the rates they lost their old age pensions and other things. if women had to leave the factories they would be forced to work at something cis° such as " charing " and "laundry work," which was a good deal harder than work in factories. It had been stated that women had not time to vote. Well, it was a disgraceful thing if she had not. If eight hours work were enough for a man, why on earth was it not enough for a woman who was so tench weaker? Referring to the wages of women received in Warrington she said that they were disgraceful. She knew some people who had made cotton blouses for shops in Warrington for 4d. each, common shirts 4d. each, and flannel shirts is. each and they had to provide their own implements She also knew of a girl who worked in a Warrington ' shop and received 6s. a week and was supposed to make V,. skirts a week for that. Queen Victoria in a letter, that might have been womanly—womanly was a difliculy word to define—said" You know, women are not made for ruling ?" (Laughter.) Though her son reached years of discretion 30 or 40 years beforwshe died the Queen clung' grimly to the crown to the end. If she did not believe in women ruling, why did not she abdicate in her son's favour? \Thy did not she live up to her say- ings? She (the speaker) did not believe a bit in what, Queen Victoria had said. The Empire got on very well under Queens Victoria, Elizabeth and Anne. She theught that when women had power in politics men would come up to the scratch more tban they did at present. The injustices that women were suffering from were obvious. She said that 9 man could divorce a woman for what a woman could not divorce a man. That was unjust. Tf an only son died and left money and his father was alive it, went to him, but if only his mother were alive it did not go to her but to his next of kin. It was not only laws that were unjust it was also charities. She then mentioned that there were not the homes for girls,' or lodging houses as there were for the other sex. A woman risked her life more universally than a man as she had to carry on the race. She maintained that it, was not brute force that governed a country or all nations Would be governed by democracy. Nations were govrned by ideals. Concluding she said that it was not only it question of politics, but of morals that the women ought to have the vote, (Applause). An interesting discussion followed, some holding that Adult Suffrage should he pressed forward and others pleading for a limited measure first as they would be able to get the full franchise earlier than if they asked striiight away for Adult Suffrage.</p>
        <p>Baptist Church Sale of Work. The Sale of Work in aid of the funds of the Golborne-street Baptist Church was brought to a close on Saturday evening, when a short conceit was given by the Harmonic Quartette Party and Mr. (linings, the phrenologist from Cambridge, continued the reading of heads. His delineations have evoked con- siderable amusement. The proceeds of the sale will clear the deficit on the current account.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>RHEUMATISM RECEIPT.</head>
