      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>WOMEN'S SUFFRAGE.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S SUFFRAGE.</p>
        <p>Discussed at the I.L.P.</p>
        <p>A good muster of members of the I.L.P. debating class assembled in the I.L.P. quarters on Tuesday even- ing and discussed t.he question of Women's Suffrage. Mrs. Ransome presided and Miss Waring was the prin- cipal speaker. Mrs. RANSOME said that at first sight it might seem rather unnecessary for them to place before them their aims in regard to the question .of Women's Suffrage, but when their society gave them au invitation they must of course accede to it. Being a Labour Party, she expected that most of them were in favour of adult suffrage, and perhaps they did not support a limited measure, so they no doubt would have an interesting discussion. The Women's Suffrage Society asked for the vote for women "on the same terms as it is or as it may be granted to men." Although members of their society believed in adult suffrage, they were simply asking that women should be treated, in the question of the vote, just the same as men. They thought that by putting forward the women's case first, in the end they would have adult suffrage sooner. Even if women Y,-ere granted the vote as they asked for it, it would not mean many more electors. (Applause.) Miss WARING in a somewhat lengthy address said that there were a great many people who allowed that it was just that a woman should have the vote,but they maid," Why have justice when you have chivalry ? " She wanted to know what the value of chivalry was when it did not include justice. When they came to think of it justice was the fl-st step toward chivalry. If they were going to be chivalrous it was only just that women should have the vote. Centuries ago the English constitution decided that taxation should not be without representasion. If that were so why did not the women have the vote? The cotton and woollen industries were almost supreme in England. Women were the chief employees, and had therefore produced the great wealth derived from those industries. The best chivalry of all was to give the down-trodden the power to help themselves—(hear, bear)—and not to help them when they wer in need. The vote would help them very largely towards the raising of women's wages. In a speech at the Albert Hall, the Chancellor of the Exchequer said that if women had the vote it would be impossible fer the Government to pay their women post office officials less than men got for the same work. They were told that the home was the women's sphere. In what way were women tree in the home? She had not the right of guardianship to her own children. While he lived the husband was the guardian of the children,and if he died he could appoint another guardian. How could those chivalrous gentlemen endure to see 51 millions of women earning their own living, and on the average only earning is. 6d. a week. It had been said that women were usurping men's work. It would be far easier to prove that men were doing women's work. What a man could do successfully was a man's work and what a woman could do successfully was a woman's work. She thought that the very undesirable state of affairs with regard to the employment of women would he remedied if women were granted the vote. Sixty years ago when the ordinary man was without a vote the candidates took no more notice of him than they did of a dog running down a street, but now it was all different and it would be the same when the women got the vote: they would run after the women the same as they had run after the men and they would then have power. Women had an influence now, but thatinfluence</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>BUDGET.</head>
        <p>THE BUDGET.</p>
        <p>SPEECHES IN THE HOUSE BY LOCAL M.P.'S.</p>
        <p>Mr. Crosfield and the Alternative of Tariff Reform.</p>
        <p>SIR JOHN BRUNNER ON INCREMENTS.</p>
        <p>In the third reading debate on the Budget in the House of Commons on Tuesday, important speeches were delivered by three local -members, viz., Mr. Crosfield, Sir John Brunner, and Mr. Seddon. The Member for Warrington was preceded by Mr. Fell (U.—Great Yarmouth) who said the Budget had greatly increased unemployment, parti- cularly in the building trade and the trades connected with the licensed victualling. Altogether it was estimated that the Budget would throw out of employ- ment 189,000 people.</p>
        <p>of shame in asking any payment whatever, except respect of liquor and tobacco, from a man earning no more than 12s. a week. Taxation was a demand by the community from the individual in payment of services rendered. What service had the community rendered to a man who was only getting 12s. a week? (Cheers). He deplored the need for Dread- noughts, but when the House decided that Dreadnoughts were to be bought he took it_to_be his duty to pay his share towards them.</p>
        <p>While he had no pity or those who first cried for Dreadnoughts and then refused to pay for them—(Mr. Carlile : "Who- are they ? ' and Labour cries of "You,"),—he was delighted to offer them some con- solation, for he did not believe the need for Dread- noughts would last very long. The Germans had. learnt better, and would, he believed, so conduct their affairs in future that our need for Dreadnoughts would diminish.—(Cheers.) He rejoiced in the increase of the whisky tax, and that the result had been a reduction in the consumption of whisky. That reduction meant grater sobriety and happiness in the</p>
        <p>homes Of the poor, and it meant' ihat the money formerly spent on whisky was now spent on commodi- ties that gave ten times the amount of labour that the manufacture of whisky did. Referring to old age pensions, he said the money was immediately con- verted into goods, and this expenditure of the poor did more to improve trade than the expenditure of the wealthy. He looked upon the land taxes as only the beginning of a great reform. A reform of local rating would do more even than the Budget to bring land into occupation and to distribute the public burdens more fairly among the community. In the matter of local rating things were very far wrong. The pro- portion of the poor rate was less upon one house cost- mg £lOO,OOO than upon twenty costing £5,000 each; it was _less upon the twenty than it was upon one</p>
        <p>_ hundred costing £l,OOO each ; and, again, it as leas upon the hundred than upon five hundred costing 1200 each. Mr. Snowden: The same is true of rent.</p>
        <p>Sir John Brunner: Quite so. If they took cubic. contents a house in the East End of London was much more highly rented than a house in the West End. He welcomed especially the reversion duty. It was hardly an exaggeration to say that every hundred years we had to buy our towns back again from a. few individuals. Be would very much rather pay</p>
        <p>that enormous fine to the St-ate than to a lei;r- individuals. The lessee in this country was in the position of a toad under the hamow. He lived under a system of grinding oppression, which had been perfected and refined and polished through the desires of generation after generation of grasping superiors and the extreme and highly paid ability of a learned profession. (Cheers.) He welcomed the reversion duty as an earnest of the intention of the-Liberal leaders to bring to an end honestly and by fair payment the leasehold system. If they could make all leaseholders freeholders they would have an amount of trade springing up which would exceed anything the country had ever known. With regard to the question of development, he was convinced that ynless we abandoned the policy of laissez laire for one of State action the trade of the country would_ suffer. He did not agree with the Attorney-General that State or municipal action was a full definition of Socialism. If it were, he would fall down and worship the next lamp-post he looked at. (Laughter.) He desired that trade should prosper and extend until all felt the benefit, and it was because the Budget gave good promise in that direction that he would vote for it with great cheeriness. (Cheers). A BREAKWATER AGAINST FOOD TAXATION. Mr. Seddon (L.-lb.—Newton) expressed his pleasure at having an opportunity of supporting the Budget. It was not a Budget that warmed his heart to the greatest enthusiasm. He would probably be much' more enthusiastic over a Budget introduced by Mr. Snowden. But be accepted this Budget as a means of doing something to mitigate the social evils of the present time. If they were anxious to prevent the great army of misery and want from becoming a law- less mob they had to do something to lift it out of the slough of despond. Ile was opposed to indirect taxa- tion of any sort because it hit the poor hardest. The duty on tea was not only burdensome. but unjust to the poorest in the land, who bought the lowest grades of tea. If ,our taxes must be indirect they ought to be ad.raloretn. But he was prepared to support the Budget because it did not increase the taxes upon neeessaries of life. . People could stop smoking and drinking whisky, and as they were not necessaries he accepted the taxes upon them. Much had been said about unemployment. Surely no man believed for a moment that fiscal juggling would solve the unemployed problem unless he was blinded by Tariff' Reform "leaflets and Budget Protest League pamphlets. The statistics of unemployment in pro- tected countries showed that such a belief was</p>
        <p>unjustified. He supported the Budget for the reason that it was a breakwater against a tax upon the food of the people. When he was in Germany a short time ago with a number of other members of the Labour party he was struck by the high percentage of con- sumptives in Berlin, and was told on inquiry that it was due to the fact that owing to the high taxes upon food the German workmqn was unable to buy the same sustaining load as the British workman could buy under Free Trade. (Cheers.) STEAK 2s. 3n. A POtND.</p>
        <p>I Visited a friend of mine in Berlin, Mr. Seddon went on, and partook not of horseflesh but of a decent beefsteak. I commented on the quality of it, and my friend's wife replied It ought to be good. It cost me two marks (25.) per pound. I mentioned that fact when I returned, and in July I wrote tp my friend and asked him to get his wife to buy a pound of beefsteak in that city and send me the bill--of course I did not want the steak. (Laughter.) I got the bill. It was dated from a Berlin butcher's on July 20th, and the. pound of steak cost 2 marks 25 pfennig—that is con- considerably over 2s. per lb. I believe that if the working classes of .this country had to pay that amount for a pound of beefsteak the stamina of the nation would rapidly go down.—(Cheers.) I support the Budget because it will prevent that catastrophe. I hope it will pass with a majority which will give the other House pause. If they are once more going to fulfil the old saying, " Whom the gods wish to destroy they first drive mad," they will throw the Budget out. We shall be quite ready for that event. We Shall not go to the country with any misgiving. We shall go believing that we have more than one account to settle With that Chamber,believing that this Budget is based upon right principles, believing that it aims at the prospe, ity of the greatest number, and, believing that it will save the workers of this country' from a great catastrophe.—(Cheers.). THE THIRD READING CARRIED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>MR.. CROSFIELD'S SPEECH,</head>
        <p>MR.. CROSFIELD'S SPEECH,</p>
        <p>Mr. Crosfield said :—The Hon. Member for Yarmouth has devoted a good part of his speech to the question of employment, and I propose in the course of the brief remarks I am about to make to refer to that question. But I would like first of all to give one or two reasons why I most emphatically support the Budget, and shall vote for the Third Reading of the Bill. In the first place I think there is pretty general agreement on both sides of the House that the money is wanted. I think further there is a consider- able amount of agreement as to the objects for which the money is wanted. Imperial Defence, and Old Age Pensions, and the best possible educational equipment for the young, and purer air and more of it. -for both old and young, and particularly for the populations of our overcrowded and con- gested centres—these things cost money; and we also need money for the remedial measures that are necessary to mitigate to some extent unmerited hardship and distress. (Hear, hear.) Welk I sup- port this Buduet because I am perfectly satisfied that the money is wanted and I most heartily approve of the way in which the money is going to be spent. (Hear, hear.) I must say that to my mind these remedial measures to which I have referred strike me as becoming more and more urgent every year that passes. It is said as Civilisation progresses, the capacity for enjoyment among civilised people increases, but I think it is very important for us to remember that unfortunately there is another side to the picture, and that the capacity for suffering also Increases with it. That is one of the reasons why I entirely agree with what the Chief Secretary for Ireland said the other day, that it is many a long year since any measure has been passed by Parliament more calcu- lated to reduce the sum of human suffering and to add to human happiness, than the Old Age Pensions Act that %as passed last year. (Hear, hear). That is a humane measure for our old folk of over seventy. Ido not know that the Contributory. Schemes for which this Budget provides funds --con- ' tribntory schemes of insurance against the risk of sickness, of premature breakdown, and of unemploy- ment—l do not know that these are not every bit as important as the Old Age Pensions Scheme itself. We need money for these schemes, and we need money for the Development Bill, and for providing Labour Exchanges, and I must here say that these do strike me as two very practical ways of helping to mitigate what to my mind is perhaps the greatest curse of modern life—the evil and tiagedy of unemployment. (Ht-ar, hear.) That brings me to another reason why I very strongly support the Budget. I feel as the hon. member for Salford (Mr. Belloc) put it in his very able speech this afternoon, that if it were only on the question of the alternative I should be a strong supporter of the Budget, because although the Hon. Member for Yarmouth would not tell us etactly what the alternative plan is we do know a good deal about it. It has been preached at the street corners all over the country, and it is no secret to us in the great manufacturing districts of England. We know perfectly well that by this alternative plan we are deliberately invited to increase the cost of manufacture in the mills and factories and workshops of the whole country. I submit that that will have a simply terrible effect on the question of employment in both the great branches of our vast national industries—both in our, export trade and in our home trade. We *have had one illus- tration of that given this afternoon by the Hon. Member for Preston (Mr. Cox) in his able and brilliant speech. He referred to the cotton trade and we all know that the cotton trade of</p>
        <p>Lancashire, together with our shi pbu ild ng and our carrying trade is the wonder and the envy of the whole world. (Hear, hear.) I would like just for a moment to consider what the effect on the cotton trade of Lancashire will be if this Budget is defeated and with it the Government. We know that even since 1903 when the Right Hon. Member for West Birmingham, whose illness we all regret, told us that cotton was doomed, there has been an enormous development of</p>
        <p>that Industry in Lancashire—an industly that pro- vides directly and indiretly employment for literally millions of our population. A certain amount of that increased trade and of the whole cotton trade is held b 3 pretty narrow margins, but it is held and in the vast neutral markets of the -world Lancashire leads the Ly. What, I would ask, is going to happen if with ncreased cost of manufacture we lose ten, fifteen or</p>
        <p>way</p>
        <p>twenty per cent. of that over-sea cotton trade? Ido not say that cheapness of production is the only essential to the great success of the cotton industry But it is one of the essential factors, and if the cost of production is increased and a considerable fraction of the trade is lost, then I do say that there will be an increase of unemployment that is simply terrible to contemplate. (Hear,- hear.) And at the same time while laying stress on the eotton trade, I must say that the results on the whole of our home industries will be more serious still. Increased prices mean diminished purchasing power by the people, and therefore a diminished demand for manufactures and for labour to produce them. (Hear, hear.) In that connection there is another reason, and one that appeals to me most strongly for supporting the Budget, and that is the question of industrial peace. In this illustration quoted by the Member for Preston of the cotton trade we have considered what would be the effect of increased cost of manufacture. May I venture to carry the illustration just a step further? Suppose you had increased cost of manu- facture and a certain reduction in our over-sea cotton trade, you have beside that increased cost of manufac- ture a certain increase in the cost of living in the country. What follows when yon have such an increase in the cost of living? The Lancashire operative at one goes to the cotton spinner and asks for an advance in wages to meet it. The cotton spinner would in this case say, "No, my trade is down, my output is reduced, and with reduced output all stand- ing charges are a heavier burden upon me as a manu- facturer. If lam to recover that 15 or :?0 per cent. of the trade and keep my mills going and find employment for the operatives I must reduce my wages bill." That is why I submit that the policy of the Tariff Reformers takes us along the road that leads to industrial strife. (Hear, hear and shame). There- fore in conclusion I give emphatic support to' the Budget and I am prepared to go on fighting for the principles which it embodies. (Cheers.) Ido for the</p>
        <p>'following reasons : I support it because it does not put a single penny of additional taxation on any single article which is vital and necessary to .our population as a whole. Because it does very substantially develop a principle that we already have in our taxation, the principle of graduation,so that through this whole scheme of taxation the Chancellor of the Exchequer has carefully borne in mind the ability to pay of those from whom he 'asks the money • and I also support this Budget because in the Land Taxes we have embodied the great principle that wealth socially created shall con- tribute to social needs. (Hear, hear.) I respectfully submit to the House that a policy of this kind, narrow- ing the chasm between extreme opulence and extreme poverty, and tending in the direction of a better dis- tribution• of wealth, makes not only for industrial peace but for the welfare and the prosperity of our whole people. (Ministerial cheers). . SIR JOHN BRUNNER'S SPEECH.</p>
        <p>Sir John Brunner (L—Northwich), in another part of the debate thanked the Chancellor of the Exchequer for the brilliant lucidity and good temper with which l be had put the case for the Budget before the House and the country, and congratulated him on the fact that he was to-day as good a man physically as when he began his task. (Cheers and laughter.) The right hon. gentlemen was a proof of the proverb that hard words broke no bones, and he was glad to gather from a recent speech that the leader of the Opposition did not actually hate Mr. Lloyd-George. (Laughter). The Budget was framed to find money for Dreadnoughts and old-age perisions. He believed it was framed on perfectly sound lines, especially in that it made its chief demands upon the men of wealth. No man, woman, or child' would go short by a jot of food, clothing, or adequate shelter on account of the Budget. (Cheers). He, however, felVa sense</p>
