      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <p>Mr.</p>
        <p>WALTER LONG, MR. LONG AND quite unintentionally, THE DUKES. as at would appear, made something of a sensation in' the House of Commons on Wednesday. He said that he and his friends opposed the Budget with all their strength, both in the House "and elsewhere." This was at once interpreted as a signal to the House of Lords, whereupon Mr. LONG ;hastened to explain what he really meant. He was not thinking of the House of Lords, he said, but of the public platform. We should have been inclined to suppose that Mr. WALTER LONG thinks of the public platform as little as possible just at present. His experiences thereon have not been very agreeable. He supposed -himself to be capable of destroying the Budget out of doors, and founded the Budget Protest League with that end in view. But the country would not protest. Even ticket meetings would not protest with any approach to unanimity. The Budget Protest League is now practically defunct, and if Mr. WALTER LONG had been willing to accept the verdict of the public itiatform, and to act upon it, he would on Wednesday have been speaking on the side of the Government, not against the Budget, but in its favour. Another remark made by Mr. WALTER LONG, in his rather panicky speech, calls for a, brief notice. The CHANCELLOR of the EXCHEQUER, he said, had led his followers to an attack on the Dukes. However, he :added, the charges made Against the Dukes and other owners of property are now not 'very much heard of. This 'must have sur- prised most of Mr. LONG'S hearers. The Dukes not only called attention to them- selves in the first place by threatening to put :a stop to their charitable contributions, but they have since kept themselves in evidence in many quite remarkable ways. The DUKE .of NORTHUMBERLAND, who told the House ,of Lords the other day that there is no ' -urgency about the provision of cottages in-the rural districts, though everyone else knows -that this is one of the most pressingly urgent questions of the day, has had a number of ,cottages on his own estate closed because they ate not fit to live in. More recently— within the last day or two, in fact—one of the Urban District Councils of Northumber- land has spoken its mind very frankly about .other Ducal cottages which are uninhabitable Nothing that Mr. LLOYD GEORGE has said, at Limehouse or elsewhere,is so nicely calculated 'to concentrate attention on the Dukes as the action which has been taken by some of their own order just when the most ordinary .common-sense should have suggested to them that their best policy would be that of BRER RABBrrr.. They should have "laid low and said nuffin."</p>
        <p>We have not for our own part noticed any falling off in public : interest in the Dukes. Mr. WALTER LONG is certainly very much mistaken in that particular. The Ducal caste does something almost every day to awaken astonished attention. The edict that has gone forth at Edensor to the effect that the .cottagers in that very remarkable village are not to keep fowls, or to take lodgers, or to •sell refreshments, is a quite startling and ill-timed example of how oppressively great 'landowners sometimes use ;their powers. But, as Mr. LONG admitted, the Budget does not propose to deal with matters of this kind. It contains no provisions for limiting the powers or the privileges of the Dukes. If the Finance Bill should pass into law, the Dukes, will still, just as before, be at full liberty to make the rural districts intolerable places to live in. All that is proposed is to lay a little more taxation on wealthy persons, almost in exact proportion to the new taxa- tion that is laid on people with small incomes. What will the DUKES and their friends do in the matter: That is the immediate ques- tion of the moment. It seems, from a speech .of the DUKE OF NORFOLK. that they do not at present know. They have not made up their minds. The question will take a Peremptory form in something less than 'three weeks hence. The Finance Bill left the House of Commons on Thursday with an immense majority behind it. Now we •shall see what we shall see.</p>
        <p>CEMETERY HOTEL BOWLING CLUB. Visit of Mr. Crosfield, M. P. Last evening Mr. A. H. Crosfleld, M.P., attended the annual tea and social gathering in connection with the Cemetery Hotel Bowling Club and presentad the cup and medals gained by the club by their brilliant per- formance in winning the Lancashire and Cheshire Championship, as well as the prizes awarded in the Closing handicap of the season. The winners of the handicap awards were :—Messrs. T. Bolton, A. Warburton, A. Forster, R. Antrobus. NV. Gibson, E q.leave, A. N.l. Burdett. C. Holden, W. J. Wood, A. -B,cott, W. Drury, T. Hnmphreys, T. Bellis.R.Trigger, Rathbone W. Barton, G. Monks, W. Taylor, R. nothwell, W. Cottom. W. Swanton, J. Melbourne, W. 'Vernonf3utterworth, F. Yarwood, J. Ashton, G. Ball sear., A. and J. Baldwin. The toast of 'The President" was honoured on the I3roposition of Councillor LOCKER. In responding, Mr. CROSFIELD referred to the lrapending political crisis, but said .he was sure the friendship he had enjoyed for twenty years with the members of that club would not suffer by reason of ny party warfare. Whatever the future had in store, 'e hoped to have the pleasure for many years of coming aown to congratulate the members of the Cemetery sllotel Club on such signal victories as had been won 'lB year, on their prowess as bowlers, their ability as sport amen and on the splendid tone which had always tharacterised the club. (Applause.) ...During the evening songs were effectively given by esars. Humphreys, Draper, Harrison and Sutton. Mr. r oster was the accompanist.</p>
        <p>It was undoubtedly ELECTION PROSPECTS. a source of pleasure MR. CROSFIELD to Mr. Crosfield's AND THE BUDGET. constituents in War- rington to find on Wednesday morning that he had, the previ- ous day, taken a considerable and creditable part in the striking debate on the third read- ing of the Finance Bill in the House of Commons. There were some notable speeches on this occasion and Mr. Crosfield I was certainly in good company. He inter- vened in a discussion between such masters of forensic skill as Mr. A. Chamberlain, Sir W. S. Robson, Mr. Harold Cox, Mr. Philip Snowden, Sir John Brunner, and Mr. G. Harwood, and made a thoughtful, well- reasoned defence of the finance proposals of the Budget which earned respectful hearing. He condemned the Tariff Reform alternative with all the weight of a large manufacturer and expert in those concomitants which bear most closely upon the industrial situation. Employmentindustrial progress,and the cost of necessaries would all be adversely affected by protective tariffs whether scientific or otherwise, and this was the burden of Mr. Crosfield's closing argument, which undoubt- edly carried weight. It is so long since we had a representative who could catch the Speaker's eye and say something worthy of the attention of the House, that the value of Mr. Crosfield as the Borough Member is properly appreciated, and will count for a good deal when the next election comes round. .</p>
        <p>_ _ The imminence of that election is a topic which we had the opportunity of discussing with Mr. Crosfield last evening. He came to I Warrington inspired by the magnificent triumph of the Government in the closing scenes of the Budget debate, capped as it was by the convincing majority of 230 for the third and final reading which precedes the passage of the Bill to the Upper House and to a fate which no man can predict with accuracy. Mr. Crosfield, in the course of the interview, which we print in another column, gave it on high authority that the political barometer ,was set at "stormy," and reading between the lines it is obvious that there is good ground for the belief that the irresponsible and selfish crowd of " backwoodsmen " in the Lords will push the Tory party to that extreme which the saner men justly depre- cate and dread. Even Liberals must view with real concern the deadlock—financial and legis- lative—which the rejection of the Budget will inevitably bring about. But, if there must be a trial of strength between the hereditary House and the representatives of the people there could. be no clearer issue or more oppor- tune time. Mr. Crosfield emphasises the oneness of conviction permeating the ranks of all shades of Progressives that the will of the people shall prevail, and for our part we welcome the crisis which impends. Let the Lords throw down the gage of battle. It will be eagerly accepted, and in the fight to come who can doubt that the forces of democracy and the cause of humanity will prevail? The day of acquiescence in class domination has gone, never to return. England vibrates under the marching of a host as determined as Cromwell's Ironsides. Dare the Lords attempt to stay that march with the bauble of autocracy?</p>
        <p>T h e "preliminary THE MUNICIPAL canter" for the general ELECTIONS. election seems to have</p>
        <p>given both sides satis- faction in Warrington. The Liberals added Fairfield to their recent victory in Howley, and lost Whitecross, while the Tories claim Town Hall because there a Conservative replaces a Conservative Non-Trader. Won one, and lost one, is the correct result of the November Ist contest in Warrington for each political party concerned. We hardly think that the jubilation of our Tory friends is as real as assumed. They had announced that the municipal elections were to be made a test of their strength in view of the coming general election, and they imported a lot of imperial politics into the ward contests to the general discomfort of their own candidates except where—as in Whitecross—the Conservative candidate was independent enough to have none of it. On the results declared on Monday there must needs be a lot of optimism among Tories to persuade themselves that War- rington has spoken for Conservatism, for Tariff Reform, and against the Budget. For ourselves, we do not admit that these things had the slightest weight with the electorate. We do not claim Fairfield or Orford as victories for the Budget, but there does seem an indication which cannot be mistaken that certain parts of the town which have been consistently Tory are coming over to Liberalism, i.e. the south-east side comprised by Howley and Fairfield Wards. But the real test will come when the Parliamentary candidates are in the field, and of the issue then we have not the slightest fear. Take the returns from all England and Wales for November Ist and see how things are going. The " Manchester Guardian" summary of party gains up to midnight on Monday,exculsive of London, is as follows:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>LIBERALS</head>
        <p>LIBERALS</p>
        <p>52</p>
        <p>CONSERVATIVES LABOUR SOCIALISTS INDEPENDENT</p>
        <p>The comparison with the general party results of last year's elections is striking. A similar analysis of the returns last November showed the following party gains :—Con- servative, 118; Liberals, 31; Labour, 11 ; Socialists, 9; Independents, 4. In Lancashire alone this week there have been 28 Liberal gains against 11 Tory gains. This does not augur well for the magnifi- cent Conservative victory vainly imagined last week by Mr E. J. Hall, when he antici- pated the return of Mr. R. Pierpoint for Warrington, and said with more grandeur than grammar This repeated all over the length and breadth of the land would bring such a victory for England, as would cause to ring again the glorica they had had in the past, and they would go forward and hand them down to their children."</p>
        <p>Children are generally taught to say "For what we are about to receive, Lord make us truly thankful." It is fortunate that their only hope of " glory " is not confined to what the Tory party can offer, else they might very probably have to "go empty away."</p>
        <p>There are several LOCAL CONTESTS features of the six REVIEWED, contests in Warrington worthy of special note. To take a Liberal defeat first—St. John's Ward! This was a moral victory for the progressive forces and the temperance party. Mr. James Shaw, in his first fight at the polls, pulled the majority of Councillor T. S. Steel down from 522 in 1906 to 49— surely a magnificent performance. With another twelve hours' work—nay three hours—Mr. Shaw would now have been Councillor Shaw, and Mr. Steel would have been left lamenting the inconsistency of the electorate. Mr. Shaw will win next time, and we already look forward to November twelve-months hence. In Orford Ward Councillor Cunnington increased hie majority, and the Tory party will surely be tired by this of putting mine host of the "Horse and Jockey" up to be knocked</p>
        <p>down. The temperance organizations intervened here, too, and were well repaid for their spirited appeal to "keep the publican out." Mr. C. Jackson Holmes won a great victory right in the centre of the Tory camp, where even the Rylands influence was unavailing. In Whitecross the Liberal was within thirteen votes of victory, and the loss of a seat here can be largely attributed to the personal popularity of Mr. Bellian and the energy of his enthusiastic band of workers. It was not a victory for Conservatism, which, in point of fact was judiciously kept in the background. Town Hall Ward returned Mr. Jenkins because his opponent did not work, and in our opinion, notwithstanding his political colour, the better man lost. Mr. Lord can ill be spared from the Council, and we hope to see him soon return to the sphere in which he has rendered such valuable and unstint- ing service. Latchford remained blue and true to its traditions. Mr. Hewitt fought a plucky fight against overwhelming odds and unscrupulous methods. He was hardly a strong enough candidate to hope to wrest this seat from the Conservative grip and repeat the triumphs of so popular a man as the late Alderman Ellison.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Uo'‘'‘'FES AND NOTIONS. -f('</head>
        <p>Uo'‘'‘'FES AND NOTIONS. -f('</p>
        <p>The Parliamentary game of "Ure another" has been handsomely won by " Ure's truly." Old Parliamentary hands say that they never recollect seeing Mr. Balfour so crushed and humiliated as he appeared after the Lord Advocate's castigation on Wednesday. * * *</p>
        <p>As the great fight comes nearer, the enemies of the Budget have recourse to very remarkable tactics. They are trying to catch the poor man with a little ground-bait taken from the rich man's fishing basket. They cannot go into an election simply on the woes of the rich, and therefore they are trying to work up an agitation about the wrongs of the poor. It is a little difficult to see how they can make out both cases at once. * * *</p>
        <p>How is it done? Well, take an instance. A leaflet is being distributed stating that the pool' man's tobacco is being taxed more heavily than the rich man's cigars. How do they make that out? The duty on packet tobacco is now 3s. 6d. a lb. The duty on 6d. cigars is now 7s. a lb., or just twice as much. The duty on cigarettes is ss. Bd. a lb. Those are the real facts, but what do facts matter when such warriors are on the warpath.</p>
        <p>It is not by tactics- of-this kind that a great people are going to be deceived. The main issue has now become quite clear. It is between the People's Budbint and the Rich Man's Budget—a Budget that takes a small toll from wealth and a Budget that places a heavy toll on poverty. For which of these will you decide? You will soon have to decide. For the time of argument is rapidly drawing to a close and the moment of decision is on us.</p>
        <p>* * * To see ourselves as others see us is usually profitable. "The Call to the House of Lords to throw out the Budget," thinks the "Sydney Daily Telegraph," is simply an incitement to the Peerage to try and wrench the right of self-government out of the hands of the people. For if there was one thing made clearer than another at the last appeal to the country it was that the electors were in favour of Free Trade." We turn next to the "Sydney Morning Herald," which observes that the Unionists are to be called upon to make a grand final assault on Free Trade under the guise of a feint attack on Socialism, and the Lords are to be placed in the forefront of the battle, whether with equivocal motive or not we need not surmise." * * *</p>
        <p>The increasing needs of the Warrington Infirmary are becoming a source of anxiety, and it is recognized that some special effort will have to be put forth to augment the annual revenue of the charity. An important meeting to consider" ways and means "will be 4eld in the new pavilion on Monday after- noon and the presence of all townspeople who are willing to help the institution is cordi- ally invited. We hear that a million pennies scheme has been suggested, and is likely to find favour. It will be a sad reflection on the spirit of Warrington if the noble work of a splendid institution is to be allowed to suffer through financial embarassment, but we have more faith in the generosity of all sections of the public than to believe that such a state of affairs is at all likely. War- rington has shown itself willing to stand by deserving causes in the pas, and it will once more rise to the occasion.</p>
        <p>* * * I desire with all my heart the realisation of the ideal of legal equality between the sexes.—President Fallieres. There is at • this moment no wickeder person in the world than the King of the Belgians.—The Rev. C. Silvester Horne. I believe in my heart that the administra- tion of the Congo is not only a crime, but the very greatest crime ever committed in the history of the world.—Sir A. Conan Doyle. People who play golf and bridge on Sundays have not half the buoyancy and good spirits and robust strength of those who honour the Lord's Day.—The Rev. F. S. Webster.</p>
        <p>In my opinion the most Socialistic feature of the Budget is the super-tax on wealth; and I prophesy that in years to come future Chancellors will bless the present Chancellor for having instituted the super-tax.—. Mr. Philip Snowden, M.P. s *</p>
        <p>Fe* people have any idea of the enormous amount of tea which is being drunk annually. There are some interesting statistics in an article on the subject in the "Magazine of Commerce"</p>
        <p>At the present time the average annual export of tea from the countries producing it, irrespective of the amount consumed in the countries themselves, totals 1,108,828,0001 b., of</p>
        <p>a value of over £16,000,000 stirling. Of this huge total the British Empire is responsible for nearly 350,000,00014., worth no less than £9,217,000. India heads the list with a total export valued at £5,830,000, followed by that of China, valued at £5,500,000. During the last few years the annual import of tea from all sources into this country has averaged no less than 255,112,0001 b., costing us £8,683,000. We are followed by Russia and the United States.; then by Holland, Australia, Canada, Germany, and New Zealand.</p>
        <p>The Tory Party evidently think that they have got hold of a good thing in their attack on the Lord Advocate, and are prepared to work it for all it is worth. Mr. Long in a letter circulated by the Budget Protest League, suggests two precautions which the Unionist Party ought to 'adopt without delay: _</p>
        <p>- First, that wherever Mr. tire's statement has reached the people a leaflet should be circulated to the effect that there is no foundation whatever for the suggestion and, secondly, that Unionists in different parts of the country should have a brief form prepared guaranteeing the pension to such pensioner by name—the form to be signed by two or three well-known local Unionists.</p>
        <p>It is preposterous to assert thatas the result Of Mr. Ure's speeches there is any general panic among the pensioners, and this sug- gestion of the signed guarantee is a mere device to.turn a not very honest penny for the Tory Party. But if we are to have the guarantee in black-and-white, signed by prominent Unionists, why confine the guarantee to pensions? Why should not these well-known local Unionists also under- take that under Tariff Reform:</p>
        <p>(a) The foreigner shall pay the tax: (b) There shall be no increase in the cost of living; (c) Wages shall be higher; and (d) There shall be work for all? That would_ be .really something like a guarantee.</p>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Election Expenses. The expenses incurred by Mr. C. Jackson Holmes (L) in Eairfield Ward have been officially returned at -ClO 16s. Od.</p>
        <p>Messrs. Crosfield's Appeal. At the Court of Appeal on Saturday, the 'hearing was Concluded of the appeal of Joseph Crosfield and Sons, Ltd., Warrington, from a refusal of Mr. Justice Swinfen Eady, notwithstanding . the opposition of Lever Bros., Ltd., Port Sunlight; to direct the Registrar of Trademarks to proceed with the registra- tion of the word " Perfection " as applied to Crosfield's common or household soops.—Mr. Sargent having been heard on behalf of the Controller of Trade marks, Mr. Walter replied upon the whole case on behalf .of the appellants. —At the conclusion of the arguments judgment was reserved. Local Wills.</p>
        <p>The will of James Newton, of Orford-lane, Warring- ton, whose death occurred on the 11th September, has been proved by the widow, Mrs. Ellen Newton (during widowhood only), Mr. Frederick George Taylor. solicitor, and Mr. Reuben Higham; tanner, both of Warrington, the executors, at £7,113 17s. 2d. gross, and £5,459 Ws. id. net. Councillor John James Nield, of 18, Salisbury- street, Warrington, for three years the representative of the Fairfield Ward on the Warrington Town Council, and a native of Ashton-under-Lyne, who died on the' 27th June, left estate valued by the widow, Mrs. Mary Ellen Nield, the residuary legatee, at £462 his. sd. Presentation to Mr. R. W. Cook.</p>
        <p>- _ A very interesting presentation took place at the Apollo Choir annual social held in the Winmarleigh Cafe on Thursday, when Mr. R. W. Cook was pre- sented-with a secretaire as a recognition of his 1 years' faithful service to the choir as secretary. Mr. H. Berrey in making tfie presentation said he wao sorry Mr. Cook had resigned as he had been a faithful and trusted servant. Of all the secretaries of choirs he bad had to deal with Mr. Cook was the best. Mr. Cook thanked them for the kindness they had always shown towards him and said that all the members of the choir had worked very harmoniously with him. During the evening, the company of about 70 engaged in various games and a musical programme was also gone through. Mr. Cook has been smceeded by Mr. George Morrall.</p>
        <p>MOSELEYS FOOD.—A most ilgrePable and acceptable food, stimulant and restorative for _</p>
        <p>invalids and convalescents.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Miss Marie Tempest in "Penelope."</head>
        <p>Miss Marie Tempest in "Penelope."</p>
        <p>Miss Marie Tem-post, one of the most popular actresses on the London stage, pays a visit next' week to the Shakespeare Theatre,Liverpool,in "Penelope" comedy by W. Somerset Maugharn, a playwright whc has come rapidly to the front within the past year or two. • Miss Tempest is " presented" by Charles Frohman and wilt be supported by the entire -4ondon Company. The part of Penelope is one that admirably suits Miss Tempest. In London, where the piece ran this year well on from January until the Autumn, it was declared to be quite her best performance, most enjoyable alike in the comedy and in the serious moments. Miss. Tempest is one of the smartest dressed actresses of the day, and the gowns she wears in Penelope should be an attlactioo for lady visitors. The box plan is now open at the theatre-. Penelope is preceded each evening by a one act play entitled, "Pride of Regilnent" by F. D. D •ne. The engagement is fur six nights only and matinee Saturday at 2.0.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Margaret Booth. Our readers will be interested to learn that Mrs. Margaret Booth, the well-known Warrington vocalist, sang at one of the Gentlemen's Concerts at Manchester on Wednesday afternoon. Her performance is conpnented upon by S. L„ the musical critic of the "Manchester Guardian" in the following terms :—Mrs. 'Margaret Booth, an old Manchester College of Music student who has recently studied in London, hardly seemed to overcome the unnatural difficulties of the hall, and her singing was in consequence tinged with a little nervous flatness. In every other way she sang well, and she gave Italian arias by Gordigiana and Handel, well-known songs by Hadyn, Purcell, and Arne, and modern songs by Henschel, Hugo Wolf, and Brahms in appropriately varied style. Best of all was Hugo Wolf's Fussreise," which was not only well sung, but, what is almost more important; was exceedingly well accompanied by Mr. Forbes, the entrancing imitation and interludes for the piano being perhaps more " taking " than anything in the concert.</p>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Band of Hope. A very enjoyable evening was spent in the Bold- street schoolrOom on Wednesday. Mr. 11. E. O'Callaghan •of Manchester gave a very instructive and interesting limelight lecture to the members of the Band of Hope and. Sunday School scholars on "'Kindness to Animals" showing how we can yain animals from a state of savageness to be our friends and protectors, and exhortigg the young people present to be kind and .considerate to animals. Mr. Edward Plinston occupied the chair and gave some sound advice to the children, telling them when they grew up to be true to the principles they had been taught in the Band of Hope. During the evening Miss Florrie Woods sang very sweetly. A chorus entitled "Temperance Bells" was given with great effect by the members of the Band of Hope Choir. Another pleasing feature was a dialogue given by some of the little ones called " The Teetotal Dolls." ,The children had been trained by Mrs. Whitmore and Mrs. Nurse. ' Miss Holt was the accompanist. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded the lecturer and all who had taken part in the proceedings.</p>
        <p>TABLE AND HANGING LAMPS, new designs now showing.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge at., Ward ogton.—Ad vt. Williams' Jubilee Singers. Races as a whole are endowed with varying talents, and if Williams' Jubilee Singer's, who are now on a tour rout:o the world, are atritxample of their kind, the negro race is in a special degree favoured with the musical talent. These singers only arrived from Chicago on Saturday. last, and their performances in Bold-street schoolroom on Thursday and Friday evenings received well-merited applause —in fact nearly every item was encored. On Thursday evening there was only a moderate attendance, but those who were there were simply delighted for the programme was splendidly varied. Truly it is novel for an audience to spend an evening with the "blacks," but as the programme is gone through, the feeling of novelty gives way to one of admiration. The troupe is composed of the following t —Messrs. C. P. Williams (second tenbr and comedian), J. H. ,Johnson (baritone) G. L. Johnson (first. tenor) and J. S. Crabb (bass); Mesdames C. P. Williams (contralto), Mrs. Hackley (soprano), Mrs. Green (soprano), Mrs. Hobbs (contralto and pianist.) Each item was rendered in a most satisfactory manner and the blending of voices • was simply a treat to hear. . The Fropramme which was a long one, included the following "Peter, go ring dem bells" •, "Picking on a chicken bone," (chorns) ; Melody," Ding dong bells " Mrs. Williams ; " If I had a. thousand lives to live "Mr. G. 1,. Johnson •, " Emme!ine " Mr. J. H. Johnson; " Carissima '.' Mrs. Hackley ; "Steer away to Jesus " Mrs. Green ; q Asleep in the Deep" Mr. J. S. Crabb ;and " Good evening Caroline "Mr. C. P. Williams. The concluding concert will be held this evening.</p>
        <p>FLOWERS AT WHOLESALE PRICES MARKET GARDENERS AND OTHERS</p>
        <p>SUPPLIED. McDERMOTT, "Cheapest Man Alive." MARKET AND BRIDGE STREET.</p>
        <p>. Company Promoter's Failure. George 'Rennet, the Poplars, Great Sankey,appeared for his public examinntion in bankruptcy before Mr. T. J. Ridgway, yesterday at the Warrington Bankruptcy Court. Its liabilities were £356 155.2 d. and he had no assets. He had traded as a moss litter merchant and attributed his failure ta law costs, out of pocket expenses in connection with the formation of the Cambrian Peat Industries Syndicate, Ltd., and inability to obtain payment therefor. The examina- tion was closed. MOSELEYS FOOD.—" Made in a minute." Strongly recommended by the Medical Press and Medical men. Ask your doctor.—Advt. Penny Popular Concerts The popularity of these excellent concerts must be most gratifying to the promoters, who are to he complimented upon the interesting programmes which they arrange every week. There was another large audience on Saturday evening and the entertainment was much appreciated. The chairman was the Rev. T. Miller, who, during the interval gave a short address. Mr. W. Davies, the prize concertina player, was very well received; also the Lancashire humorist Mr. David Sutton. The other artistes were the Misses E Barnes and May Isherwood, and Al essrs. J. Powell and I. McArthur who acquitted themselves with credit. PEVERIL " FIRE ORATES still unsurpessed, Inspection invited.--J. S. GARLAND, Kitchen Range and Stove Warehouse, 51 to 55, Bridge at.., Warring- ton.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Death of Surgeon-Major Crockwell. Much sympathy will be felt with Mr. and Mrs. John Dodds, of Great Sankey, in the death of Mrs. Dodds' father, Surgeon-Major William Henry Crockwell,M.D., on Sunday night last at his residence, Brooks' Bar, Msnehester. Surgeon-Major Crockwell, who was 56 years of age, was formerly cOmmandet of the Man- chester Volunteer Medical Staff Corps, but retired nine or ten years ago. He was also formerly Surgeon- Major in the Army Medical Reserve of Officers. The funeral took place at Southern Cemetery on Thursday, in the presence of the relatives of the deceaskl and a large number of personal friends. The service was conducted by the Rev. N. Aspinall,M.A., of St. Edmud's Church, Whalley Range. Messrs. Satterfield, Bye and Co. had charge of the arrangements.</p>
        <p>Warrington- Charities Committee. This _committee, which has done much useful work met at the Lion Hotel on Wednesday evening when it was resolved to repeat the principal efforts of the past year, by organizing another Charity Sports for Whit-Monday 1910 on practically the same lines as the first meeting which was such a great success and by running another draw or competition tbis winter (Id. tickets) giving RAO in prizes. By each of these efforts it is hoped to raise about £lOO and thereby help to lesson the Infirmary debt and assist rhe Nursing Association. It was also resolved to give all the help in the committee's power to the proposed million pennies scheme, to wipe out the Infirmary's debt. MOSELEYS FOOD.—An ideal food for Infants. Soothing, comforting, easily digested and full of nourishment.—Advt. Women's Circle. lirs.,Clatworthy presided at the Circle on Sunday. Mrs. Letheren gave the address, and solos "Into Thy hands" and " OnlS7 a little word" were given by Miss. Ramer and Miss Mitchell. At the Young People's Society the subject was "The book, chapter or verse 'I love best in the Bible, and why? " and contribu- tions were brought by Misses Annie Roberts. Rose Tynan, Lizzie Heywood, Mr. Perkins and Rev. It. E. Letheren. On Tuesday afternoon at a well attended meeting of the B.W.T.A. Mrs. Simpson presiding, a graphic and very iuteresting Recount on a recent War with the Ardennes was 'given by Miss Bessie Duttcn, supplement:4d ,by Mts. Garnett. Mrs. Kenworthy sung very beautifully "Idle words" and " My 'ain folk." HERALD" KITCHEN RANGES save 50 per cent of fuel.—Agent .T. S. GARLAND, Ironmonger, 51. to 55 Bridge st.; Warrington.—Advt. • Death of Mrs.) William Coates.</p>
        <p>We regret to announce the death of Mrs. William Coates, of Bank House, Friars' Gate, which occurred on Thurday morning last. Mrs. Coates was a well- known resident—to the older generation more especially—and connected by descent and marriage with old Warrington families. As a daughter of Mr. Joseph Hall, of Bewsey (a notable townsman in his day), she married Mr. William Coates, the founder of the present business at Friars' Gate now carried on by his son Mr. E. Hall Coates). Until failing health intervened she was a devoted worker on behalf of the parochial institutions in Holy Trinity parish. By her family and relatives she was greatly beloved and by a wide circle of friends her memory will be long and affecti inntely cherished. Beyond a few days tit the last sh was not called upon to suffer unduly and her end was peaceful and quiet. Surviving her are three sons, viz. :—Mr. E. Hall Coates, Mr. Ernest W. Coates (Government Education Department, Cairo), and Mr. Percy C. Coates., The funeral takes place to-day Saturday) at Stretton, at four o'clock in the afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH. Annual Sale of Work.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON PARISH CHURCH. Annual Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>-4 -</p>
        <p>VISIT OF MR. AND MRS. A. H. CROSFIELD.</p>
        <p>Fairfield School presented a bright and attractive appearance on Thursday afternoon when the annual sale of work in aid of Various parochial organizations was held. The stalls were very tastefully arranged and there was a good display of useful and fancy wares. The following ladies superintended the stalls Parish Church Stall.—Mrs. Willis. Rectory Sewing Party Stall.—Mrs. J. G. Craik and Mrs. S. Taylor. _ _</p>
        <p>G.F.S. Stall.—Miss White and G.F.S. associates. Institute Stall.—Wives of the Institute members. St. Clement's Mission Stall.—Mrs. Hall. St. George's Mission Stall.—Miss Hetherington. Flower Stall.—Mrs. Farrell. Refreshment Stall.—The Misses Brooks, Sankey and Woods.</p>
        <p>Sweet Stall.—The Misses Collin. THE OPENING CEREMONY.</p>
        <p>The sale was opened on Thursday afternoon by the Ron. Col. and Mrs. Lumley (grand-daughter of the late Lord Winraarleieb) in the presence of a large gathering. The RECTOR (Rev. Canon Willis) said they hoped to make £lOO each day of the sale—(hear, hear)—and they earnestly hoped they would not ask in vain, lie had divided the objects under three heads—(l) The Church, (2) the Institute and Missions; (3) the schools and other parochial needs. That. day he wiehed to refer particularly to the Church. They had, he was thankful to say, a very good ordinary income of about £6OO a year from the offertory—about Ell a Sunday on the average—but they needed about £lOO more in order to supply their wants, and if it were not for the sale and other sources of income they would not be able to raise it. At the present time the repair fund was only about EGO and there was a great need for a substantial fund for that purpose. The church was built of soft, crumbling red sandstone. and they had constantly to be making little repairs. In addition they had two other objects under consideration. One was the better lighting of the church. He supposed the Parish Cnurch was looked upon as the finest church in Warrington, but he was told it was the worst lighted. They had electric light in the choir and Boteler chapel. but they had only ordinary gas throughout the chancel and the nave, and therefore they were considering a scheme for lighting the whole of the church by electricity. In addition, though perhaps not in the immediate future, there was the restoration of the chancel. They had made the nave as beautiful perhaps as they could make it, but they had still to do the chance !and when they undertook that they would also have to attend to the vestry and heating apparatus, and it would be a costly scheme. When he (the Rector) came to Warrington twenty-one years ago there were four notable personages living in Warrington and neighbourhood and they were all nonagenarians—namely, Mr. Beamorit, Canon Hearn, Sir Gilbert Greenall, and Lord Winmarleigh. He would never forget his first introduction to Lord Winmarleigh at the anniversary;of the Clergy Daughter* School! He was then struck with his noble and benevolent features and he always found him, as everybody else did in Warrington, one of the kindest and best of benefactor and friends. They were fortunate that day in havingq the presence of Lord Winmarleigh's grand-daughter Mrs. Lumley. (Applause). Mrs. LUMLEY commended the objects of the site and said it was always a great pleasure to be able to do something for their Church. She remembered so well that her grandfather always took the keenest interest in Warrington and in everything connected with the Church, and she need hardly say that her aunts, the Misses Wilson-Patten, also did so. (Applause). Councillor CRAIK (Rector'n warden), in proposing a vote of thanks to Cal. and Mrs. Lumley, said that as a Warringtonian it gave him particular pleasure to move such a vote to the descendant of such a fine old English gentleman as Lord Winmarleigh. Mr. C. WHITE seconded, and said he was very well known to that dear, supreme old English gentleman Colonel Wilson Patten, afterwards Lords Winmarleigh. To know him was to love and admire him. He was almost the patron saint of Warriniton, and whenever he came to the town they used to congratulate them- selves that the Colonel had once more come amongst them. It was with peculiar pleasure that he (the speaker) saw Lord Winmarleigh's direct descendant present that day, and renewed the old ties of friendship</p>
        <p>with the family of Wilson Patten. which Warrington had always held dear. (Applause). The vote was heartily accorded. Colonel LUMLEY, in responding, said It was a privi- lege every husband did not enjoy nowadays to speak on behalf of his wife. (Laughter). Considering that it was now nearly forty years since the late Lord Winmarleigh left Warrington and that the last time hie (the speaker's) wife resided there she did not drive about in a motor car but her mode of progression was a perambulator—(langhter)—it was wonderful and most touching and gratifying to see the kindly memory which they all seemed to have for the Wilson Pattetx family. (Applause). The Misses Wilson Patten took an extraordinary interest in the welfare of Warrington, , and they regretted exceedingly that they could not he present at the sale of work. He really believed one of the reasons why they did not come was that they nrst so many of the grandchildren of the girls they used to teach In the Sunday school, and it made them feet old—which none of us loved. (Laughter). The business of the sale was then proceeded with. During the afternoon a capital dramatic performance entitled "Don't let the lady go" was given by the Misses Lister Kaye and Captain Mott. In the evening there was an entertainment by girls of the G.P.S., including a pretty dance and a dialogue "Two rooms to let" in which the parts were well taken by Misses: Vera Collin, Elsie Read, Lily Hall and Dorothy Wainwright,_</p>
        <p>-YESTERDAY'S PROCEEDINGS. OPENING BY MRS. A. B. CROSFIELD</p>
        <p>Yesterday afternoon the sale was re-opened by Mrs. A. H. Crosfield wko was accompanied by Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P. Therewas agaih a good attendßnce. The RECTOR thanked the gathering most heartily for the splendid results of the previous day's sales. On the first day last year they got exactly £100: this time they had raised £l2l. (Applause). The Rector proceeded to urge the special claims of tht, two missions and the Church Institute. They owed a debt of grati- tude to Mr. Ridpath for all he had done in acquiring the extension of St. George's mission. It had cost about £1.200 and owing to the great kindness of Lady Greene!' who had given or would give in instalments the last £75, they had completed the full sum. There were, however, some little extra needs which they hoped to clear by that sale. The funds of St. Clement's Mission were now in a satisfactory state and he was thankful for it, becatne it would have been hard upon Mr. Vicars, their new deacon, who would come to them in Advent, if he had had to face a debt there. They wanted very much to make St. Clement's mission as large and comprehensive in every way as St. George's. With regard to the Institute, they had hada most generous gift of the site by Colonel Ireland- Blackburne and they had erected one portion of the Institute at a cost of £7OO, towards which they had raised £6OO. That was only the first part of the scheme. They wished to build a reading room and encourage intellectual pursuits amongst the men, and they also hoped to provide a gymnasium and a large parish hall in which their various entertainments and meetings could ' beheld. They desired to cater not only for the men but for the ladies by providing tennis courts and a croquet lawn for which there was ample space. In welcoming Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield, the Rector observed that they were all more or less politicians at the present time—(laughter)—but on that occasion they kept polities to themselves. (Laughter.) All he wished to say MR that he was sure that in their Member they had one who would be very politic that day—(laughter) —and he had brought with him a most charming and eloquent lady. (Applause). Airs. CROSFIELD. who had a very cordial reception,. after expressing the pleasure which it afforded her to be present, said she knew some people thought bazaars were rather a toil than a pleasure, but she did not share that opinion in the least. On the contrary a bazaar not only helped worthy objects, but the:e was another aspect to which she would call attention. She was sure most of then) in their youth learnt needlework not because they had a special liking for it—(laughter) —but because there was a bazaar in their town or parish and they wanted to' make something pretty and suitable in order to help the good cause. The work also developed their taste and artistic imagination because they all wished to make something original and pleasing. After expressing her admiration of the flue display of articles on the, various stalls, Mrs. Crosfield said she had much pleasure in declaring the sale open. (Applause). Mr. S. TAYLOR (people's warden) in proposing a vote of thanks to Mr. and Mrs. Crosfiald, said he had had the Pleasure of knowing Mr. Crosfield a long time and if Mrs. Crosfleld only followed in the footsteps of her husband in regard to generosity, end the patronage of sport and other objects, she would acquit herself in a proper manner and qualify for a suffragette. (Laughter.) He had no doubt Mr. Crosfield would be glad to have company in the House of Commons. Councillor LOCKER, in seconding, said the Parish Church was very fortunate in being able year after year to obtain estimable people to serve the interests of that valuable work. It was his (the speaker's) pleasure last year to second a vote of thanks to the honourable opponent of their Member. Mrs. Pierpoint showed her interest in the present sale by attending the previous day, and they hoped Mrs. Crosfield would please the ladies at the stalls just as well. (Laughter). Long before Mr. Crosfield aspired to the great position he had attained he had the love of the Parish Church at heart, and a long friendship had existed between his family and the Rector's family. (Applause.) The vote was heartily passed, the RECTOR, in putting it, remarking that Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield had come down to Warrington at great sacrifice to themselves. Mr. A. H. CitosriELD, M.P., in responding, said he would not fail to take advantage of the hint dropped by his old friend the Rector. He could not be more politic than Canon Willis was in his references. (Laug4ter.) The political barometer was pointing, is some plsople said at the moment, to " stormy "—(langh- ter)--osoillat ng a little bit, it was true, and possibly going to point to" fair weather" a little later pn. He I really would not prophesy anything, but at such a time as this it was extremely pleisant to come down to the old town and say a few words at a perfectly friendly and peaceful gathering. (Applause.) None of them knew what was in store, it was possible there might be other gatherings later on where things were not so tranquil, as. they were that afternoon. (Laughter.) Whatever happened, he was perfectly certain of one thing—that neither the friendship between his old friend Mr. Pierpoint end himself, nor, he would venture to say, the friendship he had had the pleasure to claim equally with Conservatives and Liberals in Warrington—and which had never suffered in one single instance sinca he became actively associated with politics—(applause)—would be found to have altered when the contest was over. (Applause.) So far as the objects of that bazaar were concerned, they were most enterprising and he hoped that all resent would do what they could to make it a thorough success. (Applause.) - - _</p>
        <p>During the -afternoon Mrs. CroFfield gave a piano solo in hnr usual skilful style.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>ROLLER SKATING.</head>
        <p>ROLLER SKATING.</p>
        <p>PALATINE RINK.</p>
        <p>Anothr novel attraction was provided by the enterprising management of this rink on Thursday. The whist drive held in connection:withjthe Social and Hockey Club was a pronounced success so much so i that it s contemplated to hold them regularly 'during the winter session. The annexe was tastefully decorated for the occasion. The whist party, who numbered upwards of 100, when not engrossed aspiring to win premier honours at the game. were enabled to listen to the band and obtain a glimpse of the merry skaters who ntimbered quite 200. Whist over, they joined the skaters. The final for the egg and spoon race was then c-nnpeted for. Afterwards came the prize distribntion and then the grand finale which culminated with a grand march, the wheeling, advanc- ing and retizing, finishing with a long sinuous line of skaters forming figure eights and serpentine like course. The hour of closing came all too soon. Everyone left highly pleased, hoping to participate again in so pleasurable an evening. Prize winners :—Whist drive, highest score, ladies, Miss Barker; lowest, Miss C. Duckworth; highest gent, Mr. Shakeshaft ; lowest, Mr. A. Few. Balloon race, Miss B. Cotterill ; egg and spoon race, Miss Singleton. ' The coming events are as bllows :—Masked carnival in aid of the Infirmary Million Penny Fund Scheme. Mile Championship ; Lancashire and Cheshire Ladies' Hockey Match. Next Thursday at 8.40 there will be a Hockey Match, Palatine (champions of Warrington) v. St. Helens.</p>
        <p>History of the case of Mrs. Farley,</p>
        <p>17,</p>
        <p>Milton-st.,</p>
        <p>Southend.</p>
        <p>PATIENT'S FATHER Died of Kidney Disease at 32, PATIENT'S SISTER Died of Kidney Disease at 46, PATIENT HERSELF Seriously ill with Symtoms of her Father's and Sister's dread Disease.</p>
        <p>Cured by DOAN'S PILLS 2i. Years Ago—Well Ever Since.</p>
        <p>Mrs. E. Farley esides at 17, Milton-street, Southend-on-Sea, and is well known and highly respected. "My father died of advanced kidney disease at the age of thirty-two," she says, "and lost a sister forty-six years old with the same disease. "Naturally, therefore, I was greatly con- cerned when 1 noticed symptoms of the dread disease myself. I used to wake up in the mornings feeling very tired and heavy, and with a bad hack. The urine as obstructed, and my body began to swell with water. If I pressed my finger into the swollen flesh the impression would remain, just like pressing dough. My heart acted so strangely at times that I was positively alarmed. I was so nervous that the least sound caused palpitation. I began to grow weak and despondent, and every now and then I would have violent attacks of pain in the loins and back</p>
        <p>Mv back keeps all right. and I do not suffer from the pains in the loins. The dropsical swellinca have ell gone down, and the bladder acts freely and naturally. In short, there is positively 110 sign of the disease I inherited from . lay father." CURED 21 YEARS AGO—WELL EVER SINCE.--2i years later Mrs. Farley s.id : "Ever since my cure I have kept perfectly well. I have the hiehest opinion of Doan's Backache Kidney Pills." MANY MEN AND WOMEN inherit kidney and bladder disease—and they recognise the weak back, aching loins, palpitating heart, dropsical swellings, rheumatic twinges, urinary disorders, and gravel, as signs of their parents' terrible disease creeping on them. But inherited kidney and bladder troubles are curable, provided Doan's Backache Kidney Pills are commenced with as soon as the above unmistak- able symptoms appear. This medicine is especially for kidney and bladder disorders, and promotes a healthy action of the kidneys and bladder, so that all urinous poisons are flushed out of the system in the natural way. Of all chemists and stores, or direct from Foster- McClellan Co., 8, Wells-st., Oxford-st., London, W.. at 2s. 9d. per box, or l3s 9d. for six boxes. Be sure you get DOAN'S Backache Kidney Pills, like Mrs' Farley had.</p>
        <p>"My doctor told me I had advanced kidney disease. and in my anxiety to get well I tried so many kinds of medicine that I could not possibly remember them all. But I have never had anything to do me so much good aa Dosn's Backache Kidney pills, and thanks to them I am now enjoying better health than I have ever known before. I wake up in the mornings feeling bright and well, and all through the day I keep active and energetic—l never get tired out like I used to.</p>
