      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>EMPIRE RINK</head>
        <p>THE EMPIRE RINK</p>
        <p>This idereasingly popular rink presented a scene of gaiety and animation on Thursday evening when a highly successful gymkhana was held. There was a large attendance and the spectators watched the evolutions of the skaters wtth a c msiderable degree of interest. The contests were very keen and the results were as follows :—Mile amateur championship of Warrington and District. Heat 1, Messrs. C. Baxter; 2, W. Baxter; 3, Wadsworth and 4, Bowcock. (The final will take place on Tuesday). Three-legged race : Messrs. Walton and Madeley. Potato gather- ing race : Mr. Davies. Barrow race : Mr. Whittaker. Ladies' egg and spoon race : • Miss C. Ormsby. The three-legged race attracted particular attention and the result was a surprise. C. Davies and Whittaker led up to the last lap but they were overtaken by Messrs. Walton and Madeley who won by about two yards. The prizes were distributed by Mr. W. Chalmers, one of the directors.</p>
        <p>Licensing Transfers. At the Borough Police Couat on Monday Mr. A. Itrown made an application for the transfer of the licence of the Royal Oak Branch from Mr. James T. Bate to Mr. George Tryner, and for the transfer of the licence of the Victoria Vaults, Mersey-street, from Mrs. Elizabeth Whittaker, to Mr. Edward Handley.— Mr. Steel solicitor asked for the transfer of the Royal Arms from the Lite Mr. Charles Roddy to his wife Mrs. Anne Roddy. All three applications were granted. ,</p>
        <p>TURSDAY.—Before Messrs. H. Roberts, C. Jackson</p>
        <p>Homes and Edwin .Woods.</p>
        <p>A HUSBAND DESCRIBED AS A "'SLOW-MURDERER."— John Parkinson, 3, Slater-street, was summoned by his wife (Hannah Parkinson) of 117, Thelwall-lane, for desertion, whereby she asked for a separation.—Mr. T. S. Steel appeared for complainant and Mr. A. Browne for dafendant.—Mr. Steel said complainant asked for a separation order on the ground of her husband having deserted her. Complainant was married to defendant on the 12th of June last; be was a widower, she a widow with two children. On the morning of Wednes- day, the 13th of last month while the complainant was at the back of the house defendant wrote a note which be left on the table and went away. The note asked her to make her terms for a separation. Since the note was written defendant had not con- tributed anything towards his wife's keep though he had been in regular employment.— Mr. Brown submitted that there was no desertion. He said that defendant did return to the house but found the door locked.—ln ans% er to Mr. Browne complainant admitted having sent his box of clothes to the Town Hall where defendant was employed with a label attached bearing the following words 'John Parkinson, the slow-murderer,, Town Hall."—Mr. Browne: Did you not lock the door and go out.—Complainant went to Lymm.—Mr. Browne: Her husband name back again at night and found the door locked again.— The magistrates stopped the case saying there was no desertion and the case would be dismissed.</p>
        <p>OBSTRUCTING THE TRAMS.—CETARGE AGAINST A FARMER.—In the adjourned case against Robert Dakin, Orford, who was charged the previous Monday with obstructing the tramway in Manchester-road and furiously driving a pony cart.—Mr. Browne, who appeared for defendant, said the adjournment was asked fur that Dakin might be medically examined. He had now been examined by Dr. Joseph, and be asked the Bench to consider the doctor's report in private. He was prepared to give an undertaking that defendant should not again cause trouble again.—Having read Dr. Joseph's report,and accepted Mr. Browne's undertaking, the Bench agreed to the withdrawal of the charge on payment of costa.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Mr. C. White (chairman). Alder- man Smethurst, Messrs. J. W. Hayward and Dr. Joseph. DRUNK AND ASSAULT.—Joseph France, of no settled abode, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Manchester-road, also with assaulting P.C. Rylatt, while in the execution of Ma duty.—P.O. Rylatt stated he was on duty at 2.25 the previous afternoon in Manchester-road, when he saw the prisoner in com- pany with another man. He WAS very drunk and behaved in a disorderly manner. Witness was obliged to take him into custody. Prisoner deliberately gave him a blow in the face. With the assistance of a civilian they made their way to the Police Station.— Prisoner said he was as sober as a judge and the police- man struck him flrst.—He was sentenced to a month's hard labour.—Thomas Braney, of 16, Ship-yard, was fined 10s. and costs or 14 days for being drunk and dis- orderly in Bank-street the previous day. He was described as a "worthless loafer" by the Chief Constable.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Before Messrs. C. White (chairman), J. Rymer Young, W. Roberts, W. T. Williams, and</p>
        <p>D. Naden.</p>
        <p>A VIOLENT PRlSONER.—Elizabeth Sculley, a married -woman, living in Ship-yard, was charged with being drank and disorderly in Ship-yard the previous day, also with breaking four panes of glass in a lodging. house and doing damage to the amount of Is. 6d., and further with assaulting Sarah Kendal.—P.C. Burton said that late the previous night, he was on duty in Buttermarket-street, when a Mr. Hennessey, who kept a lodging-house in Ship-yard, informed him that a dis- turbance was taking place and that prisoner had broken four panes of glass in his house. Witness on arriving on the scene was told by Sarah Kendal that the prisoner had kicked her on the head and bitten her on her arm. Witness took prisoner into custody and she then became very violent. She threw herself to the floor and had that he almost dragged to the Police Station. She acted like a madwoman all the way. She mitts sentenced to two months imprisonment. A MONTH FOR BEGGlNG.—Patrick Carey, living on Pig-bill, was seutenced to one month's imprisonment for begging in Market-street, toe previous day. Prisoner was asking for money for beer. The Chief Constable said prisoner was a worthless character." OBSTRUCTION.—Joseph Nixon, a youth with no settled abode was charged with obstructing the free use of the footbpath at the Central Station when asked to desist by P.C. Berry. The Chief Constable stated that prisoner was an absolute nuisance. The case was adjourned for a week with a view to sending prisoner to a home.</p>
        <p>, 11E1 nthe g 1 ' , - OU MS Idt 0 ....,--------------- = V</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>Our btal</head>
        <p>Our btal</p>
        <p>rE</p>
        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society.</p>
        <p>There was a crowded attendance at an open meeting of this Society on Sunday. The great attraction was the presence of the William's Jubilee Singers. The expectation of a musical treat was fully realized, the harmony andi feeling, the blending of the voices being beautiful. Cordial applause followed each item. The Rev. J. Yonge gave a short address extending a hearty welcome to the Singers, who hid just arrived from America. He said he almost envied the Singers in being able to crowd that church. He wished them success. The Secretary led the prayer. The congregation joined in "Crown Him Lord of all," and " Blessed Avsurance," which was heartily sung. A good collection was taken. St. Peter's Church.</p>
        <p>The Bishop of Liverpool preached to a large congre- gation at St. Peter's Church on Sunday evening, taking for his text the words : "These all, having olitained good report through faith, received not the promise : God having provided some better thing for us, that they without us should not be made perfect," Heb. xi. 39 and 40. At the close of the sermon, the Bishop referred to the object of the offertory—the repair and renova- tion of the church, for which .C6O is to be raised. He was thankful to say they did their best to make God's house neat, comely and beautiful. It was a good sign when a congregation cared for their church. He had noticed again and again when coming; to St. Peter's that the congregation did care for the house of God.</p>
        <p>• " • _l. t 151;1 MMJU U 116</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>Rev. Thomas Miller on " Atheism."</head>
        <p>The Rev. Thomas Miller on " Atheism."</p>
        <p>The Rev. Thomas Miller preached to a large con- gregation on Sunday evening in Bold-street Church on "The hopelessbess of Atheism." He took as his text "Having no hope and without God in the world" (2 Ephesians, part of the xii. verse.) He said he would assume that the unbeliever was serious and that he had come after careful consideration to the conclusion that 'in this world there were no evidences sufficient to demand acceptance of a Supreme Being. He did not consider that the type of Atheism that they heard at street corners was really deserving of serious considera- tion. Was it possible that the denial of a Supreme Being could bring intellectual satisfaction? If there were no Supreme Being the alternative would be in one case the eternity of matter just as we had it and that was impossible, because it was subject to such constant change. There was no stability in the world. After deal- ' ing with the regularity of the seasons, laws, &amp;c., he said that they could agree with evolution in so far as it was understood that evolution did not render unnecescary the Christian revelation. Dr. Wallace. the co-discoverer with Darwin of evolution had said that a purely materialistic progress from the small beginning of man to what he was to-day was not possible of discovery by the reason that at certain stages there were great breaks—the wonder was that they got anything to work upon. Very few evolutionists to-day were atheists, and apart from Haeckel, modern science was not at all materialistic. The forces which were around us were so wonderful that they must be guided by a Supreme Being. When we came into the moral world the result of atheism was pessimism. There was no explanation for man's moral nature. Atheism ' never created a missionary or a church ; it merely tried to destroy. There was no gospel for the future life in Atheism. There was no messsge except it he the old message of Atheism. Concluding he said that they knew little about the beyond, but they knew they need not fear death through the grace of Jesus Christ.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>Wycliffe Congregational Church.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Congregational Church.</p>
        <p>The service at this church on Sunday evening was specially for young people and the Rev. J. Yonge based a powerful discourse on the words "That I may win Christ." God had given them life in this world that they might make a good, profitable and successful use of it. lie had given tbem their powers of mind and body and soul that they might devote them to the attain- ment and the exhibition of what was good. In these modern days, life's opportunities were vastly greater and finer than ever was the case in the past. Conse- quently the ambitions and aims of young people should be directed to even far greaterand better things (speak- ing of matters temporal) than the past ages made possible. Boys and girls in these days of a better and higher system of education were being trained to wider</p>
        <p>knowledge and higher attainment than ever had been the case before—speaking, of course, of popularmational education. The system of examinations and scholar- ships, and even of university education, which was now open to all ranks and conditions of life, all went to encourage and stimulate young people to win not only prizes or degrees. hut knowledge of all kinds that could inform the mind for deeper thought and larger acquire- ment, enabling and qualifying for advancement, attainment and success. One was glad to note from time to time the scholastic success which not a few of their Wycliffe young people were achieving. The preacher counselled his hearers to take those years of educational life seriously, earnestly and industriously, for if they did they would win positions in life and opportunities of success. They could never be won without the general and specific knowledge which could alone qualify them for them. He congratulated those who by steady and industrious work had already won scholastic or even University distinction, but he coun- selled them to go on to win yet more.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME.</p>
        <p>invoking the auxiliary assistance of earthquakes avalanches, or railway collisions, the author succeeds in holding his audience entranced by the narration of a plain, homely story, of which human interest is the keynote—the story of the love of two brotliers for the same woman. Though bearing a close analogy to the parable of the prodigal, the play differs both from it and from the novel of the same name, from which it is adapted, both in handling and ending. All the members of the company engaged by Mr. George D. Day are artistes of proved merit and acknowledged standing in their profession, and are</p>
        <p>"THE PRODIGAL SON."</p>
        <p>No stronger dramatic attraction could have been selected for the boards of the Court Theatre next week than Hall Caine's" Prodigal Son." The play was undoubtedly the greatest melodramatic success which Druty Lane has produced for many years, and it's triumph has been gained by its own intrinsic worth. Unlike so many of the Lane productions " The Prodigal Son "does not rely upon the adventitioms aid of mechanical spectacular sensations. Without</p>
        <p>MR. HALL CAINE REHEARSING MEMBERS OF MR. GEORGE D. DAY'S "THE PRODIGAL SON" CO. Mn. &amp;mow BALL, MISS ISLA GARNET VATNE, Mn. DERWENTHALL CAINE, Mn. HALL CAINE, "Magnus." " Thorn." " Oscar."</p>
        <p>deemed by Mr. Hall Caine, by whom they were personally rehearsed, to be the best all round cast which has ever gone into the provinces with the piece. Mr. Scott Leighton has been chosen for the important role of the Prodigal Son "Oscar," the part created by Mr. George Alexander. A well-known Shakespearean actor Mr. Elliott Ball sustains the character of "Magnus." Miss Isla Garnet Vayne appears in the duel role of" Thorn and Elin," while Miss May Beatrice plays " Helga " The scenery, costumes, and accessories are an exact copy of those used in the Drury-lane production.</p>
