      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Bewsey-road Foreign Missionary Anniversary.</head>
        <p>Bewsey-road Foreign Missionary Anniversary.</p>
        <p>The Foreign Missionary anniversary in connection with Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church was held last week- end, services being held Sunday morning and evening and Tuesday evening. In the morning the Rev. THOMAS GLADDING preached to a good congregation on the text "I am a debtor" (Rom. 1. 14). He said that we were all debtors to God and it was our duty Co clear off that debt, just as it was oar duty to clear off debt incurred in mundane affairs. One of our debts to be cleared off was that of mission work and it was our duty to do all we could for that cause.</p>
        <p>The Rev. R. B. KEELING preached in the evening on the text" Hallowed be Thy name"( Luke 11.2.)•He explained what hallowing the name of God was and said that it was simply the direct recognition of what God's character was and what Hi" rule was. The name to be hallowed was emphatically the name " Father" and God ought to be honoured, obeyed and trusted as a "Father." He then dwelt upon the phrase "Thy will be done on earth us it is in Heaven " and said it applied to them in their missionary work, as only when all the people of the earth were converted would God's will be done in earth.</p>
        <p>During the service the choir gave a splendid render- ing of the anthem, " Send out Tby Light." A fair number of people assembled in the church on Tuesday evening, when Mr. J. A. Smith presided. He was supported by the Revs. 11. Guard-Price 8.A., of Hyderabad and E. B. K eling. The Rev. H. Guard- Price who was the principal speaker is at present pn furlough from missionary work in Hyderabad and dis- trict, India, and therefore his address was doubly interesting, In the course of his speech he mentioned that his circuit contained about 40 villages and his headquarters were at Hyderabad. He was assisted in his %ork by 17 native evangelists and he gave samples offthe splendid work that they were doing and the high character they had attained.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>Warrington Parish Church. Dedication of Memorial Screen.</head>
        <p>Warrington Parish Church. Dedication of Memorial Screen.</p>
        <p>There was an impressive service at the Warrington Parish Church on Sunday afternoon (Eveof All Saints), when the Lnrd Bishop of Liverpool (Dr. Chavasse) unveiled the beautiful oak memorial screen which has been erected in memory of Miss Cicely d'Anyers Willie, daughter of the Rector and Mrs. Willis. The memorial has cost £6OO and it is a handsome and artistic piece of work, reflecting credit alike upon the design of Mr. Segni. Owen, architect, Warrington, and the workrnan- ship of Messrs. Hatch and Sons, of Lancaster.</p>
        <p>The scree , which is of solid 'oak, occupies a position immediately beneath the large seven-light window and fills the full space between the n6rth and south wing walls of the nave, forming with the west wall an entrance vestibule both to the nave and the aisle galleries. The general design consists of a solid panelled screen, in the centre of which are the folding doors leading to the nave. The middle side panels are glazed to give light to the gallery staircases. On the nave side and against this solid screen, eight stalls are placed—four on either side of the doors—each having a traceried canopy carried by a slender shaft from the stall head. This shaft is carried up above the main lino to the screen, finishing with a pinnacled head. The main line of each canopy is the same in general outline. but each differs in detail. The canopies are formed of three ogee arches richly carved, with detached flying pinnacles at each angle, and from these spring back the flying pierced panels on to an inner traceried canopy, which is carried up to the top cornice and cresting of the screen between each set of four stalls, and over- hanging the main doors, rises the central canopy, constructed on similar lines to the stalls. but larger and more elaborate in design. Formed with twa :quarter octagon bays, it rises to some five feet above the side screens, finishing as a pierced traceried hexagon, flanked on either side by a single pinuacled shaft. Beneath this central feature, and between the two angel figures, is the simple inscription, carved on the solid screen To THE GLORY OF GOD AND IN LOVING MEMORY OF CICELY D'ANYERS WILLIS, THE GIFT OF PARISHIONERS AND FRIENDS, ALL SAINTS' EVE, 1909.</p>
        <p>There was a very large congregation at the dedica- tion service. The galleries were set apart for the Sunday school children, and the body of the church was completely filled with purishioners and subscribers to the memorial. The clergy present, in addition to the Bishop, were Canon Willis, Canon Stevenson, Rev. F. Earle d'Anyers Willis, rector of Utfington. Stamford, Rev. W. Stanton, Rev. H. B. Firth, Rev. G. Vale Owen, and the Rev. T. A. J. Ridpath.—The service•opened with the hymn "We love the place, 0 God," after which shortened Evensong was gone through. The prayers were intoned by the Rev. T. A. J. Ridpath,,and the lesson was read by the Rev. F. Earle d' Anyers Willis, son of the Rector. The choir interpreted the anthem "Happy and blest are they " (Mendelesohn's " St. Paul ") with great expression. During the singing of the hymn "Jerusalem, my happy borne" the clergy and choir proceeded to the west end and here the dedication took place, the Bishop 'offering a number of special prayers. Then a return was made to the choir stalls, the hymn" Brief life is here our portion" being sung in procession.</p>
        <p>The Bishop preached from the text : " Jesus said unto him, Verily 1 say unto thee, To-day shalt thou be with Me in paradise" St. Luke, xxii., 43). Concluding an impressive sermon on the state of the departed in Paradise, the Bishop said that. while they dedicated that beautiful screen to the glory and praise of God in memory of his dear servant taken so suddenly from their midst they could look forward, and those who loved her could look for- ward, to the certainty of meeting her one day in Paradise and renewing there the love of her, only per- fectly and for ever. The service closed with the hymn " The Saints of God "and the Blessing pronounced by the Bishop. The collections at all the services on Sunday were in aid of the memorial fund and amounted to £4l 8s 6d, This completes the £6OO which the screen has cost but £3O is still outstanding for sundry expenses.</p>
        <p>CHURCH CLUBS BILLIARDS' LEAGUE. MATCHES FOR NOVEMBER 11TH. First Teams.—Widnes No. 1 v. Parish Church: St. Paul's v. St. Clement's ; St. George's v. Widnes No. 2. Second Clubs.—Parish Church v. Widnes No. 1; St. Clement's v. St. Paul's; Widnes No. 2 v. St. George's. On the tables of the first-named clubs. Commence at 7.30 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>PROPERTY OWNERS.</head>
        <p>PROPERTY OWNERS.</p>
        <p>THEIR VIEWS ON THE SLUM PROBLEM.</p>
        <p>Advice to Suffragettes.</p>
        <p>The slum problem was one of the subjects intro- duced at the annual meeting of the Warrington and District Property Owners' Assoeiation held i n Toft's Assembly Room Bridge-street, on Thursday evening. Mr. W. Hickman presided over a good attendance which included: Alderman Burton, Councillor Skelland, Messrs. F. Pendlebury, W. Throssell, R. Isherwood, R. Henshall, J. Lord, S. Gough, F. J. Owen, W. Buckley, R. Lunt, (bon. treasurer), and R. W. Cook (secretary).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>YEAR'S WORK.</head>
        <p>THE YEAR'S WORK.</p>
        <p>The Subscriptions received for the past 12 months amounted to £3O 12s: 6d., being an increase on the previous year, together with a balance brought forward of £27 4s. sd. from last year, making a total of £57 16.5. lid. The expenditure amounted to £2B 12s. 7d., leaving a balance in hand of £29 4s. 4d. Deputations attended Conferences at Manchester, Blackpool and Leeds, reports of which were duly-sent to each member .of the Association. The Association was legally repre- sented at the last audit of the Union Accounts, when the Government Auditor attended. The Nemo or Private Fund Account was objected to and ruled by him to be illegil. A letter had since been received from the Local Government board intimating that the Private Interest Fund had been closed. An important case arose as to the paving of the back passages abutting on the railway, namely Elaine-street and Hume-street. As the Railway Company objected to pave their prescribed half it necessitated the owners paving the full 20 feet passage; they objected to pave more than 10 feet, their legal share, and on behalf of the owners interested the Association obtained evi- dence which enabled the Corporation to legally contest the case, with the result that the Railway Company were compelled to pave their portion and the Corpora- tion were allowed 20 guineas cost. This saved the owners £450, and proved that the object of the Association was defence and a desire to work in harmony. In order that the Association should have perfect freedom of action it had been kept eatirely free from political basis or any introduction of party questions, It was pleasing to report a still further increase of members, making the total 286. The report was adopted on the proposition of Mr THROSSELL, seconded by Councillor SKELLAND.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>ELECTION OF PRESIDENT.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>Alderman BURTON said he did not see that they could find a better man for the presidency than Mr. W. Hickman. The way in which the business of the association had been conduoted reflected great credit upon him and it would be unfortunate for the associa- tion if they did not re-elect him. Alderman Burton concluded by moving the re-election of Mr. Hickman. Mr. HENSHALL seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. GOUGH said he thought the time bad come when they should have a change. •He did not suppose Mr. Hickman wished to remain in office continually. He moved that Mr. Robert Isherwood be elected president. Mr. HICK,MAN : I will withdraw my nomination if you will allow me. The CHAIRMAN: You cannot do that. On a vote being taken Mr. Hickman was re-elected by a large majority.</p>
        <p>THE PRESIDENT AND THE SLUM PROBLEM. A WORD TO THE SUFFRAGETTES. .</p>
        <p>Mr. HICKMAN, in returning thanks for his re-election, said be was thankful that the association had prospered during the year, and that prosperity was due to the harmonious feeling which had existed among the corn-1 mittee and officers. The year had been an important one for the society. In addition to being represented at several conferences they. had kept their eye on local events. They attended a Local Government Board enquiry on the question of paving streets and passages,l their opinion being that it would be to the advantage 1 of property owners if they could get the time extended for the repayment of the sums expended on street- making from seven to ten years. A number of their members were workingmen who by thrift and economy had acquired their own houses and perhaps others besides' land they felt very proud of having property. It was rather hard when the expense of paving fell npon them and they had to pay £2O or £3O or interest on that amount at the rate of 4 per cent. for seven years, and therafore the effort was made to get the time extended.' It was not, however, successful. The association also took action with regard to the private fund of the Board of Guardians. Thataccount had now been closed and the money_ handed over to the general fund of the Union. The Nemo Fund wis dead and buried and there was never going to be a resurrection. (Hear, hear.) The association claimed some credit in that matter, although they had not been altogether thanked in some quarters for the aztion they took, tiut as long as they were doing their duty they did not value the kicks or halfpence. He congratulated the association on its improved position both numerically and financially, the membership now beinglarger than ever it had been. Referring to criticisms which had been passed to the effect that two guineas was a paltry amount for an association worth thousands of pounds of property to send to the distress fund, Mr. Hipkman pointed out that the members had helped nobly as individuals, and the two guineas was simply a donation from the association as such. The President went on to allude to the renort presented by Alderman James Evans, chairman of the Health Committee, at the last meeting of the Town Council. That report dealt very lucidly with the slum question and the statistics which the Alderman gave were very striking. He (the speaker) went into the question somewhat fully two years ago and he had not seen any reason to vary his opinion. Alderman Evans had made out a very strong case for the property , owners and the town generally, that there must be something done by co-operative effort to remove the stigma from our town. He asked for the co-opera- tion of the owners of property in order that the con- rditions might be improved. When they considered that the infantile mortality in the slum areas was 53 per 1,000 and the birth-rate was only 54, it was some- thing appalling. He would like that Association to be a guarantee that every property-owner who was a member of it could be looked upon as being a good landlord who was death on slums. Nobody with any common sense would advocate retaining slums. It was no advantage to property-ownera to have slums; they were the most expensive and troublesome pieces of property. He could assure Alderman Evans that association would render him every assistance in his efforts to improve the conditions and reduce the infantile death-rate. ' The co-operation of three committees—the Paving and Sewerage. Sanitary and Health Committees—was also asked for, and if it were forthcoming he believed they would be able to do some very good work. He (the speaker) had inspected some of the slum neighbourhoods of the town and had visited the back yards and passages. At the beginning of the year some of the passages were not paved at all; they were a disgrace to a civilized town or even to a civilized country. Now they bai been paved and it was surprising what a wonderful improvement that had brought about in the habits of the people who lived there. He wished to give the Town Council every credit for good intentions and for the work they had done, and he was pleased the Paving and Se % erage Committee was pushing forward its share of the work. While the property o-.vners and the Corporation could</p>
        <p>work in harmony in the matter, that would not be sufficient without the co-operation of the tenants. They were told that an Englishman's home was his castle, but what condition were some of the castles in at the present time? The filth and the slatternliess of some of the people would, it had been remarked, turned Buckingham Palace into a slum if they were allowed to live there. There were in this as in every other town persons who would never live anywhere but in a slum. He trusted the report would be read seriously by the townspeople and that it would be realized that there was a duty devolving not only upon the property owners and the Council but upon every citizen to see that things were made better than they wera at the present time. There was not sufficient parental discipline in thoke days. On Monday it was pitiable to see the thousands of children who were out late at night on account of the elections. If children were to be about in the middle of the night rolling in the mud or playing in the dust, could be wondered at that there was disease ? Unless parents co-operated with the authorities there was sure to be disease and a high infantile mortality. There wss plenty of work for the suffragettes to do if they would only do it instead of shouting and howling and screeching "Votes for women." He could find them a better motto and battle cry than that. Let them cry " Help for tired and weary mothers who have large families " and they would appear more like women themselves if they would abandon their present cry and take up some useful work, by going amongst the poor and needy. How many hundreds of mothers had not even an eight hour's day? Let the suffragettes go among their poorer sisters, and take some of the little children and give them a good time out in the country or take them to their homes and see that they had a good feed. If the women he had referred to would endeavour to teach household duties to the poorer classes they would be doing more for the State and the town than by breaking windows, going to gaol and: clemming" themselves to death.</p>
        <p>Mr. GouGH -said it was only to test the feeling of the meeting, and to see whether there was any desire for Mr. Hickman to retire that he moved the amend- ment. The President had touched on questions of vital importance to property-owners, but both Mr. Hickman and Alderman Evans appeared to have over- looked the fact that other causes contributed toward, the death-rate besides slum property. He believed on, cause was the bad smells in the Longford dietric' They were enough to poison " Old Harry." (Laughter. He considered it was the duty of the Association t 4 lake the matter_ up.</p>
        <p>Mr. ROBERT IsfignwooD said the Corporation was not quite as clear as the President wanted to make out. A great deal was said at the last Council meeting about the high death-rate and slum property, but the fact appeared to be overlooked that the Corporation had a beautiful street—Gorsey-lane=which is in such a state that children could be almost drowned in the pools if men did not go and release them. That was note slum, but a main road to Orford from Padgate. The CHAIRMAN: I understand it is being repaired this week.</p>
        <p>Another MRNIBER : No, it is not. -Two or three horses were stuck there this week. Mr. ISHERWOOD, alluding to the slum question, emphasised the necessity of teaching people to leave drink alone,to become respectable and to keep their houses clean. As soon as they became sober and respectable and thinking, they would clear away the slums. He (Mr. Isherwood) did not like slums and he had also a decided objection to back-to-back houses. At one of the Federation Conferences the Leeds repre- sentatives bad a great deal to say in defence of back- to-back-housee and they said that two-thirds of their property consisted of such houses. They challenged Mr. John Burns but in the House of Commons on Tuesday that Minister condemned the 'back-to-back houses which they praised so much at Leeds. Mr. Burns had taken the advice of one of the leading experts—a doctor—which he said he would place against any other opinion. He (Mr. Isherwood) was prepared to help the authorities as much as he could but he said : '4 D 1 your duty. We pay you for doing it." Mr. PENDLEBURY said the report submitted to the Health Committee was really a reflection upon all property owners and it might be a reflection upon members of that association. He would like the com- mittee to consider the question and see if they could obtain the names of the owners of the property referred to. If any members of the association were involved he was sure they would do all they could to remove the stigma which rested upon all property owners. Alderman BURTON endorsed the President's remarks as to the necessity for co-operation if they were to stamp out the disease which caused the high death rate. The Health Committee was blamed to a large extent for the high death rate and for the nuisances which existed. It was, however, almost impossible for the committee to remove all the nuisances unless it had the help of all the other people who were interested— the property owners and the various committees of the Corporation. That co-operation was gradually being brought about and he was hopeful that before long there would be a better state of things. They must not overlook Jbe fact that already a great deal had been done and a large amount of insanitary property had been removed. He instanced the improvement' which bad been made on Town Hill. It would, he believed, be a good thing if the medical officer could report upon the death-rate in the slum districts every three months instead of only in his annual report. With regazd to Gorsey-lane he understood the improvement had been delayed owing to the committee being unable to come to terits with the owners of the land. He believed the defect would soon be remedied. The Health Committee also bad the Longford nuisance in hand and he felt sure it would be stopped before very long. The Property Owners' Association had done valuable work and had saved ths ratepayers a great deal of money. It bad also worked harmoniously with the Corporation, and if it continued to act in that spirit be had no doubt the Corporation would be prepared to carry out any reasonable sugges- tions. The association had been the means of prevent- ing a lot of useless expenditure. Looking ahead, they would see that the Corporation had many schemes in prospect. These included the widening of a number of streets, the erection of bridges, baths extension, and the provision of bowling greens and open spaces. lie was afraid there was something even more expensive that would have to be dealt with—the sewering of the town. How'rnuch money it was going to cost he did not know, but he felt rather nervous. That association would have more to do in the future than it had done in the past. It was very necessary to keep a watchful eye upon what was going on and to protest against any unwise expenditore. After all,the property owners had to provide a large amount of the money that was expended on public works. OFFICERS ELECTED.</p>
        <p>Mr. B. LUNT was warmly thanked for his services as hon. treasurer from the formation of the association and re-elected. General gratification was expressed that Mr. Lunt had been restored to health after a serious illness.</p>
        <p>The committee was elected as follows :—The President, hon. treasurer, Alderman Burton, Councillors Skelland and Dickenson, Messrs. W. Throssell, W. Caok, G. r. Diggle, W. Daintith, J. Hill, W. Buckley, R. Isherwood, H. Winstanley, R. Lee, A. B. hlarsh, G. T. Wilding, G. E. Mackie, A. W. Howard, J. Lord, F. Pendlebury, R. Henshall and .1. T. Barton. Messrs. E. Griffiths and J. H. Gittene were re-elected auditors. SHOULD THE ASSOCIATION RUN CANDIDATES? Mr. GOUGH moved a resolution to the effect that the association adopt the principle of selecting independent candidates to represent it on the Town Council and the Board of Guardians. He contended that such action would make the association more influential. Many of them believed that party politics were a curse to muni-</p>
        <p>cipal life, and therefore it was desirable in the interests of the association and of the town that they should adopt men to represent them on the Council, independent of politics. Mr. OwEx seconded and said it was necessary that steps should be taken to put down politics in the Council.</p>
        <p>Mr. LUNT moved an amendment that the proposal be not entertlined. Previous associations of that nature, he said, had frequently been made a stepping stone to the Council with the result that when the candidates had been returned they had either been tarred or they had found they had an axe to grind, and the societies had died. One of the greatest causes of the success of the present association was that it was non-political. Mr. GOUGH : It need not be political because it runs candidates.</p>
        <p>Mr. LUNT : You cannot run a candidate without entering into politics in 'Warrington at the present time. (Mr. Gough: "Question"). There was no ques- tion about it. If they ran a candidate in the name of the association, continued Mr. Lunt, they would be met with the statement "Oh he is a member of the rent raising association" and the candidate would find him- self at the bottom of the poll. Who wa.s going to pay the piper if the candidates were run? Mr. Goucir :_ That is a detail.</p>
        <p>Mr. LORD said that if the association decided to run candidates and selected the proper men, objections pould not bo raised against it. The matter wanted care- ful consideration and he suggested that it be referred to the committee.</p>
        <p>Mr. LUST: When you begin tint, the society begins to die.</p>
        <p>Alderman BURTON seconded the amendment. Hitherto the association had done excellent work and there had been no friction with regard to politics. He was afraid that if they once introduced the metho I of running candidates, they would do themselves more harm thaa good.</p>
        <p>- Mr. HENSHALL said thl,l ideally and logically there was nothing to be said agellast the proposal, but he was afraid that on practical grounds Mr. Lunt was right. If they rtn candidates they wanted money, and the moment they began to deal with candidates they would split. Mr. IstiEswooD objected to the proposition. He bad bad 'experience in these matters, and he knew that as soon as such candkdates got into the Council they had nothing to do with the Property Owners' Association. Mr. GOUGH said Mr. Lunt had raised a frivolous objection. On a vote being taken Mr. Lunt's amendment was carried by 13 votes to 3.</p>
        <p>WORKING WOMAN'S TRIBUTE. Suffered Acutely from Rheumatism. • Dr. Morse's Indian Root Pills prove Effective. Even when a woman is in good health the daily duties in the home, shop or factory are often difficult. to perform, but for a woman suffering from female trouble it is daily torment. There are thousands of such women struggling along in increasing misery. There are thousands who have found a complete cure in the use of Mn. MORSE'S INDIAN ROOT Plus. A case i • ' • MRS. ANNE PAGE of 24, Saville-street, Wellington-street, Leeds, who writes :—" So fully convinced am , I of the good that DR. MORSE* / INDIAN ROOT PILLS have done me, that I unhesitatingly write W proffer my thanks to you. For a. good while my system was in a. state of nervous depression, and completely run down, which made me susceptible to rheumatism, and from which I suffered severe attacks. Naturally, having to work hard for my living, it caused me the greatest anxiety in addition to the acute pains which I endured. I have taken several bottles of Dn. MonsF.'v INDIAN ROOT PILLS, and can with truth say that they have made a decided change in my condition, for which I am thankful. Not being able to see you personally 1; am sending photo along with this, which you may use.' DR. MORSE'R INDIAN ROOT PILLS cleanse the system healing inflammation and ulceration, nourishing the nerves, giving vitality and vigour. They make weak woman strong and sick women well. They are a positive and permanent cure for Constipatioo and Biliousness, Sick Headaches, Impure Blood, all disorders arising from deranged Liver and Kidney and Female Irregularities. Sold by Chemists price Is. 1-Id. per bottle, of THE W. If. COMSTOCK CO., Ltd. 21, Farringdon-avenue, London E.C.,</p>
