      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Alb</head>
        <p>Alb</p>
        <p>CROSS COUNTRY RUNNING.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON A.C.—Owing to the unavoidable absence of the Captain and Sub-captain last week's teans race was postponed and there was only a moderate muster for an ordinary run over about seven miles. R. Whittaker set a strong pace over the first two miles when J. Parsonage took the lead followed by A. Siddall and F. Antrobus and this order was maintained to the finish. W. Siddall, F. Mounfleld, All. Antrobus, G. Nicholson and W. Drury were most prominent of the second division. T. Litherland and J. H. Hands showed improved form. The pack included two visitors' one from the Olympic Harriers, Glasgow and one from Broughton Harriers. All thoroughly enjoyed the run. The fixture for to-day is a paper chase, the cycling section to carry the paper in relays, the last unit of the cgclists to start with the fast pack seven minutes b4hind the advance division who leave the Bridge Inn at 3.15 p.m. Visitors cordially invited. WARRINGTON UNITED HARRIERS.</p>
        <p>Thirty-four members of the above club turned out from their headquarters, the Black Horse Inn, Sankey Bridges. on Saturday last. The runners lined up in Hood-lane and a brisk race was run over about seven miles of country round Buttonwood and Bold way. The first man to arrive home was W. Bolton who seems to be running better than ever, followed closely by the Captain, A. Wilcocks who is coming hick to form. W. Stewart a nromising young runner was third and Bradbury fourth. With the novices coming on,the club should be as strong as ever.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>SOME COSTLY MISTAKES.</head>
        <p>SOME COSTLY MISTAKES.</p>
        <p>Every day people make serious mistakes which the, experience of others should have taught them to avoid. For instance, there is the fool; with the gun that he 'didn't know was loaded" until it went off, and hurt - somebody. There is the person who goes to find an escape of gas with a naked light, and .so causes an explosion. Again, there is—perhaps in your, house- hold—somebody who makes the everyday mis- take of. thinking that they can be well even though they cannot digest their food. Now, health and strength can be obtained only from food well digested, and the first approach of Indiges- tion is a serious matter. Neuralgia, anannia, and rheumatism result from Indigestion as surely as pains in the chest, head or back ! Such troubles arise from a disordered stomach, and that condition you can remedy with the aid of the root and herb extract, ' Mother Seigel's Syrup, which tones and strengthens he_stoinach and stimulates the liver and bowels.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Bland, 20, Park Place, Darling ton, says : "About ten years ago I was attacked by neuralgia. Day and night I suffered cruel torture. The usual ' remedies ' had no good effect. A friend pointed out that, as I had Indigestion and was in 'a weak, run- down condition, I could not hope for relief until I improved my general health by taking Mother Seigel's Syrup." ' Indigestion was stealing away Mrs. Bla nd's strength and vitality. Her food was not nourishing her, and so her starved nerves were iryiny out. Mat ie neuralgia. "I bought a bottle of Mother Seigel's Syrup," adds Mrs. Bland, "and began to take it. Not only did the neuralgic pains leave me, but in a week I was stronger than I had been for a long time. From that day I have been free from my troubles, and I don't think there are many women of my age, and who work as hard as I do, who enjoy better health." If you have stomach or liver troubles, if you have "nerves," or neuralgia, Mother Seigel's. Syrup will prove as good a friend a friend to you as to Mrs. Bland. The 2s. (id. bottle contains 3 times as much. as the is. 11d. size.</p>
      </div>
