      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>THE</head>
        <p>THE</p>
        <p>WELSH PIT DISASTER.</p>
        <p>SCENE ( F THE EXPLOSION.</p>
        <p>The fatal explosion at the rarran Pit, Glamorganshire, by which twenty-ix men lost their lives, was marked by the magnificent heroism of the rescuers, five of whom succumbcd in their gallant efforts to save their comrades. The men were unable to live in the foul air of the after-damp, and the work of rescue was consequently carried out under difficulties of the most appalling kind. Mr. Bowen, the general manager of the collieries, who led the rescue party, lost his life in the desperate effort to reach the entembcd miners, and Dr. Turner, the miners' surgeon, who descended to render assistance, bad a providential escape from death. He was found lying unconscious on one of the roadways, and was brought back to life by. the application of oxygen. According to the mining experts the after-damp was the most deadly ever known in a Welsh coalfield.</p>
