      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>– , Aims , , -_-:</head>
        <p>- , Aims , , -_-:</p>
        <p>I,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>PICTURES OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>PICTURES OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>FWMINGTON CHAPTERS. &gt; GLIMPSES OF BYGONE STOCKTON</p>
        <p>HEATH.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath is at once the oldest .and newest of places. In one aspect it is a ,scrap of modern villadom, yet it wears a name that belongs to the dawn of English history. First a Heath that could not show a single dwelling; then a stockaded or fortified Heath of Roman days • sucb is the history of the name. Let us first take a glimpse of the Heath before it had acquired the adjective. If the observer could have climbed the slopes of Hill Cliffe in the early years of the Christian era and peered through the thick foliage of the trees he would have surveyed a wild heather-covered expanse spreading from Lumb Brook to Lower Walton and sloping from his feet to Wilderspool and the banks of the river. _ _</p>
        <p>. _ Later a grey line crossed the wild tract and pack horses descended the slopes of the hill to call at a village at the foot. The grey line was a Roman road and the village was the Roman. _ _</p>
        <p>But the coming of the Romans did not disturb the Heath. It remained unenclosed and common land until a century ago and even then a race course was reserved as the scene of the annual horse race, and a convenient space for the baiting of bears. The history of many villagers is the history of the country in miniature and this is true in large measure of Stockton Heath. The past, moreover, stands in such striking contrast to the present that we feel sure there will be interest in every glimpse our records afford us. EIGHT CENTURIES AGO. _</p>
        <p>We lately turned the pages of that venerable record made by command of William the Conquer and diciphered the brief description made by his surveyors eight and a quarter centuries ago. They say " The same Osbern holds Epletune. (Appleton). Dot held it and was a free man. There is one hide rateable to the gelt. The land is four carncates. It was and is waste. There is a wood there half a league long and forty perches broad." Another paragraph concerning Appleton says "In Edwards time it was worth sixteen shillings." That is 'to say in the days of Edward the Confessor it was worth an annual rent of sixteen shillings, but after the turmoil of the Conquest was regarded as having no value at all. The " waste " land was doubtless the Heath, and the wood the same which still crowns the summit of Hill Cliff. Dot was the Saxon free holder from whom it appears to have been wrested by the Norman invaders. The Osbern was Osbern Fitz Tezzon and he held a big slice of country there including Gropenhale and Hanley. It remained for two generations in the hands of the Osberns. The grandson of Osbern Fitz Tezzon who took the name of Boydel parted with Stockton Heath to Richard de Aston. It was when this owner granted the land to Adam de Dutton that we get our next glimpse of the district. The Charter is so interesting that we quote the descriptive part of it leaving our readers to identity the places it names.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>AN ANCIENT LAND CONVEYANCE.</head>
        <p>AN ANCIENT LAND CONVEYANCE.</p>
        <p>"Know all men present and to come, that I, Richard de Aston, have given and granted, and by this my present Charter have con- firmed to Adam de Dutton, all that my land of Stoketon, according as the great way goes from Walton Mill to Gropenhale, as far as to Lgmbroc and as Lumbroc descends into the Mersey,and as the boundary of Walton divides Walton and Stoketon fields by a little rivulet which descends from the aforesaid great way as far as the Merse. And on another part of the way as the great way comes from Werington by Wildrespul towards Sale as far as the rock between the footpath which goes from Little Waleton to Apeltun. And all tbis before mentioned land I have given to the aforesaid Adam and his heirs, for his homage and services to have and to hold of me and my heirs freely, absolutely, quietly, and honourably, with all the appurtenances in wood, in plain, in ways, in waters, and in pastures, and in all commons belonging to my said viii of Apeltun. Rendering to me and my heirs for every 'secular service and exaction twelve pence yearly at the feast of Saint Bertoline."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>AN ECHO OF THE CRUSADES.</head>
        <p>AN ECHO OF THE CRUSADES.</p>
        <p>The son of Adam de button was Geoffrey de Dutton, and he has left his mark on the village in way he could never have expected. Like many others of his day he became a Crusader and went to help to drive the Turk from the Holy Land. He returned safely after many battles and died at his home in 1248. To signalise his successes in the East the family took as its crest a Saracen's Head. The head of Saracen in Holy Land Sir Geoffrey won it with his own right hand.</p>
        <p>Possibly Geoffrey made himserf famous as a decapidator of the Turks; at any rate the head became the crest and it lingers with us still in the familiar tavern sign.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>MULBERRY TREE.</head>
        <p>THE MULBERRY TREE.</p>
        <p>Another tavern also enshrines in its name a bit of history. Just three hundred years ago, in 1609, King James I. became possessed of the idea of introducing silkworms into England. It was his purpose to create a new industry and if possible make silk an extensive manufacture. He therefore sent out letters to the Lord-Lieutenants of the various counties, asking them to promote the planting of mulberry trees as extensively as possible, the trees being of course for the feeding of the silkworms. In obedience to the command trees were planted at Stockton Heath, and the inn either commemorates the event or took its name from the grove of trees. It is needless to say that King James' worthy effort ended in failure. The climate did not favour the mulberry and we continue to import the raw material from which our silk goods are made. Eight years after the order had been issued James passed across Stockton Heath and would see dm grove of trees planted in loyalty to his wishes. Whether the inn had arisen we have no means of ascertaining: probably it had, for in 1636 when the gifted Richard Braithwaite (better known as Drunken Barnaby) passed through he names the Mulberry Tree as a place of call. We should imagine that Barnaby's list of inns is pretty complete for he seldom seems to have passed one by without making its affectionate acquaintance. On the occasion of his journey in 1636 he arrived in Warrington from the north. A flood had made the roads impassable and this roistering genius spent the days. in sampling the ales of Warrington's taverns— a very willing prisoner as he gives us to understand.</p>
        <p>Came to Warrington, banks o'erflowed, Travellers to the town much rowed ; , But supposing it were better To be drowned on land than water, Sweetly, neatly, I sojpuined Till that deluge thence returned. His record mentions besides the Mulberry</p>
        <p>Tree, the Malt Shovels at Hill Cliff, the Cat and Lion and the Bottle and Birch.</p>
        <p>THE CIVIL WAR.</p>
        <p>When the great storm cloud of Civil War burst over England Stockton Heath consisted of a number of thatched huts that clustered round the Mulberry Tree. It had no strength as a village and few natural advan- tages, but circumstances made it the scene of conflict and bloodshed.</p>
        <p>Warrington was held by Earl Derby for the King. Nantwich and Manchester were held for the Parliament, and at the end of March, 1643, Stockton Heath was decided upon as the meeting place of two forces that were to gather there and make an attack updh Warrington. Sir William Brereton was to lead the attack, but before he arrived Captain Arden, of Alvariley, appeared with troops and proceeded to throw up entrench merits near the Mulberry Tree garden to defend the village. Becoming restless and perhaps anxious for farrie he led his small force down to Warring- ton to make an attack. The Earl of Derby perceiving their weakness sallied forth to meet them, and soon put them in full retreat. There would have been a complete rout but for the arrival of Sir 'William Breretcin from Manchester.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>BATTLE OF STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>THE BATTLE OF STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>It was evening and Earl Derby had followed this attackers as far as the village when he found himself confronted by trained troops under a trained and able leader. _ _</p>
        <p>Tfie Earl knew the district and the new arrivals did not. By a ruse he was able to make a flank attack, and, Sir William Brere- ton was doubtless grateful when the failing light made further fighting impossible. April 3rd, 1643, was the date of the battle of Stockton Heath, and the sunset of that Easter Monday saw the heath dotted with tfi-e"lrdead and dying; and its torn heather stained with blood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>CROMWELLIAN VICAR.</head>
        <p>A CROMWELLIAN VICAR.</p>
        <p>The vicar of the parish, John Ley of Bud- worth—for Stockton Heath was in Bud- worth Parish—was an ardent supporter of Cromwell and he had stirred his parishioners mightily in the cause of Parliament. They marched down, many of them rustics unskilled in the use of arms, and followed Captain Arden in his ill-considered attack. The following Thursday the Vicar read the funeral service over six of his flock whose bodies had been gathered from the field of battle. A year later this zealous man preached before the House of Commons and chose as his subject " The Fury of Warre and the Folly of Sinne." Five years later the villagers of Stockton Heath saw the last episode in the first stage of the Civil War. Duke Hamilton routed on the Lancashire side of the river fled across the., bridge and followed by many of the nobles of Scotland made his way to Delamere Forest.</p>
        <p>Three years more and the village is again stirred to the point of excitement. The second Charles had won the heart of Scot- land and with a force as great as could be rallied was making his way south in the hope of winning the crown of England. He had spent the night previous at Bryn. Now, after a skirmish at Warrington Bridge he passed through Appleton and Stretton and pitched his tent at night in a field in Higher Whitley and from thence wrote a long letter, still preserved, which was dated "From our Royal camp at Higher Whitley," But the writer of the epistle had yet to. meet Cromwell and the adjective " Royal " was not needed for many a long year. • A. M.</p>
        <p>(To be continued).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>ILLNESS OF *JUDGE SUIRESS WILLS.</head>
        <p>ILLNESS OF *JUDGE SUIRESS WILLS.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday morning Judge Shiress Wills, R.C.' took his seat on the bench at the Birkenhead County Court for the first time since his prolonged illness, Mr. Cuthbert Smith, on behalf of the members of this legal profession, congratulated him upon his recovery. How- ever, the felicitations proved a little premature, for, after sitting for about two hours, his Honour was sub- ject to another attack of a distressing cough, and it became necessary to adjourn the court. The luncheon interval was taken at this point, and extended to over three-quarter of an hour. The judge returned after the interval, finished the case upon which he was then engaged, and adjourned the others until Wednesday next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>HOCKEY.</head>
        <p>HOCKEY.</p>
        <p>l'ilE LATCHFORD CLUB.</p>
        <p>Lafebford defeated Crewe Permanent Way at Crowe by 3-2' in a very close game. In the first half Crewe played well and aided by a rather lucky goal at the interval 2-0, but afterwards the visitors showed up to much greater advantage good defence by the backs and clever combination between the halves and forwards resulting in three goals being scored all of which came from Parker's stick. The Latchford half-backs wore very much in evidence and backed up the forwards well. The final score may be taken as a true reflex of the play. Thol Second" Elevens met at Latchford, and here the home team won 3-2 after a ding-dong game. The start was somewhat sensational as Crewe ran straight up the field and scored, but Latchford gradually pulled the game round and led at half-time 2-1. The visitors strove hard in the second half for an equaliser, and good passing by their forwards enabled their left-iuside to do the trick. Shortly afterwards, however, one of tbe home forwards after a splendid MI put Latchford in fro9t. Dixon, Eccles, an Knight played well in defence</p>
        <p>The First Eleven against Manchester Y.M.C.A. at hone to-day is :—J. Appleton, goal ; C. Plinston, W. 11. Pickles, backs; F. Tole, J. B. Wilkinson, J. Albinson, halves; R. A. Dale, IL Booth, 11. Parker, E. Banks, C. Hackney, forwards. Referee, Mr. Cobb. Bully-off 2.45 prompt. The Second Elevens meet at Alexandra Park, Manchester. The Latchford team is :—A. Hill goal; J. Wilkinson, T. Dixon, backs; W. Isaac, A. Knight, G. Unswort.b, halves; J. Welding, P. Allanson: A. Starkey, W. T. Eceles, J. Pearson, forwards. Referee, Mr. Warburton. Train from Central 1.50.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>DISFIGURING ERUPTIONS.</head>
        <p>DISFIGURING ERUPTIONS.</p>
        <p>"My daughter, aged six years, had been suffering from running sores in various partsof the body and we had tried innumerable ointments and treatments from chemists but to no avail. She got into such a deplorable condition that we took her to-- Hospital also the --- Hospital but she got worse instead of better. The sores broke out around her ears and underher nose, quite.disfiguring the child. We had to keep her away from school and had all her hair cut short. "Happily, we saw about Cuticura and tried it although we had lost all hope that she would ever be cured of the ailment. The effect was really wonderful. In a month she was completely cured and to-day her shin is as clear as you can possibly wish it to be. Not only have the wounds been healed but not a scar remains. People continually ask what cured her. We and they know how disfigured and dreadful she was to look at. In fact at one time we almost thought her ears would drop off, and now she has a beautiful skin. We only used one box of the Cuticura Ointment. All thanks is due to Cuticura. Mrs. E. A. Hobart, 71, Victor Road, Kensal Rise, London, N.W., May 22 and 28, 1909."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>NEGLECTED LAWNS.</head>
        <p>NEGLECTED LAWNS.</p>
        <p>A \\liter in" Gardening Illustrated" says : I had two side lawns as bad as bad could be—simply a miss of the usual weeds, daisies plantains, dandelions, etc., with tree-roots underneath'. I had the whole surface cut as if for turf, about three inches thick, and turned over where it lay, then beaten over to level it, and left. it for the winter. In the early spring about two inches of good compost., composed of loam, burnt garden refuse sifted, and dscayed hot-bed material, sifted and, well mixed together, were spread on the surface. The ground was then well rolled, and sown with the best fine grass seeds, very thickly. The result is that now, at the end of the second summer, both lawns present a most excellent appearance; not one weed came through, and there is not a daisy or a plantain to be found.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</head>
        <p>FROM LABOUR'S STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>By Philip Snowden, M.P.</p>
        <p>A STRIKING COMPLIMENT.</p>
        <p>Imitation, it is said, is the sincerest form of flbAtery. The Labour Party ought,there- fore to be highly flattered by the decision of the Anti-Socialist Union to raise a fund of £50,000 to finance genuine working class candidates on the, anti-socialist and tariff reform ticket. I do not suppose there will be the slightest difficulty in raising the money for such a purpose, nor will there be any dearth of genuine working men who are ready to come forward as candidates on these terms.</p>
        <p>Already two or three tariff reform lecturers have been adopted as Parlia- mentary candidates by the Conservative party, and when the funds come in more will be put in the field. I do not want to make fun of this latest device to capture working men's votes for the capitalists, though it is a subject more suitable for humorous than serious treatment. As a, matter of fact I do not regard it with indiffer- ence, for I see considerable danger in it. I think it is a cute move, and one which is likely to turn out to the political advantage of the Tory party.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>NECESSARY SAFEGUARD.</head>
        <p>A NECESSARY SAFEGUARD.</p>
        <p>The glamour of a lord or of wealth has not that fascination for even a Tory working man which it once had. The democratic spirit of the age has touched the working men of all parties and they have developed a class consciousness and a clasg respect while still retaining their fidelity to the party of dukes and plutocrats. I see no reason why a smart workman should not make a popular Tory candidate, and so long as the, party officials take care that such candidates are used merely as decoy ducks and have no authority in the councils of the party no harm can come to the party. A PATHETIC SIGHT.</p>
        <p>But a working man standing as anti- labour candidate is a pathetic sight. If there had been such in the present Parliament we should have seen them opposing the Trades Disputes Bill, the Workmen's Com- pensation Bill, the Feeding of School Children Bill, the Old Age Pensions Bill, the Licensing Bill, and this session the Budget proposals which seek to get a little of the cost of Dreadnoughts and Pensions from the abund- antly wealthy. If we get these Tory working men into Parliament we shall have them asking in the name of the poor that the taxes should be taken off the dukes and other landowners and put upon the food of the people. We shall have them advocating that the cost of living should be increased as a means of finding work for the unemployed, which is really what tariff reform would do. All this is very tragic ; but it was ever so. The oppressors of the poor have ever been of their own class and household, and it seems that the lesson of all history is still to be learnt by great masses of the working people. THE BERMONDSEY RESULT.</p>
        <p>The result of the Bermondsey election has given satisfaction to no party; but the Labour party, it must be frankly confessed, have the least cause for jubilation over the result. Experienced electioneerers foresaw such a vote for some days before the polling. In the early days of the contest everything seemed to promise a great • Labour victory. But the tide of opinion turned, and it was evident for days before the voting that the Labour candidate would be a bad third. If one might speak plainly one would say that the way in which the Labour campaign was conducted invited such a result. Idealism is alright in its own time and place, but an election is a very practical thing, and the man who comes clown from the clouds and deals with the living issues of the hour will gain the support of a practical people, and the practical people after all are the people who decide elections. IDEALISM AND PRACTICE.</p>
        <p>The fight was really on the Budget, and a Socialist candidate who can apply his socialist principles to practical politics ought to come out on the top every time in a con- test on such an issue as the Budget. He can give as much as the Liberal candidate and he can promise a great deal more. But the promises are likely to do him far more harm than good unless he can show his ideas to he practical and capable or application now. In a sustained contest I have never yet seen mere enthusiasm for ideals overcome the force of simple and practical ideas. Idealism is one. or the best assets a political party can have, and I would not for the world have the enthusiasm and idealism of the Labour and Socialist movement weakened or dimmed; but I do want to see the men and women of our cause applying their brains a little more to reducing their theories to practice and applying their principles to the hard work of constructive statesmanship. If some Socialists would shout less and think more the movement would be all the better for the change.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>ALL SORTS AND CONDITIONS.</head>
        <p>ALL SORTS AND CONDITIONS.</p>
        <p>lam giving that bit of candid advice in the best spirit of comradeship, and I hope it will not be taken as having more than a limited application. There is no political party in the country which can compare for a moment with the Labour and Socialist ' party in the matter of the knowledge of political and social questions possessed by its rank and file. But like all movements this movement attracts all sdrts of people and for all sorts of reasons. We have the purely emotional type who are swayed hy sentiment and appeals to their hearts ; we have the hard, envious _type to whom the movement appeals as to one to gratify their spite because by birth or accident they happen to be poor ; we have the intellectual type to whom the logic and symmetry of socialist theory appeals; and we have, and they are the over-whelming majority, the ordinary man and woman who have both heads and hearts of the ordinary sort and who are touched by the misery around them and who have carefully thought ont the socialist theory and are convinced of its jpstice and practicability. Socialism and the Labour movement will succeed just in so far as it can harness sympathy to reason, and it does not deserve to succeed only in so far as it can convince the head that the impulses of the heart are right.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>ELECTION OMENS.</head>
        <p>THE ELECTION OMENS.</p>
        <p>The omens point to a General Election in January whatever happens to the Budget in that "other place" to which it has been translated. -Every party is making prepara- tions for an election at that time. If the Bermondsey election had given the Tory candidate a majority over both candidates it is a moral certainty the Lords would have thrown out the Budget. The Tories in the House of Commons were disappointed with the result; they expected a larger majority.</p>
        <p>.• . • -</p>
        <p>What will happen now nobody appears to know, but the general impression seems to be that the Lords will reject it. I express no opinion, for to do so would be mere guess work. I think the January election may be taken as a settled thing in any case. I think it would be much better for the chances of the Budget supporters and the anti-House of Lords party if the elections were to come while the fate of the Budget was still unsettled. There is a real danger that if the Lords pass the Budget and the election comes after, with nothing but an abstract issue at stake and the country in a state of collapse after the excitement of the last six months, that indifference will largely prevail.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>LABOUR OUTLOOK.</head>
        <p>THE LABOUR OUTLOOK.</p>
        <p>I bear very excellent accounts from all the constituencies in which the Labour members are seeking re-election. The meetings which are being addressed by the M.P.'s who are now carrying out the winter tour are very fine, and the organisation is much better in most cases than it was at the last election. The forecasts which r see some Labour members are making 9f the strength of the Labour party in the next Parliament seem quite justified by all the available material for making a prophecy. If the work of the Labour Party in Parliament during the last four sessions does not receive the gratitude of the working class- electorate in the form of renewed confidence there is less gratitud e in politics than is even popularly supposed. The Labour Party has really only been serving its apprenticeship in this Parlia- ment, and it has gained a knowledge and experience which it would be a criminal waste for the working classes to throw upon the scrap heap. But there is little prospect of that. There will be gratitude enough and self-interest enough at the coming election to send the old members back with many new colleagues.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The Need for Land Reform.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Rural District Council was held on Wednesday afternoon at Bewsey Chambers. Mr. R. Forshaw presided and those present were :—Dr. Murray, Messrs. A. Brooks, W. Furness,.G. Mather, J. C. Sutton (clerk). B.S.Newcombe (surveyor), H. 11. Burnage (assistant surveyor), F. T. Kerfout (inspector) and Dr. Sephton (medical officer),</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>SPITTAL BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>SPITTAL BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>Mr. BROOKS moved that the Seal of the Council and the signature of the Chairman be attached to the agreement with Mr. Houghton for the purchase of the land for the improvement of Spittal Bridge. He con- sidered that it was an exorbitant price to pay for it. If this Council and other councils were going to be srpnezed as they had been all along they would have to have the land laws altered; He did not blame Mr. Houghton as he was only doing what nine out of ten I.ndlords did.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN: I would not say squeezed. Mr. BROOKS: I do say squeezed. As Soon as we got the Valuation Bill through he will object to his land being valued at that price. Mr. FURNESS' seconded.</p>
        <p>Mr. MATHER said that Mr. Brooks had said that 9 out, of every 10 landlords would do the same as Mr. [fought on. Almost the whole of the landlords in his township and district for the last 20 or 30 years had given land for public improvements and they had had the same powers as Mr. Houghton. Mr. BROOKS maintained that it was an outrageous price. Mr. ISTATHER said he agreed with him. Dr. MURRAY said, he was still of opinion that the county ought to buy the land. Ile thought the expense ought not to fall on the districts. \ The CLERK said the County Council said that if they wanted new land they must pay for it and they (the County Council) would do the rest. Although he thought that the whole of the cost ought tq fall upon the County Council they bad no need to complain as other districts bad to do the same. The resolution was carried. LIGHTING AT' SANKEY.</p>
        <p>The CLERK said that Mr. Joseph Smith, clerk of the Great Sankey Parish Council, had been to see him with reference to the lighting of flightleld-avenue and lluntley-street, but unfortunately ho was away when Mr. Smith called.</p>
        <p>The question of lighting was referred to the General Purposes Committee.</p>
        <p>CHANGE IN NAMES OF ROADS.</p>
        <p>It va.3 decided that the name of "Cemetery-road," Penketh, be changed to "St. Afary's•road." Mr. FURNESS moved that tablets be , fixed on the following roads and lanes: Long-lane and Fairclough's- lane to be named • "Sycamore-lana " ; the lane near Linglcy Green, "Lingley-road " ; "Thirrow Hall-lane" and "Station-road"; all in Sankey. Victoria,road and. Cinnamon-lane, Fearnheid were to be marked "Cinnamon-lane."</p>
        <p>This was agreed to. PROPOSED ROAD IMPROVEMENT AT RIXTON.</p>
        <p>Upon the motion of the CHAIRMAN of the General Purposes Committee the Clerk was instructed to write to the Vicar of llollinfare with a copy of the report by the Surveyor upon desirable read improve- ments at Rixton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>KEEP PEPS ALWAYS HANDY.</head>
        <p>KEEP PEPS ALWAYS HANDY.</p>
        <p>A Boon For all Classes &amp; All Ages.</p>
        <p>You cannot treat a cold too scion, and experience proves that tke Peps way is the perfectly natural, safe, and certain. way of curing and brushing aside all complications.</p>
        <p>Don't go out in bad weather without a few silver- jacketed Peps in your pocket; and so have them handy when an attack of sneezing gives warning of a fresh cold. Peps will ward off the threatened attack, and are a true preventive of pleurisy and pneumonia. Don't start on a train journey or ride on the top of a 'bus without a box of Peps. The risk of chill through the cold wind or dampness, or contact with people who may be suffering from, highly infectious chest and lung .complaints, is very dangerous when the ever-present draughts in a train make your chest and lungs particularly susceptible. Don't forget that Peps will stop that tickling cough, by ridding you of the cause of the irritation, and will save you from a severe attack of sore throat or bronchitis.</p>
        <p>Don't forget to keep Peps at your bedside, and the troublesome cough which keeps you awake at nights will be promptly got rid of. The significant morning cough, which so often speaks of deep-seated lung trouble, is best treated with the pure volatile essences in Peps. Don't fail to.keep Peps always-bandy in the nursery or children's room. These wonderful little tablets will save mothers many an anxious hour by bringing ease and rest to little sufferers from colds, whooping cougli, or croup. Don't fail to have Peps handy when at work, for nothing is more annoying than a persistent cough in business hours. In office, factory, or workshop a few handy Peps tablets will prove the best safeguard against sore throat and the never-absent dangers of dust and germ infection. Don't forget that the air of every public meeting place is poisoned by the microbes of many a deadly throat and lung disorder. So whenever you go to concert, theatre, ball, or public meeting take a box of Peps with you. They will ward off chill and rid you of the evil results of breathing foul air. Remember that in a crowded room, the air you breathe is polluted by the carbonic acid gas exhaled by all present. And this carbonic gas is poisonous, but Peps counteract it. Don't forget that for old folks Peps are a legacy of comfort, for they stop that exhausting body-wrecking cough, and thus preserve the strength that is so precious in old age. Preachers, lecturers, singers, public speakers, and teachers should remember that Peps are the best preventive of hoarseness and laryngitis, because they keep the breathing or air-passages in a clear and healthy condition,and fortify them against the attacks of the cold-germ. Don't forget that 'the throat and lungs are the most vulnerable parts of the body, but'Peps will make them invulnerable. Therefore, keep Peps always handy where rer you are, for one never knows what perilous infection of consumption, influenza,colds, laryngitis, or sore throat may be carried about by people we are cpnstantly coming in contact with.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>OLDEST SHEEP IN ENGLAND.</head>
        <p>THE OLDEST SHEEP IN ENGLAND.</p>
        <p>"Nellie," said to be the oldest sheep in England, is still to be seen grazing in Mr. Pagets paddock at Aylestone Park, Leicester, says "Farm and Home." She is twenty years old, and during that period has had twenty-eight very fine lambs. She was bred by Mr. Harry Cuffiin, farmer, Switbland, and now residing at Desford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>OUR SERIAL STORY. t</head>
        <p>OUR SERIAL STORY. t</p>
        <p>[ALL RIGHTS IMERVED.J</p>
        <p>;ne easiest tiling in uf lottd to aide a 'man— perhaps for ever. Yes, my lawyer man, what I John Baxendale were hidden for ever?" "You wouldn't dare ! " breathed the oltl nan, with a startled look at him. Rufus Cridge laughed again. "And why tot?" he demanded. "There is only one fohn Baxendale known, and here he is." He truck himtself ea the breast with one hand iS he til.z.trid to he f.et. "The other man lisappears, and he was never even known or xivated. Who inquires? Who cares? I tell my friend, it'll :take someone cleverer thas you to turn this tangle inside out." "Well, at all events, we can't stand here liscussing the matter," said the old man tfter a gloomy pause. "Let's go home and :alk about it there. I'll admit your clever. ices, and I'll admit you hold the whip hand. But that is only for the moment; you'll be )utwitted yet. At all events, since money is :he chief thing likely to appeal to you, we'll tee if we can't arrive at some solution of the lifficulty that way. I warn you, at any rate, that this shan't be allowed to go on. I'll break my oath and tell the truth, if neces- sary." And once again I tell you that no one will believe you, and that they'll write you down a dreamer and a dotard, retorted the other man. .</p>
        <p>They walked on to the house in a silence that was unbroken, save for the snatches of 'song with which Rufus Cridge enlivened the way Coming near to the house, Felix Goode laid his hand on the man's arm i token of silence. They could distinctly hear the rattling of the chains :Ind the restless move- ments of the great dogs.. "how did they receive you?" asked the old man.</p>
        <p>"They were wiser than the humans," said Cridge, with a laugh. " I'll have 'em shot or poisoned. I'll get a new lot. I thought they'd tear me to pieces when I first came into these grounds." They went on to the house. The lawyer explained to the servant who admitted them that he would stay that night (he glanced at Cridge as he spoke) "at the request of Mr. Baxendale." Then they went together into the smoking-room, where Rufus Cridge, com- pletely master of the situation, threw himself into an easy-chair And awaited whatever the lawyer might have to say. "Now, without going into any particulars as to your past, or the life you have led," be- gan the old man, "let us come to the task you have in hand. I will admit, to begin with, that you have been badly treated. But that was not the fault of your father, at least, not directly. Your brother, you tell me, is willing to give up half his fortune to you, so that you may go abroad and enjoy it for the rest of your life. That, I undergtand. you refuse?" "Absolutely," returned Cridge. "It's all or nothing for me. I'm going to be John Baxendale. It's my -turn. I don't even admit that the other man is alive, and, in any ease, I defy you to • find him. John Baxendale is in his own home "—the man leant back in his chair comfortably and stretched out his legs—" and in that home he means to stop." "Have you thought of the difficulties?" asked the old man. " Difficulties of writing, of knowledge of this part of the world, of faces familiar to John Baxendale, of people with whom he has passed all his life, and whom you do not even know by sight. There are two sides of the scale—the one that you shall take half this great fortune and go away; the other, that you may at any mo- ment, through my efforts or your own indis- cretion, be discovered, and so get nothing." "I'll chance that," said the other. Be- sides, in any case, I should get half as my father's other son."</p>
        <p>"No you would not," said Felix Goode, quickly. Whether your father contemplated any such -happening as this I do not know; but he made a very explicit will, leaving everything of which he died posaessed to his on John Baxendale. He did not even style him his 'only son,' 60 that there can be no mistake about that."</p>
        <p>"I'm much too comfortable to argue with you," said Rufus, closing his eyes. "I'm taking all the risks. It's not for me to do anything, I leave all that to you. Besides, there's ts charming lady in the business, a lady who bel-bngs to John Basendale—which 18 me."</p>
        <p>"You won't dare to approach her!" ex- claimed the old man. "Don't be violent, my friend. I have al- ready dared, as you term it," retorted the other, with a laugh. "Now go away, and leave me alone. It's a pretty puzzle you've set yourself, and I wouldn't mind wagering you anything you like that you won't be able 'o solve it. More than that, I'll make you help me, lawyer man. I'll shelter myself be- hind you if I come to a tight place and can't manage _on my own account." -</p>
        <p>The- old mail went away, and spent an hour or two pacing about his room, and wondering what he should do. Now he was for breaking the silence of the year; and telling the truth; yet, in that ease, who would believe him? Every record of the lost boy had been care- fully destroyed or obliterated; the doctor who had been present at his birth was dead; only one son of the house of Baxendale had ever been regl6tere4. This- creature was a thing apart, outside ordinary laws. Indeed, even if the real John Baxendale we i found, it would be difficult enough to prov • hat he was John Baxendale et all.</p>
        <p>"For the present, at all events, I'll stay here," thought the old man finally, as he pre- pared for bed. "I can do some good here. I can watch events. I can put the drag on this man, for John Baxendale's sake, if he starts going down hill too fast. A bad bueiness, a sorry business. The sins of the fathetis come home to roost with a vengeance." The night passed, and brought wits it to the old man's mind no suggestion as to what he should do. It passed, too, wearily enough for the subject of his thoughts—that John Baxendale who wandered the streets ef Lon- don, shabby and forlorn, and homeless and penniless. For John Baxendale was unused to privation of any sort. He had lived com- fortably and softly all his days, and he had had no experience of any other sort of exist- ence. He recognised that Fate had been too strong for him, and that he must for the time bow his head to Fate's decree. But that humiliation was not the more pleasant be- cause it was compulsory. He waited about near Saxon Inn, Holborn, until the gates were fully opened to the public, and the night porter who had denied him admittance was gone. Then, after much hesitation, he slipped in, and climbed the stairs to his friend's rooms, only to be re- buffed at the last moment, when he was within a yard or an of his destination. The day porter had come up on some errand or other, and was emerging from the rooms as John Baxendale came within eight of the door. Haughtily enough lit waved the shabby creature before him away. "We can't 'ave no beggars 'ere," he said, "it's against the rules of the Inn. Be off with von!"</p>
        <p>"I want to see Mr. Richard Munsy," ex- claimed Baxendale, in despair. "He knows me; I'm an old friend of his. I must see him."</p>
        <p>"There's too many of your sort about," said the porter, beginning to hustle him down the stairs. "And they're most of 'em old friends. Come, don't give any trouble, or I shall know what to do with you." Protesting and pleading, John Baxendale was being hustled away from the 'door, when the jovial face of Dick Munby appeared round the edge of • it. Dick was clad in a dressing-gown, and had obviously just come from his breakfast. He looked down at the little struggle going on a few steps below him. "What's the matter, porter? be asked. "Man 'ere trying the old dodge, sir. Says 'e's a friend of yours," replied the man, look- ing up. " Dick! Dick! For the love hf Heaven let me speak to you!" cried John Baxendale; and at the sound of the voice Dick munby came hurriedly down the intervening steps, grasped his friend by the shoulder, and looked into his face.</p>
        <p>"Jack! What in the world has fitippened to you?" he cried. "Here, get out of the way, porter. Go and mind your own busi- nces and don't be so !lewdly officious in tiler</p>
        <p>future. How dare vou turir 'gestieman</p>
        <p>away!" . John Baxendalo .!and himself being hur- ried into Dick Munby's rooms, while the porter retired, muttering to himself certain observations concerning gentlemen in general and this ragged one in particular. Inside the rooms John Baxendale sank into a chair. covered his face with his hands, and shuddered. "Oh! the horror of this night!" he ex- claimed. Dick Munby stood staring down at his friend for a minute or two in utter bewilder- ment, now noting the shabby clothing, now the touzled hair and unkempt appearance. Suddenly remembering his duties as host, he made a dash for some hot coffee upon the table, and poured it out, and offered- it to 'John Baxendale, who drank it eagerly. As he handed back the cup he started to his feet and caught Dick by the arm, and stared at him like one distraught. "Dick, look well at me, examine me closely," lie exclaimed. "Who ti I?" "Come, pull yourself together, old chap," said Dick, a little Awed in spite of himself. "Of course, you're Baxendale—Jack Baxen- dale. Though why in this get up—" "You're certain of that?' broke in Baxen- dale, sharply. "You're not deceived in any way, BO that you think by chance I may be someone else, but that you dare not tell me so?" "I think you want something stronger 4.lan coffee, if you're going on like this," said Diek Munby, with a laugh. "How in the world did you manage to come here, and to find me, and know me, if you were not Jack Baxendale? Now tell me all that has hap- pened, and why you are in London in this plight." Very gradually, with many pauses and much hesitation, and some going back over points that did not seem to be clear, John Baxendale told the story of all that had hap pened, and filially asserted his belief that the man who was his brother had actually taken his place, and was then masquerading down at Baxendale Hall. "What am I to do, Dick—what am I to do?" asked Baxendale, when he had finished. "What hope is there for me to establish my- aelf again in the place Rufus Cridge has usurped?" Dick Munby stared at his friend for a mo- ment or two, and then suddenly smote hie forehead with a cry. "By Jove! Then it wasn't you I saw down at Baxendale Hall yesterday!" he ex- claimed. "It wasn't you that had such A difficulty in knowing the way to Miss Kay- lor's house—" "Has he met her?" broke in Baxendale, quickly. "Heaven forgive me! I left them to- gether," said Dick. "Of course, I never thought it could be anyone but you—in those clothes, and with that voice and that appear- ance." _ _ .</p>
        <p>"Now, perhaps, ,you understand what this man's power is," said John Baxendale, "Save by a trick, I cannot re- establish myself. lie is more cunning than I am, and he may be able to persuade people of his bona fides more easily than I can do. At the present moment I am Rufus- Cridge— penniless and hopeless in London." "Not hopeless, and certainly not penniless after this, said Dick Munby, cordially. "There's only one way that I can see, and that is for us to gq down to Baxendale Hall and try conclusions with the man there. As you say, for your father's sake the story must not be told; but we may match cunning with cunning, and trickery with trickery, and out- wit this felbiw yet. Once bring him back to the condition of Rufus Cridge and we can dictate terms to him. At present he is in a position to dictate to us." "But can't you get me some clothes, Dick, something better than these?" asked John 13axendale, looking down at his deplorable attire.</p>
        <p>"Well, in the first place, nothing that I !Ave would fit you," said Dick Munby, with I laugh, " and, in the second, I think it -would be madness for you to put off the dis- pise yet awhile. Remain Rufus Cridge until in some fashion you can change with the im- postor. and so becom9 John Baxendale in outward appearance. I tell you that our first business is to get to Deepington—under 'cover of the night, if possible—and see what i best to be done. Is there any other friend you have who will be likely to help you?" "There's old Felix Goode,- the lawyer," said Baxendale, after a moment of thought. "He knows the story, and will understand." "Very well, then, you wait here and I will go off and find him. You needn't be afraid," said. Dick; "no one will disturb you here, and I will, if possible, bring Felix Goode back with Me. Make yourself comfortable, and don't worry." Dick Munby returned in less than an hour with a rueful face. "No one knows exactly where he is," he reported. "HiS clerk says that he left town last night, and was expected back to-day. Beyond that they knew nothing. I'm afraid we can't do much in that quarter." "At least we can get to ,Deepington," urged John Baxendale. "We can't travel together, Dick, because I'm a sight to frighten crows, but we can meet down there and decide what is best to be done. There is one who knows the story, and who will not hesitate to speak, if I call upon her." "Who is she?" asked Dick. t.</p>
        <p>"A woman who is straining every nerve to get hold of Rufus Cridge, to draw him back to her. While he remains apparently John Baxendale he is lost to her. She may prove a powerful ally for us in case of need." It AVM finally arranged that Dick should supply Baxendale with money for the ~pres- ent, and that they should start for Deeping- ton that evening, 60 soon as darkness had fallen. As a matter of fact, they travelled by the same train, though without taking any notice of each other; and when they got to Deepington the obsequious stationmaster, who had touched his cap to the spurious John Baxendale not so long before, looked after this shabby figure shuffling out of the station, and wondered frowningly what 119 was doing in those parts. But within it couple of hundred yards of the station the apparent outcast met his friend Dick Munby, and together they sat on a fallen tree in a wood a little below the high road, to discuss the situation.</p>
        <p>My plan, Dick, is for us to get into the house, and for me to get into my own clothes, and declare who I am. That's the simplest way—and the easiest." Thus John Baxen- dale, after several suggestions had been made by Dick and by himself. "And so bring two John Baxendales to life, and let the one with the most cunning %%in— to say nothing of declaring the story to all the world," said Dick. "No, my dear Jack, there's a better php that that, I'm sure, and we shall light upon it." It was quite late when they thought they had lighted upon that plan by the merest ac- eident. The high road above where they sat stretched along under the moon, a broad grey streak; and presently they heard coming along it in the distance the sound of foot- steps—unsteady footsteps as they got nearer, and mingling with them the sound of a man singing. They got slowly to their feet, loek- ing at each other in the darkness and listen- ing; then, as by a common impulse, they stole forward towards the edge of the, wood. That which came along the road was a startling sight, to Dick Dfunhy, at least; for there, advancing towards them in the moon- light, and swaying unsteadily from one side of the road to the other, came, apparently, John Baxendale, in his habit as he had lived, and as Dick had seen him any ttine this past, four or five years. It was hard to believe for a moment that Dick himself had not been cheated, and that this man beside, him was not the outcast and impostor he looked. But in a moment he had flung that thought away from him, and had whispered excitedly' to Baxendale: "Our chance! Get him now, and strip him. Change with him as he changed with you. The thing is easy enough." John Baxendale nodded quickly, in token that he understood, and the two men crept forward so as to- be able to spring out upon the road and interupt Rufus Cridge. But when that unconscious individual was within twenty yards of them another figure sprang out from the shadows and made towards the reeling man—and this time it was the figure of a woman. ller face shewed clearly in the moonlight, and John Baxendale, with a gasp of surprise, sprang at her and seized her .and o;nnerl a hand ova her Duluth as she wall na</p>
        <p>the very point of crying out. She wriggled herself partly free and looked at him; struggled fiercely to get away. But he held her tight, until the man, who had hesitated for a moment on the road as the sound of that slight scuffle floated to his ears, had gone on again; and only when Cridge was out of. Eight and hearing did Baxendale ta.ke his hand from the woman's lips. "Well, Bella," he said, sternly, "so you meant to play a trick on me, did you?" "Yes, I did," she retorted, sullenly. "I meant to warn him. I didn't want him caught in a trap. And I'll do it yet if I get the &amp;wince.' "This is the woman I told you about," saici bha Baxendale, turning to Dick, and still keeping his grip upon her. " She's got a game to play as well a.s others, it seems; she's dangerous." "What are you going to do to him?" de- manded Bella.</p>
        <p>"That depends," said Dick, speaking for the first time. "You'd best keep out of it; it isn't work for a woman. It'll be bad for you if by any chance you interfere." "My man's worth two of you," said the rio, looking at the shabby figure of John Baxendalc contemptuously. "He's done you once, and he'll do you again, and yet again. If it comes to his matclung his wits against yours, I know who'll win." The business was desperate, and every moment of value; it became a question first of expostulations and then of threats to the woman. Finally she promised she would not interfere—without in the 'east meaning to keep thy t promise. Ansi BO at last they left her and crept on together towards the great house.</p>
        <p>Even= had certainly taken a strange turn when, f )r the second time within a matter of days, that man who might have been called Rufus .Cridge by any who met him broke into the house of the Baxendales. This time, however, the new Rufus Cridge was not with- out support. Dick 3lunby, listening carefully for ar.y chance sound in the house, followed his friend along, the silent corridors until they came to the door leading into the smoking- room; and there, they listened for a moment or two, hearing. nothing; and so decided at last that the usurper had gone to bed. John Baxendale opened the door cautiously and wKnit in; saw That the windows were cur- tained, and so put up the lights. "So far, so good," whispered Dick Munby. "Now, what's the next move? What are we to do now? "</p>
        <p>"I think our friend is going to solve it for hitneelf," whispered Baxendale, _in reply. "At all events, I can hear someone coming cautiously down the stairs." "Then in that case you'd better get one side of the doorway, and I'll get the other; then we can pounce on him when he comes in," suggested Dick. And adopting that plan they waited for the _ man, who.se stepb they could now hear with some distinctness.</p>
        <p>The door opened at last, slowly, and a man walked quickly in. Not Rufus Cridge, but. astoundingly enoUgh, to 'their eyes, at least, Felix Goode. In a moment Munby had clapped to the door; and as the old man turned, with a faint cry of surprise, he faced the two young men. ." What's this? " he demanded, sus- piciously: "What are you doing here, Mr. Mu nby, at this hour—and 174 ho is this fellow l"</p>
        <p>Baxendale stepped forward and looked the old man squarely the face. "Don't you know me?" he asked. And in a moment Felix Goode had seized him by the hand, and was shaking it vigorously, and calling -him his dear boy, and .thanking the good God that had r,ent him back again. After that they sat, down and proceeded to di 3C1166 the situation from every possible point of view, rejecting this idea and tenta- tively touching • upon that as a mere posed. bility—and all in the manner of. earnest and desperate men. For the man sleeping above in his room was a force to be reckoned with,. and must be fought with his own weapons. "If I could get him into these clothes, and myself into my .own again, all would be well," said John Baxendale more than once: "I'm of a mind to go up to the room that k mine and drag him out of bed,- and compei him to dress in these things "—he touched his own clothes as he spoke—" and then bundle him out of the place, neck and crop, to tell what stay he liked.". "And have him rouse the house with his cries, and bring the servants down upon us, with impossible explanations to follow,'4 said Felix Goode. "I've a better plan that %hat, and a simpler one. Come with me and I.'ll. show you." _ _</p>
        <p>Guided by the old man they went upstairs until they reached the door of John Baxen- dale's bedroom. After listening for a mo- ment the old man turned to them, and, mur- muring, "In a drunken sleep," opened the door and went in, followed by them. There on the bed lay Rufus Cridge, Sleeping heavily, and beside him—torsed upon the floor, or anywhere else where he had hap- pened- to fling them—were John Baxendale's clothes.</p>
        <p>"Now," whispered Felix Goode, "here's your chance. Change. now, and leave these shabby garments of his beside the bed. 'Then I will unscrew the bulbs from the electrio light, so that he may switch away for hat hour, and he won't bc able to get a light et any kind. Leave the rest to me. Lock the door of your drersnig-room, so that there is no possibility of ins getting any other sit:ic Now come away and leave him."</p>
        <p>Very rapidly Baxerulale changed his clothes, leaving Cridge's deplorable garments handy by the bedside; then, wondering ft little what was to happen, came away with Felix Goode and Munby. But when they got down into the 6moking-room again the lawyer explained his purpose..</p>
        <p>"Go out, Mr. -11Tunby, to the stables, and ring the great alarm bell that hangs in the turret there. Ring as you never rang before, and rouse the house."</p>
        <p>In a couple of minutes the silence of the house was broken hideously by the clamour of the bell. Doors were flung open, and frightened voices cried to each other. There was much knocking at John Baxendale's door. and shouts to him to get up, for the house was on fire., Rufus Cridge, waking hazily from that drunken slumber, tumbled out of bed, and pulled on his clothes in the darkness, staggered down somehow in the confusion into the open air, and ran on wildly, scarcely knowing what he did, through the grounds. So presently it happened that, in the moon- fight, with the clamour of the bell ended, he stood in the silence, and looked at himself and woke to the full consciousness that he was Rufus Cridge again—homeless, with the great silent house of the Baxendales closed and dark before him. (To be continued.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>MS FATHER'S SON.</head>
        <p>MS FATHER'S SON.</p>
        <p>DY TOM GALLON</p>
        <p>author of " Tatterley," "Dicky Monteith,' "Kiddy," "A Rogue in Love," "Fate's Beg. gar Maid," "The Charity Ghost," &amp;e.</p>
        <p>SYNOPSIS OF PRECEDING CHAPTERS: The guests have separated after the dinner which celebrates John Baxendale's inheritance of his fortune, and he has told Evelyn Kaylor, the beautiful girl he is to marry, that he has not a care in the world. Yet there is a strange ioreboding of coming ill. He feels practically certain that someone is prowling about. the grounds. When the last guest has retired, Felix Goode, the family lawyer, begins the unfolding of the sinister secret which is to alter the whole tenour of John Baxendale's life. In revenge for a wrong whi;h his father did to a woman named Johanna Crigge she had stolen away one of twin sons, leaving the awful message that, just as her life had passed into darkness and evil, so should his. He should be educated, and yet trained to a life of infamy. That brother of John Baxen- dale may now at any time confront him. This story is told him at his father's request, under his dying irjunction that it shall be preserved a secret. Baxendale, when on the way to his room, bears a sound in the drawing-room. Entering, and go- ing to on of the windows, he confronts and seizes a burglar, to find himself face to face with a man in own likeness. Then John Baxendale does a dramatic thing. Inquiry cimvincing him that this man is his own brother, he astonishes him with the assurance, that he purposes to divide equally the inherit- ance bet amen them on the sole condition that the new-conaer leaves the country and keeps the secret He is taken with Rufus's suggestion that Le should go and see the life he has lived, and so one evening makes his way to Middle- ton': Rents, on the south side of the river. On enPering the house he is felled from behind. With mischief in his eye, Rufus Cridge puts on the clothes of his wealthy brother.. He goes to Baxendale Hall, and there meets D:ck Munby, the barrister, who—astonished at "13axenda1e's" gruffness -- leaves him with Evelyn Kaylor, with whom Cridge makes good his opportunity to plead for an early wedding. That night the family lawyer, passing the village inn, is drawn in by the landlord. "John Baxen- dale" is within, intoxicated, but Felix Goode's sharp ears and eyes tell him that here before him stands "the other man." Where is John Baxendale? John Baxendale is bidden in the basement ef Johanna Cridge's house. "Bella," whose love has been given to Cridge, stands outside, per- plexed at his absence, mechanically kicking a corner of the thick basement glass let into the pavement. A piece falls through, and hearing s. moan for help, she stoops, discovering, as she believes, her lover below. While Johanna sleeps Bella rescues him. Baxendale tells her his story, and, stung with jealousy, she goes to the Hall to claim Cridge as hers against all the Evelyn Kaylors in existence!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>CHAPTER V.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER V.</p>
        <p>That worthy man of law, Mr. Felix Goode, had been faced with perhaps the deepest pro- blem of his life, and the one most difficult of solution, when he stood before the drunken, swaying figure of Rufus Cridge, in the like- _ ness and appearance of John Baxendale. His alert mind took in the details of the scheme at once, and in taking them in shelved him clearly the terrible difficulty in which he and the real John Baxendale stood. His oath to the dead man kept him silent; more than that, he knew that it would be impossible for him to make anyone unacquainted with the story believe that such a change as this had taken place. In some mysterious fashion that man whose advent he had feared had sprung into the heart of the story, and stood here in John Baxendale's shape, and with John Bax- endale's character in his hands, to make or to mar. There was, of course, too, that other problem, what had become of John Baxen- dale himself?</p>
        <p>The possibilities of the thing were so great that for a little time the lawyer was too shaken to do anything but stare at the man before him. For he realised -what that man was; had known enough of Johanna Cridge to know that she had kept her word, and that this man was what she alone, in her vindic- tive spirit, could have made him. And this was the man who held the destiny of the house of Baxendale in his hands, and who was, so far as the world Was concerned, or .so far as the world could know, the man who had the right to claim Evelyn Kaylor for his wife.</p>
        <p>His first proceeding was to lock the door of that room in the inn, and then to make some attempt to reason with the man, and to get him away from that place, before others should overhear anything he might say in recklees talk. It took a long time for him, even with threats, to have any sobering effect upon the man; but at last Felix Goode got him into a more tractable state, and having seen to it that the startled landlord had got ,the pas- sages and doorway clear, got his man out into the streets of the sleepy little town, and so away in the direction of Baxendale Hall. Keeping tight hold of the swaying man, and listening with disgust to his, mutterings, and his attempts at singing, and his curses, the lawyer led him along the road under the sum- mer stars, until he was far away from any human habitation. Then, as the man wrenched himself free, and faced him, Felix Goode prepared himself for what he had to say. "Well, my fine lawyer man, what do you think of it all?" demanded Rufus Cridge, boisterously. "Here's a turn of the wheel for you; here's an upsetting of nice little plans, duly laid and properly arranged! Down goes John Baxendale, and up comes John Baxen- dale number two! What are you going to do about it?"</p>
        <p>"Send you back to the gutter you came from, to begin with," said the little old man, stoutly. "No good; try something else," spluttered Cridge. "You must have a John Baxendale, you know, and it'll puzzle you to find any other but me just now." "What have you done with the real man?" demanded Felix Goode.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rufus Cridge seated himself on a bank at the roadside, and thrust his hands into his pockets, and stretched out his legs, and rolled about with laughter. "That's what I'm not going to tell you," Ire said at bat. "Why should you be, so infernally particu- lar, my lawyer man? Isn't one John flaxen. dale enough for you? Besides, this one's a great deal jollier than the one you've lost. Square your books afresh, and start again with a better man."</p>
        <p>"Not so much of your lawyer man, my friend," retorted Goode. "And how, by the way, do you happen to know who I am, or what I am?"</p>
        <p>"Because I've seen you before," said Cridge, with another laugh. "I. was in the house the night you were there; John Baxen. dale told me that that was the night on which you told him the story about ne. lie wanted to find out if the likeness was as great as he believed and I believed; so he stuck me be- hind the chair when you came knocking at the door in the small hours; then he changed places with me afterwards. You spoke to both John Baxendales that night, lawyer man." "I remember now," said the old man, slowly. "I remember that I thought I had beard voices."</p>
        <p>"That's it; we were arguing it out to- gether. A rare good sort was that John Baxendale ; swore he'd give me half his for- tune if I'd go abroad." "That was like him, generous fool ! " re- torted the lawyer. "But half wasn't good enough for me. wanted the lot or nothing. He'd had his twenty-five years, and the beet twentylive, too. I wanted mine. So here I am, and a good time I'm going to have of it, too." "I'll expose you. I'll tell the story every- where, and turn you out in the road," ex- gaimed the other. "You mustn't. llc told me that you were bound by, a promise not to do that," said Cridge. ' Besides, who'd believe you ? " " I'll find the real John Baxendale and oring him back here." "You've got to find him first," said Cridge, with another laugh. "You forget the shady I'Ke lived amongst ttll thesfi ware. Ire</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Bold-street Bible Class.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Bible Class.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett, J.P., addressed the members and friends of the Bold-street Bille Class on Sunday after- noon on "Some impressions of the Continent." He said that religion should not he severed from every day life. The main reason why religion was unpopular was because it was so devorced from ordinary life and it seemed to him that in their desire to attain to the City of God which was incorruptible they might attempt to make the present life more human. All qucstions connected with the uplifting of the masses were in harmony with Christian teaching. The Alderman gave a review of his visit to the Continent a full account of which is appearing in the columns of the "Exarnicer.'</p>
        <p>TALK ABOUT.. TARIFF REFORM!</p>
        <p>THIS MAN HAD 30 YEARS' ABSOLUTE PROTECTION:</p>
      </div>
