      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>His Majesty's Birthday.—The Kirg will next week complete his sixty-eigth year. and with five or six exceptions, his Majesty has attained a greater age, than any previous occupant of the English throne. King- ship was not a long-lived profession in olden times, but Edward I. who died in 1307, lived to be 68, as did also JRII3CI3 I. nn 3 George IV. George 111. and Queen Victoria wore the longest lived Sovereigns, both reaching 81, George 11. was 77, William IV. 72, snd Queen Elizabeth 70. Thn King however would seem to have discovered the secret of "how not to grow old," an art in which Queen Alexandra has been no less successful. The means and leisure to command all that is best that the world can offer, do not in every case mean good health, and long life, but few indeed have succeeded better in the art of remaining young than Queen Alexandra. whilst it may be said of the King that he leads a more active life than most men of his age, Golden Weddings.—The Duke and Duchess of Buccleuch this month celebrate their golden wedding, as will also the Earl and Countess of Lucan, who were married on November 17th, 1759. The Duke of Buccleuch is in his seventy-ninth year. His estates which are administered by his son and heir, the Earl of Dalkeith, extend to nearly half a million acres, and next to the Duke of Sutherland he is the largest ducal landowner. The Duchesses of Buceleuch have been favourites with Royalty for generations past, and the present duchess, one of a family of ten brothers and sisters, including the Duke of Abercorn, and Lady Lansdowne, has held the office of Mistress of the Robes since 1895. Her father, the first Duke of Abereorn was known among his intimates as "Old splendid." The Earl of Lucan is the son of the distinguished Field- Marshal, and served with his father in the Crimea, while the Countess, who has done much to promote Irish home industries, is the youngest daughter of the fifth Duke of Richmond.</p>
        <p>The Eternal Feminine Question.--Lady McLaren, who is ono of the leading Liberal hostesses, is the author of a pamphlet entitled "The Women's Charter of Rights and Liberties." There is reason to believe that the public is becoming a little tired of the Feminine Question, but it has come to stay, and it is a subject on which Lady McLaren writes with authority. Her charter would mainly deal with the laws concerning the wife, inheritance of property, the guardianship of children, the gereral conditions of married women in the working classes and many other matters relating to the status and interests of women. One of last Sunday's papers, which prints extracts from its corresponding issue of a hundred years ago, records that on a certain day in October, 1809, a man publicly sold his wife at Westminster for two guineas, 'amidst a concouree of spectators who had assembled to view the novel (in that part of the town) and disgraceful scene." This sug- gests that the practice of wife selling was common enough in the east end of London, to justify the jibe which the French long levelled against us as s nation, but although the married woman is no longer a "chattel " much yet remains to be reformed, including the unequal laws of divorce, to secure the rights and liberties of women quite apart from the question of the Parliamentary vote. The Divorce Commission.—Lady Frances Balfour, who is one of the two ladies on the Royal Commission, which is to inquire into the law of divorce, is distinctly a happy selection. A daughter of the late Duke of Argyll, and sister-in-law of Mr. A. J. Balfour, she has always taken the greatest interest in question affecting the rights and liberties of her sex, and is a leading figure among the non-militant section of the Woolens' Suffrage movement. The other member is Mrs. IL J. Tennant,formerly a superintending inspector of factories and now the wife of Mr. Asquith's brother-in-law, the Liberal M.P. for Berwick. The Croquet Temper.—There wit I be no use for croquet mallets until next Easter, unless perchance the new maid happens to take them to break the coals. Players however will be interested in the dictum of a judge of the High Court who said the other day that "he knew of no game that put one out of "temper so much as croquet." There are few players who will not agree, especially when they happen to be the inferior player, or have the worst of the luck. No doubt it is because a player always uses an advantage to leave his opponent in the worst possible position. Similar manceuvres tire often adopted at billiards, but it is a fact that people who play other games with perfect good temper, often find croquet very aggravating, especially if they are playing a losing game. The Walking Skirt.—We fought hard a long with the fashionable tailor, but at length we have our way, and the walking skirt is really a comfortable garment, instead of a mass of material to hold nn. We all-know that the skirt "which just clears the ground "—so beloved by tailors in days gone by—is very charming to behold, but we also know that it is one of the most uncomfortable specimens in which to walk. that is if we have the faintest respect for our skirt hem.. Walk- ing skirts 'or town wear reach only to the ankles, while those destined for the country are shorter still.</p>
        <p>Fashionable Trains.—Every house and evening gown possesses a train, or at any rate an appendage that goes by that name. Mannequins, so perfectly &amp;Mel that doubtless they could acquit themselves gracefully in a bolster case, manage, it is true to glide about in such a manner that the funny little train creepg after them quite smoothly, but the ordinary individual presents a ludicrous appearance when wearing the approved train gown. The customary procedure of the " train" is very much as follows. First of all it assumes a most undignified wiggle-waggle, then it turns over and over, until it is all curled up into an nnsiehtly-looking Needless to say, such an appendage does not make for charm,</p>
        <p>A Useful Wrap.--The hitter] .coat lus evidently come to stay. It is now possible to match almost any colour and even when difficulties arise in this direc- tion it is a simple matter to have a coat dyed to order. The majority however still prefer white or cream, and worn over a short pleated white or cream serge skirr, these coats look particularly well. They possess also the merit of being possible wear with coloured skit ts, though the effect is not so good as when coat and skirt match. The latest idea is to add collar revers, and deep cuffs, of soft suede, the suede matching the tcne of the wool precisely. The idea mnl(en for fismartness, but we snould do well to remember that in white, cream or light shades, the suede spells a visit to the dry cleaner, whereas the perfectly plain varieties can be inexpen- sively ane quite easily washed at home. Eccentric Veilings.—A few extremists are wearing fine black lace veils, bearing fearsome designs. One for instance takes the form of a spider in his web, another a snail, and the veils are so arranged that a spider or a snail—as the case maybe—appears upon one cheek! At first sight, the wearers seem to be hopelessly disfigured, or stricken with some terrible Ain disease and it is only when the veil is lifted or thrown back, one experiences a feeling of relief that this is not the case. Few things are more becoming than a carefully chosen and carefully Arranged veil, and just now the most charming and altogether delightful vailings are being placed uron the market. One can only marvel there- fore, that women can be found so ill-judged and foolish as to don such atrocities as those described above</p>
        <p>MADGE.</p>
        <p>PIONEER OF GROUND VALUES TAXATION. The Berlin correspondent of the "Times" states that Herr Leopold Sonnemann, the founder of the "Frankfurter Zeitung" died on Saturday at the age of seventy-eight. His paper is the principal democratic organ of South Germany. Herr Sonnernann was a member of the Frankfurt Town Council, and he is believed to have originated the system of taxation of ground values, which has since caused the example of Frankfurt to be so frequently invoked in discussior about taxation for municipal purposes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>WHAT IS TRUTH ?</head>
        <p>WHAT IS TRUTH ?</p>
        <p>Interesting Paper at the Academy.</p>
        <p>A paper entitled "Truth "was read by Mr. J. Jagger, of St. Helens, at the Old Academy on Monday evening in connection with the Warrington Society. Mr. L• Greening presided over a good attendance. The ESSAYIST said that their subject was a very comprehensive and intricate one, and it appeared to them to be absolutely necessary to examine the methocht by which they arrived at so-called trutb. Those wer! two in number, which they called the inductive aec; deductive methods. He said the inductive meth of investigating and discovering truth was nevi' recognised as the scientific method. Anothef term for induction was generalisation and the gret , object of science was to generalise. Individual fel"' had their value, of course, simply as individual facts' just as a brick in itself was worth something as a brick. But the real object of science was to Fe able to state e vast number of particular or individual facts as ece great fact which included them all. Such a statement was a generalisation or induction, and its value bore the same relation to any of the individual facts which it expressed th it a mighty building bore to any of the bricks of which it was composed. In the ordinarY language both of science and common speech, generalisations such as they had described NigBr? commonly called laws. Every generalisation withow; exception was more comprehensive than the series 01 particular facts on which it was erected. They conic' never have too many facts upon which to build and theY could never be too modest in the expression of On" elusions. Deductive reasoning was entirely dependent upon induction for its foundation. Neither the ind tive nor deductive method of investigating and dis- covering truth could stand alone, and they leigu" safely say that no scientific conclusion was perfectii established until it could be reached alike by induction and deduction, alike by direct observation and by true reasoning, of the results of each rendered great service! to the other. Diversity of opinion always hid and still obtained in science, in religion, in politics, lc ethics and in social questions of every kind. The oeli; fiicting views were so abundant that one might well wonder if there were any truth at all. But had that apparent confusion no meaning? Might it not 10", necessary to the pursuit of knowledge and the truth They thought it might. Man's knowledge was limited and therefore his knowledge of truth must limited and and being limited they could never say that it waS perfect. They took it that absolute, unchangeable and infallible truth presupposed infinite knowledge and infinite knowledge,aa.only possessed by the Infinite Mind. In conclusion he said that they never got to f the real und ultimate essence of things, the range o their observation was never sufficient to cover all the facts which their generalisations embraced and as long as that was the case, although their knowledge toighti he relatively true and sufficient for all practica purposes of life, it would be liable to change as their knowledge increased. (Applause). An interesting discussion then took place in which the following took part Messrs. Lofthouse, Grosvenor, F. R. Roberts, Madeley, Jolley, Gibson, Greening, Dr* Naden, Rev. C. Harvey-Cook and Mrs. Ransom.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>BEWARE OF NOVEMBER</head>
        <p>BEWARE OF NOVEMBER</p>
        <p>The Month of Maladies.</p>
        <p>November is the worst month of the year for all who are weak and ailing. This dismal month of aches and ills finds out the special weaknesses of old and young of both sexes; and Influenza, Rheumatism, Neuralgia, Sciatica, Lumbago, and Chest Complaints become alarmingly prevalent.</p>
        <p>To those to whom November brings these trials the advice of Mrs. Pamela Waters, of The Common, Gilwern, near Abergavenny, is opportune and invaluable. ." Every Autumn, since I was a young girl," saii.cl Mrs. Waters, "I have suffered severely wit" Rheumatism. But last November the Rheumation seized me worse than ever, and by Christmas tirne it had crippled me. The pain first attacked al left leg, just above the knee. Then the other joints swelled, and the leg became absolutely useless. "I only got snatches of sleep at nights because my muscles twitched with the excruciating pain -- just as though someone was sawing the limb off. "We did not call in a doctor because we live so far in the country, but I tried various remedies; however, I could not have borne the dreadful Rheumatism much longer. I could eat very little' and my nerves were completely unstrung. "At this stage my husband got me a supplY Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, and after taking a fel boxes the pain abated, and the swellings we.?, down. I kept on taking Dr. Williams' Pink PI until all traces of Rheumatism disappeared, and was able to move about actively. Then the Pill! - invigorated my nerves, and fully restored 01Y general health. I owe my freedom from Rheumatism this year entirely to Dr.Williams' Pills, and I recommend the Pills to anyone w whom the Autumn is a time of triAl. "My mother also used to be a martyr t?, Rheumatism every winter. Before each attae* she was out-of-sorts, and of a sallow complexlos- - Rheumatism attacked her causing era agony—almost beyond description. "The doctors treated her for a long time, -+5 they could not take the pain away. All her jo111:11 were tender and swollen. She suffered, too, vci,`r fearful pains in the head and back, and each wilite she became so weak that it was pitiable to see lief'. "Dr. 'Williams' Pink Pills cured her perfectly a: they cured me. After she had taken a few boxe; ,of the Pills she was strong and well, and she 113 remained well ever since her cure."</p>
        <p>re Dr. Williams' Pink Pills create New, Good Blood, and r. invaluable to both old and young, at all seasons of the Yea" They have cured Anemia Debility, Indigestion. Ecseinp the After-Effects of Colds,'lnfluenza, Fevers ; Rheumonti,e_rt.; Sciatica, Lumbago, the many forms of Nervous Die dreaded by men, St. Vitus' Dance, Paralysis, and the of the weaker sex. 2s. 9d. for one box, or 13s. 9d. iy'; six boxes post free from Dr. Williams' Medicine ConlPan2 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, B.C. ; also of dealers. Substitutes are unless and never the same as</p>
