      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>DISTRICT NEWS. ....,--...-..,....,„--...-‘,-..,,A,</head>
        <p>DISTRICT NEWS. ....,--...-..,....,„--...-‘,-..,,A,</p>
        <p>Winwick.</p>
        <p>MOTOR COLLISION.—There was a startling affair at liVinwick on Monday when a collision occurred near the Huime Smithy between a large van belonging to Messrs. Maginn, of Wrrrington, box manufacturers, and a motor car containing a lady and gentleman. The back portion of the car was badly smashed but, fottunately, the driver of the van and the occupants of the car escaped with a severe shaking.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>ST. THOMAS'S BAND OF HOPE.—'J here was a success- ful gathering of this society in the Parish Room, on Monday, when Miss Bessie Dutton of Moore delivered an address of kindly and useful counsel. Songs were Riven by Miss M. Blackburn, Miss B. Caldwell apd Sam Smith ; and recitations by A. Rutter, D. Hallows, L. Manuel V. and E. Vernon, C. Welsby, L. Whiteman, B. Reward, and M. Shepherd. The Vicar, who was in the chair, warmly thanked Miss Dutton on Lehalf of him- self and those present for her presence. Mr. Hallows urged the parents and friends to act on Miss Dutton's advice and take a deeper interest in the society. A vote of thanks, proposed by Mr. Whiternim, was passed to the Vicar for prssiding and to all who had taken Part in :he meeting. HILL CLIFFE AND STOCKTON HEATH UNITED BAND OF HOPE.—Mr. Arthur Nlountleld presided at the second entertainment of the session which was held in Providence Schoolroom on Monday evening. The pro- gramme consisted of a abort lantern lecture givEn by Mr. Mounfield, several interesting pictures being shown relating to the founding of the teroperauce movement at Stockton Heath. Many other slides were shown, illustrating the advantages of temperance. Other items consisted of songs by Master Reggie Grounds, Evelyn Pollitt and Frances Gamble. Reciations by Elsie Jarems, Elsie Roughsedge. Reggie Moss and 'Thomas Pearce, were well given, whilst Ernest Clarke and Harold Stringer conlributed the dialogue, "Timothy's clock," in good style. A hearty vote of thanks to all Who had 'taken part in the proceedings, and to Mr. Moubtield for his address, was proposed by Mr. G. Whitfield and careed. Mr 0. A. %Nhittield acted as *ccompanitt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Penketh.</head>
        <p>Penketh.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF INTR. JAMES HAY.—It is with regret we record the death of one of Penketh's oldest parishioners in the person of Mr. ;lames Hayes, which took place at his residence in Warrington-road on Friday night of last week. Mr. Hayes who had been ailing tor some time, was in his seventieth year, and at one time was in business in the village as wheelwright and joiner'. 'I he interement took place cn Mcnday at Sankey, the Rev. J. R. Jones, M.A., performed the last rites at the gravebide. The funeral was attended by many sorrowing relatives and friends. Deceased leavts a widow and six children.</p>
        <p>WRIST DRIVE.—A most successful • whist drive was held at the Liber.il Club the other evening, there being present about 40 members. This is the first whist drive this session, but as the members enjoy these !unctions so much, it is the desire or the Committee to hold several during the winter months. Mr. F. Dendy proved himself the winner of the gentlemen's prize with 116 trick., and Mr. R. Darlington (playing as lady) won the ladies' prize with 120 tricks. 'the credit for the success of the whist drive is due to Messrs. H. Davies (secretsry), F. Davies (caretaker), W. Dwerry- house and the Committee.</p>
        <p>SANKEY LADIES' HOCKEY CLun.—The Great Sankey Ladies' Hockey Club held their fifth annual whist drive and %lance in the Great Sankey Sunday school on Friday of last week, commencing at 7.:30. The music for danc- ing was provided by Mr. A. Starkey, and the programme proved to be all that could be desired. The committee are to be congratulated on the way the room bad been decorated. Messrs. H. Kertland and L. Pierpoint acted as M.C..s. The winners of the whist prizes were: Mrs. Collin Cooke, ladies' prize • and Miss Tinsley (playing as gentleman) won the gentleman's prize. LANTERN LECTURE.—On Monday evening a most interesting lecture illustrated by limelight views was given by Mr. G. E. Thompson in the Lecture Hell at the Friends School. The subject was "A journey in the South of Ireland." Views of the following places of interest being given : Cork, Killarney, Bantry Bay, Limerick, Buskmills, the Giant Causeway. and the Blarney Stone. The lecture was greatly enjoyed and Mr. Thompson, who was very humorous in his remarks, evoked much laughter. The meeting was presided over by Mr. W. E. Brown. A very hearty vt,te of thanks to Thompson was proposed by Mr. A. Roberts and seconded by Miss B. Brown. Mr. Robinson worked the lantern.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL CLE B.—The second debate this sesslon took place at the Liberal Club on Tuesday evening when about 50 members and friends turned up to hear a debate on "The economics of labour." Mr. E. Marshall opened the debate in a very able manner. He referred to a remark made by Viscount Wolmer in a speech he tnade in the village a little time ago when he said that labour had nothing to do with producing capital, but that capitil produced labour. Mr. Mar.hall ma.de his points very clearly. He said capital was stored up labour. The debate was of such a character that those present must have felt it a pleasure to be there. Several members spoke in a manner that made it clear that the working men of Penketh are thinking for themselves.— Mr. .1. H. Mainly presided over the meeting in a very tactful manner. A vote of thanks was proposed to Mr. Marston and the chairman by Mr. A. T. Clay seconded by Mr. W. Davies and supported by Mr. D. Ridyard. The chairman informed those present that the next debate would take place on the 16th November the subject would be "Should bachelors be taxed," the meeting then closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>SANKEY PARISH 00UNCIL.</head>
        <p>SANKEY PARISH 00UNCIL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the above Council was held on Tuesday night in the Sanlsey, Council Schools. Mr. W. Ta)lor presided, and the following were also present, Messrs. W. Furriest J. W. Walters, W. H. Ireland, H. Fairclougb, W. Ditchfleld and J. Smith (clerk).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>LIGHTING OF HIGHFIELD_AVENUF:..</head>
        <p>LIGHTING OF HIGHFIELD_AVENUF:..</p>
        <p>With reference to the lighting of Highfield-avenue, the clerk said that Mr. Sutton, the clerk of the District Council, had written to him saying that they had come to the conclusion that the Council ought not to light the avenue. Mr. Fairclough : I thought we had no power to light Hi ghfield-avenue. • The Cterk It is a complicated question and Mr. Sutton has spent considerable time in going into it. The Chairm n suggested that the lamp which is at Present situated by the Vicarage, should he removed and put on the other side of the road opposite Highfield- avenue in order to light up the entrance to it. Mr. Ireland was in favour of this, but Mr. Furness staid that if the lamp was removed to the spot proposed the rays of the lamp would not reach far enough down the avenue to he of any use.</p>
        <p>a - .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>9UGaESTED TELEPHONE.</head>
        <p>THE 9UGaESTED TELEPHONE.</p>
        <p>The Clerk read the following letter which he bad written to the Postmaster at Warrington, dated 28th October.— bear ir,—At a meeting of the Parish Council on Monday inquiries were made as to whether the Postal authorities eould be induced to arrange for a telephone service at</p>
        <p>Great Sankey Post Office. At present it is necessary to take telegrams either to Warrington or Penketb,botn being a considerable distance away and a telephone service is considered very advisable. It is suggested that a telephone would result in an increased number of telegrams, also they would be able to deal at once with urgent business such as fire, medical aid, ete., etc. We shall be pleased to hear tbat the request has been favourably considered."—Continuing he said that he had received a reply to the above letter saying that the matter would receive attention.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>MOTOR SIGNALS.</head>
        <p>MOTOR SIGNALS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Furness said that the District Council were in possession of the warning signals, and in the course of a week or ten days they would be placed before the school. The signals will hear the word "school." He thought that would meet the difficulty. Mr. Ireland said that the signals would give the police better facilities for capturing the motorists, as they would have no excuse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>DANGEROUS CORNER.</head>
        <p>A DANGEROUS CORNER.</p>
        <p>With reference to the dangerous corner near Mr. Ashcroft'i Farm, Mr. Furness 'aid that Mr. Newcombe, the district surveyor, had ihstructed the tenants in the vicinity to have their hedges cut. The Chairman explained that the corner was dangerous in itself, and was made worse by high hedges on each side of the road, which prevented drivers of vehicles seeing one another approaching.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>NAMING OF ROADS.</head>
        <p>NAMING OF ROADS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Furness reported that the new road from the church to Miss Bolton's house bad been named St. Mary's-road. and two name plates had been erected accordingly. , A discussion then arose as to the naming of the road, which led from Penk&amp;th Lane Ends to Mr. H. Fair. dough's Farm. The Clerk said that "Sycamore " would he appro- priate, as Charles Peace once stayed at Mr. Fair- dough's Farm, which was then known as Sycamore Farm. After some discussion, Mr. Furness said be would make the following recommendations to the District Council :—That the road leading past Mr. Fairclougb's Farm shall be named Svcamore-road • that Barrow Hall road shall also be named: that toe lane eading from Liverpool road' to Lingley Green be named Lingley road; and that Back-lane shall be named Park-road. Moore.</p>
        <p>RUMOURED DROWNING CASE.—At the end of last Vieek it was rumoured that the body of a young game- keeper, named Ernest Long. who resides in Moore, had been taken from the Ship Canal at Walton. Strangely this rumour spread rapidly over the district and was generally believed to he true. However, fortunately this is not so, Mr. Long is still persuing his employ- ment in the best of health. BILLIARD MATCH.—On Thursday evening the Stock- ton Heath Reserve.team paid a return visit to Moore. he match was played in the Church Institute and resulted in a win for the home team. scores :</p>
        <p>bi 00 RE. STOCKTON HEATH. R. Gandy F. Antrobus J. Oultcri W. Beddall B2 J. Peal son S. Basnett 5B R. Jadcson ......... ...... 100 E. Cartwright</p>
        <p>WESLEY GUILD.—The monthly Devotional Meeting was held in the rchoolroom, on Wednesday evening last. Mr. Meldrnm presided and Dr. Bennett of Padgate gave a paper on "The history of famous hymns from the earliest times to the Reformation." Dr. Bennett mid it was the thought that there aas some- thing to be learned from the study of the hymns, which led him to take up the study, and to judge from the very interesting paper which he read, there is very much to be learned. The history of famous hymns is so intimately connected with the lives of their authers that the two 1 ecome inseparable, which makes the subject doubly interesting. It is a surprising fact that many of our oldest and most well-known hymns were written in monasteries ; this leads us to think that the monks must have Leen of a higher and more saintly type than we generally believe. At an interval in the paper the beautiful hymn "Holy Ghost, my comforter," was sung as a quartet; this hymn was written about 900 years ago by one of the kings of Prance. At the close a very hearty vote of thanks to Dr. Bennett was proposed by Mr. Lawton and seconded by Mr. Davies. The closing hymn was "The day is past and over" which was written by St. Anatolrous, Bishop of Constantinople at a time when the Christians were suffering great persecution at the hands of the Turks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Lyinm</head>
        <p>Lyinm</p>
        <p>LYMM CONGREGATIONAL GUILD.—The third nit eting of the Lymm Congregational Guild took place on Thursday evening, when a very interesting discussion took place on "Women's Suffrage." The discussion was opened by Mr. J. D. B. Smith in favour of the move- ment. This was replied to by Messrs. J. H. Holt, J. E. Bell und G. Willan against the movement. The dis- cussions were thoroughly enjoyed by the audience especially the ladies ,there being about five times more ladies than gentlemen which showed they took a gre interest in their own aff ars. At the close of the meet- ing the Rev. A. Barrett put a resolution forward to the audience in favour of Women's Suffrage which ended In a vote of 33 to 5 against the movement. This can- clued a very enjoyable evenfcg at which a very good company bad assembled. These meetings cont!nue to be well attended and of a helpful character.</p>
        <p>INDEPENDENT METHODIST CHURCH, CHERRY-LANE. The harvest thanksgiving services were held on Sunday last in the above church. The church was very nicely decorated and was quite a credit to the ladies and gentlemen who helped to do this work. The preacher for the morning was Mr. William Mort of Lymrn, his text being taken from Psalms ctv. 24." 0 Lout bow manifold are Ihy works, in wisdom hest Thou made them all ; the earth is full of Thy riches." His sermon was much appreciated by those present. At the evening service Mr. Robert Henshall of Latchford preached, and his text was taken from Isa all ix. 3. "Thou bast multi- plied the uation.and not increased the joy: they joy before Thee according to the joy in harvest, and as men rejoice when they divide the spoil." This sermon also appealed strongly to the hearers, it being in quite a different style trom the one preached in the morning. Very excellent congregation attended these services and the collections were in every way good. Miss M. Green- man presided at the organ during the day's services.— On Monthy evening the choir from Brick-street Mission, Warrington, rendered a service of song entitled "A Harvest of Sorrow," which was much enjoyed by the audience present, and was quite a credit to the friends who gave the same. Afterwards the fruit, vegetables, &amp;c.. were sold, the proceeds being very satisfactory, and were given in aid of the church fund. LECTURE ON THE HOUSE OF LORDS.</p>
        <p>A meeting under the auspices of the Lymm Liberal Association, was held in Hirst's Pavilion on Tuesday night, the occasion being it lecture, delivered by Mr. G. R. Allen (of the 1895 Club), Manchester, entitled "House of Lords." Mr. H. H. blotobed presided. The Chairman said that they bad a number of the most earnest workers for Liberalism there that night and Mr. Allen would as soon speak to the workers as to the rank and file, and anything he said that night about the House of Lords would be of great service to them in the future. Mr. Allen was a member of the 1895 Club, which was a hive of industry in political work. They were doing most excellent work. They (the Liberal Association) had no less than three gentlemen of the 1895 Club to address them et different times. The subject of the lecture that night was the most appropriate one that could possibly be brought before their notice. With regird to the Budget, he said one hardly knew whether it was well for the Lords to pass the Budget. (A voice: "Certainly.") From a tactical point, be hoped the House of Lords would throw the Budget out and then they could go for them. (Applause), Concluding he said they always looked to the House of Lords to cheek the progress of schemes for the betterment of the people. Let the House of Lords throw the Budget out and then they would be ready to meet them. Mr. Allen, who gave a lengthy and eloquent address, said that after all they bad heard of the Helve of Lords it would be better, with the near approach of" Guy Fawkes" day to blow that institution to smithereens. (Laughter.) The speaker then outlined the early history of the House of Lords. In referring to the hereditary right of the House of Lords, be stated that all sons of peers were allowed to sit in the Lords, and ho strongly condemned the hereditary principle. hi explaining the functions of the Lords he said they had a right to criti- cise any measure put forward by the House of Commons. F bey could not, however, deal with anything relating to the Army or Navy and they could not interfere with matters of finance. (Applause.) They could stop the traffic going over Westminster Bridge, but they had no say in the matter in allowing men from other countries to fill up the places of our own workmen while they were on strike. Neither could they prevent the measure of the Old Age Pensions. (Applau.e). They could pre- vent the granting of Home Rule for Ireland, but not the granting of the same for South Africa. With refer- ence to the snpren e power and the House of Commans he said that Pitt,Wellington,Lord Rosebery and Gladstone, and at one time even Mr. Balfour supported it. With regard to the repeated rejections of measures by the House of Lords, Mr. Allen stated that Sir Henry Campbell- Bannerman passed a resolution which said that if a measure was rejected by the Lords, it should go back to the House of Commune. A conference woula be appointed. After certain amendments the Bill would go to the House of Lord,' and so on until the third rejection, and then the Bill could be passed over the heads of the Lords without them having any say in the matter. In conclusion he asked them to support the present Government in the passing of the Budget, which he believed the Lords would attempt to reject. (Applause). A question was asked if it would not be better if after a measure had been rejected by the Lords, to take it before the King and let him deal with it, and then they could see which side the King was on. Mr. Allen in reply said it would he a bad day for the country if the King took part in part politics. The King was supposed to be non-political. It would be deplorable if he interfered with politics. (Hear, hear). Mr. O. G. Smith proposed a vote of thanks to the speaker and Mr. Brooks seconded. Mr. Davies supported and it was carried,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Padgate.</head>
        <p>Padgate.</p>
        <p>WESLEY GUILD.—The monthly devotional meeting of the Wesley Guild was held on Monday evening last, when a paper on the" Vision of Dante" was given by Miss Emily Bennett, of Padgate. Miss Bennett gave a descriptive account of the vision, and at the end of the paper was very loudly applauded. The interest- ing naLure of the subject was marked by the unusually large number of members present, the schoolroom being racked. Mr. F. Wright presided, and Miss Edith Bennett officiated at the piano. Miss Bennett also give a very interesting address to the members of the Junior Guild on Wednesday evening last on "Christian Heroines and Hymn Writers."</p>
        <p>THE PRIMROSE LEAGI:E.—Last Friday evening a well-attended meeting of the Padgate Habitation of the Primrose League, took place in the Padgate C.E. School, Alderman Hennett presided, and spoke of the political situation of the moment. He said that the Bermondsey election of the previous day was a plain omen of the trend of the people towards Tariff Reform, and he felt sure that in the coming general election in would be found that the people of Great Britain and Ireland would send a majority of members pledged to throw someof the burden of taxation on the foreigners, who for so many years, had been enriching themselves by our free markets. (Applause.) Lord Wolmer blamed the Budget for being the cause of diuch of the existing unemployment. The second part of the even- ing was of a social character. Miss M. Warren rendered songs in an excellent manner,afte(which Mr.H.M.Ashton created much amusement by his fttnorous recitations. The party then separated into two portions. One enjoyed a whist drive in the Infants' school, under the segis of Messrs. James Warburton. H. Houghton and A.O. Hilditch. The prive-winners were :—Ladies : 1, Miss Bowering ; 2. Mrs. Starkey. Gentlemen: 1, Mr. Starkey; 2, Mr. C. F. Chambers. The other party indulged in a dance, Messrs. P. Royston, A. Platt and Brimelow acting as M.C.'s, the music being supplied by Mr. W. Wainwright.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>WIDNES LIBERAL GATHERING. MR. MAX MUSPRATT ON THE POLITICAL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>SIIUATION.</head>
        <p>SIIUATION.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Widnes Liberal Club was held on fuesday eveuing, Mr. Max Muspratt, J.P., C.C.. presiding. The financial affairs of the new club, remarked the Chairman, showed receipts of £383, expenditure £425, and a debit balance of £42. On the year's working they had lost £2O. That was somewhat ' satisfactory on the first year's working. It only showed that there was a demand for a club in Widnes. The membership had gone up from 64 to 162. If any- thing like the progress was maintained during the yoming ,ear, the little problem of a loss of £2O would be easily solved. Dealing with the political aspect of the country, Mr. Muspratt said that at a time like this one ought to make some exception. He assured them that he was absolutely sick of speaking. lie felt stale after a very heavy summer, and he was proud of the splendid meeting held recently, which struck the finest political note of Widnes politics. They were living in very critical times. They had throughout the country accepted the great privileges of representative government, and they were in the course of a few weeks to be face to face with a great fight, with which those liberties were won. Liberties could be lost as sell es gained, and the party and nation which failed to maintain its liberties was on the down grade, which, be ventured to say. was disastrons in every repect. For nearly 300 years the House of Lords had not ventured to interfere with a Finance Bill, but they were now going to take the tremendous responsibility of so doing. That meant a great deal more than the rejection of taxes. It meaut the scrapping of the whole Constitution ; it meant revolution ; it meant that no Liberal Government, no other Govern- ment but a Conservative one, could take power in this realm. It also meant that they would wive to revert to the Austen Chamberlains and the Bonar Laws and the Balfours, who muddled them through ten of the blackest years of our history. 'they were going to put 'up these as despots to rule us. He ventured to say that whatever individual opinions might be on the rights and the wrongs of the differences between Liberals and Conservatives, that on this enormous issue, which struck at the root of the Constitution and the very foundation of our country,Widnes at any rate will be upon the side of progress, self-respect, and democracy. (Applause). Mr. Max Muspratt was re-elected president, Mr. H. Wade Deacon, Mr. J. B. Aitken, Mr. J. B. Orr, M. V. ' C. Driffield, Councillor Lewis, Mr. James Kiddie, Mr. John Caldwell, and Mr. T. Cosier, vice-presidents ; Mr. .7. Sadler, chairman; Mr. H. Mercer, treasurer; Mr. A. F. Martindale, secretary. The following were elected on the committee :—Messrs. J. T. Wright, P. Quinn, J. Travers, W.Colquitt, J. Sproston, F. Brook, E. Jolley, :nid W. Twiss. Votes of thanks closed the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>FAILURE OF A GROCER.</p>
        <p>William Owens, grocer, 29, Sankey-street, Earlestown, appeared for his public examination in bankruptcy, at the Warrington Bankruntcy Court, before Mr. T. J. Ridgway, yesterday. Mr. I.7nsworth represented the debtor. His liabilities were £338 9s. 4d. and assets £l9 58., leaving a deficiency of £3lB 14s. 4d. He attri- buted the cause of his failure to bad debts, lack of capital and competition principally by the Co-operative Society. The examination closed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>tHEFT FROM AN EARLESTOWN LODGING HOUSE. •</head>
        <p>tHEFT FROM AN EARLESTOWN LODGING HOUSE. •</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Petty Sessions held oil Wednesday, James Gittens and Edward Singleton, were charged with stealing from the lodging-house, 13, Fairclough-street, Earlestown, the previous day, a quantity of combs, brooches, scissors, pens, spectacles' Scc., value £2, the property of John Hunter, pedlar. The prosecutor and prisoners were all staying at the lodging bouse.—John Hunter said he was a licensed pedlar and had lived at the lodging house for some days. The nag which contained the stolen goods vkas his. Tbe previous night be went to bed about 10 o'clock. Ho was aroused at 11 o'clock. He found his bag was almost empty and was cut away at one side, whereas it was perfect when he went to bed. He estimated the damage to the stock, &amp;c., was .22.—David Forshaw, a "deputy "at the lodging-house said that at 10.10 p tn. the previous night he went into the kitchen of the lodging-house and there saw Singleton, who was alone. On looking around the kitchen he saw the goods on the fire, He found the bag in the cupboard upside down and a number of brooches on a spare bed in Singleton's room. Singleton did not sleep in the same room as Gittens.—P.C. Mullin said that at eleven o'clock on the night in question be received information of the robbery, and made inquiries. He went to the lodging- holise and found the bag which had been torn open, and the goods all burning on the fire. From what he got to know he took both prisoners to the police station. While there Singleton said "I have been a fool, Gittens forced the bag open with a poker. He gave me some of the stuff, it is on the bed in my room." Witness searched the bed and found seven brooches under the mattresses. On charging the prisoners they wade no reply —The Chairman (Mr. C. White) : Gittens and Singleton we are quite satisfied that you did this robbery, you will both be imprisoned for one month each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>NEWTON URBAN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>NEWTON URBAN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Thd monthly meeting of the Newton Council was held at the Rarlestown Town Hall on Monday after- noon. Mr. W. Thompson presided, and there were also present :—Messrs. C. B. F. Borron, E. T. Evaus, T. Ritebie, C. W. Emmett, .1. Naylor,E. L. Bent, T. Davio, M. W. Seton, .1. Sankey, C. Cole (clerk) Surtees (water engineer), and Bowes (purveyor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>TECHNICAL SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>THE TECHNICAL SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Education Committee a letter was read from the Surveyor stating that he had examined the plan of the proposed site as delineated on the draft lease ' • that the plan agreed with the one submitted to the County Archetect in Sept° mber. 1906;. that the plans for the proposed Technical School showed a building which could be erected on the site and still leave uncovared a depth of about 37ft. of land at the rear of th 9 school : that the exist- ing Public Library (as meashred on the ground) was set back 15ft:3in. from Crow-lane ; and that the plans of the Techuical School showed such building to be set back a similar distance from Crow-lane. It was resolved that the draft lease then submitted, be approved. At a further meeting of the Committee a letter was submitted from the Public Works Loan Board stating that, having considered tbe Council's application for the loan of £lO,OOO for the erection of the Tecanical School, they had decided to grant the appli(ation, such loan to be repaid in thirt) years by equal haltyearly instalments of principal with interest at 31 per. cent. per annum on , the principal sum from time to time remaining unpaid, subject to their solicitors being satisfied that the requirements of the Board and the Acts of Parliament applicable to the case had been sufficiently complied with.—The minutes were approved on the motion of Mr. Borron. MEDICAL OFFICER'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Medical Officer of Health, in his quarterly report to the Sanitary Committee, stated that the population, estimated to the middle of the year (1909) was 20,714. There were registered during the quarter, 118 births and GO deaths, giving r natural increase of 58 for the quarter. During the quarter 60 deaths were registered (in the district), 34 males and 26 females. Tire public health had been very good during the quarter. The epidemic of scarlet fever which existed in the first and racond quarters practically died out before the present quarter commenced—only eleven cases have occurred—most of them being sporadic cases. The rates of the quarter had on the whole been satistactory. The infantile death-rate was apparently not so, but it must be remembered that that was taken in proportion to 1,600 births and not to the estimated population.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>BRUTAL BITING IN A FIGHT.</p>
        <p>At St. Helens on Monday, Martin Quinn, a' middle- aged man, who bad been remanded a week on a charee of wounding 'Peter Fulham, a fellow lodger, of 66, Pcke Nook-street, was dealt with on a charge or comm *n assAult. Quinn pleaded not guilty, and Fulham said that at about half-past eight on Saturday night, October 23, Quinn was using bad language at his lodgings. Fulharn remonstrated with him and they began to fight. After a severe scuffle Quinn bit Fuinarn under the left arm-pit, inflicting a strious wound. Dr. Davies said that Fulham a wound siin. long which opened up the auxilliary space. He had to put nine stitches in it. The man was also knocked about in other ways. He had examined a chair with hnrnan hair adhering, but the hair was not similar to Fulham's. It was s " fake" to excuse Quinn. He bad examined Quinn's mouth, and the absentee of certain teeth from his jaw accounted for the peculiar shape of the wound. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mrs. Fulham came forward to give evidence against her husband, and in excuse of Quinn. Her husband and this man fought every Saturday night, but it was on account of Fulham's nasty temper. She was afraid of every week-end coming round. _ _</p>
        <p>Quinn said he and Fn.lham were splendid Mends when sober, but they .always fought every time they got a drop of drink. The bench sent Quinn to prison for two months with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>ST. HELENS MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS MUNICIPAL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>• Four quiet but strenuous contests were fought at St. Helens between the Labour and Conservative parties on Monday. Several years have elapsed since an official Liberal candidate offered himself at the municipal elections, and the Tories had been allowed to secure an overwhelming preponderance in the Council. Local issues and general labour questions were the cries relied on by the candidates, and all the Conservative candidates appealed to the electors to "keep out the Socialists." The votes were counted at the Town Hall, where a crowd of about 6,000 assembled. The results slowed a Labour gain in East Sutton by the narrow majority of 6. South Windie Ward.—J. A. Baron (C), 745; E. Blease (Lab.), 346. Majority, 339. East Sutton Ward.—B. Twist (Lab.), 797; S. Boyle (C), 791. Majority 6. Labour gain. West Sutton Ward.—J. Grace (C), 678 ; A. Rudd (Lab.), 557. Majority, 121. Parr Ward.,-- T. Hamlett (Lab.), 818; R. Woodward (C), 775. Majority, 73. The candidates returned thanks' Councillor Baron stating that in South Windle it had been a clean and fair fight, and his opponent had behaved as a perfect gentleman. Mr. Blease said they had had to fight ignorance, prejudice, beer, and religious bigotry. He hoped the workers would organise, and give Mr. Peter Phy titian the biggest majority on record nest year. ' Cbuncillor Twist said he had nothing to complain of in his opponent, and it had been the cleangst fight that had ever taken place in East Sutton. Mr. Boyle thanked his opponent for his courtesy. He had succeeded three times, and had only been beaten bpsix, and he knew that changes came about at some time or other.</p>
        <p>Councillor Grace thanked his workers and the electors for his splendid majority. They had shown their confidence in him, and he would do in the future, as in the past, his best tor all sections of the ward. Mr. Rudd said he was disappointed, but if he had strength he would flpht until he found his way back to the Council Chamber. _</p>
        <p>Councillor Hamblett thanked his workers for the rematkable manner in which they had rallied round him. His opponents thought last year that they had put an end to him, and that he was dead and buried; but a ghost had appeared in materialised form in Pam It had been freely rumoured that his opponents were prepared to spend £l,OOO to keep him out of the Council, and all he could say was that he hoped that the people responsible tor that expenditure would pay their bills with a better grace than the lords and dukes were pa log for the Dreadnoughts. Mr. Woodward said that if he had been given another opportunity of serving his Ward in the Council the electors would not have regretted it. He thanked all Lis workers.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was passed to Alderman Foote, Mayor, who said that his advice was, to the winners "don't crow too much," to thA losers " Buck up," aud to all of them " Go borne." They bad bad a straight fight and a good fight, and the sooner they got home the better.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>INFANTILE PARALYSIS, Weakness and Mal-nutrition.</head>
        <p>INFANTILE PARALYSIS, Weakness and Mal-nutrition.</p>
        <p>A safe and perfect cure in DR. CASSELL'S TABLETS</p>
        <p>(1) Does your child thrive as he should do ? (2) Does he walk ,aith difficulty? (3) Are the limbs crooked or thin? 14) Is he wasting or badly developed? (5) Is he ricketty or suffering from muscular weakness?</p>
        <p>(6) Is he nervous, irritable and sleeping badly? (7) Has he any twitching of the limos? (8) Is his appetite poor or capricious? If your child is in any way weak, nervous, debilitated or suffering from any form of nerve and physical exhaustion a course of Dr. Cassell's Tablets will speedily put matters right. This great remedy of world-wide repute is pure, reliable and safe for the youngest child and contains just what is necessary to restore worn out or weakened tissues and build up a healthy con- stitution. Doctors, scientists and the public gecerally are everywhere testifying to the exttaordinery qualities of Dr. Cassell's Tablets, and it only remains for you to sive them a trial, end convince yourself of their efficacy. Dr. Cassell's Tablets cost 100., Is. id. &amp; 2s. 9d. of all chemists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>WARRINGTON FIELD CLUB.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON FIELD CLUB.</p>
        <p>The excellent work of this organization was well illivstrated at an exhibition meeting held at the Art Gallery OD Saturday evening and attended by a large. and representative gathering, including the Mayor and Mayoress, a number of members of the Town Councif and several well-known scientists from other towns. The members of the club are to be congratulated upon having got together such a splendid collection of specimens in various branches of science. It was a tribute alike to their knowledge, patience and skill, and the visitors spent a pleasant and profitable evening in inspecting the great variety of exhibits which would have done credit to a much large.. town than Warrington. -Geologists were attracted by the fine series of fossil sea-urchins and their allies, exhibited by Mr. Leslie. The same gentleman also showed a large number of flint tools and weapons, the handiwork of our far-off ancesters in palmolithic or in later neolithic times. Botanists could admire the display of Messrs. Milne, Fuller and Dunlop. These included cultivtfted cereals, wild flowers and a fine series of specimens showing the great range of variation in the foliage of oak trees. Mr. Hamblett exhibited fishes; Mr. Greening a large series of skins of British birds, including some very fine specimens of sea birds and of birds of prey • and Mr. F. R. Jolley eggs and nests of birds, mostly focal, this collection being particularly rich in buntings and warblers. Some very rare mammals, including a melanin specimen of the Orkney vole, were shown by Mr. G. Ellison, of Liverpool; whilst by means of trans- parencies and by rni i zroscopes which were under the care of Messrs. W. H. Woodcock, H. Womersley and J. Smith, many of the smaller forms of life were sub- mitted to ttie gaze of visitors. In the entomological department, for which the club has a particularly high reputation, a floe series of rare and interesting species of beetles, contributed by Messrs'. J. F. Dutton, Armstrong and Collins, was a prominent feature. There were also large and eautilul displays of lepidoptera shown by Messrs. F. Womersley, W. Mounfield and J. Robinson. Mr. Mounfield, in addition t9e his long series of moths, also showed a number of insects mounted in special small frames between sheets of glass, so that they can he handed round a lecture or class room, both sides of the insect being clearly seen, whilst its most delicate scales and hairs are protected from harm. In such frames, be showed several West African beetles, includ- ing two very fine specimens of the Goliath beetle, giants of the insect world : a gorgeously coloured Madagascar moth; some British moths with the scales removed from the wings of ODD side so as to show the structure and venation of the wing itself ; and a splendid series of Sirex gigas, a somewhat wasp-like saw-fly, whose larvw tunnel the timber of various coni humus trees.</p>
        <p>Of general interest were the beautiful and accurate pen and ink dr.twings of local plants, as by Mr. Fuller, jun., whilst the general grouping, which was highly effective, was due to 111 r. Madeley and his capable assistant in the Mnseum department, Mr. G. A. Dunlop. During the evening refreshments vtcro served in the lecture room and thanks were freely expressed to Ur. Fairhurst—who had charge of this seed( n—and to Mrs. Powys and her staff working under his direction.</p>
        <p>EHHIBITION OF ROOTS AT CHESTER.</p>
        <p>An ibteresting exhibit of roots organised by Messrs. W. R. Smith and Son attenball-road Works, near Chester, was held on Saturday, wh.n bendsome silver cups and large money prizes were offered to this enter- prising tirri,'s customers. These were divided into to class for those who farm south of Shrew shiny and those wto farm north of that town. In the competitions for the southern district Mr. E. F. Bellamy, of Newent, Gloucestershire, was fortunate in winning a handsome silver cup and a large money prize, whilst Mr. Thomas Shepherd of Burton, Denbighshire, was fortunate in winning that for ,northern counties. Great interest was taken in the division of £5O in cash awarded by 'Messrs. Smith and Son, and those who had fulilled the conditions of the competition received back.in money half their .manure account.</p>
