        <p>Latchford Election.</p>
        <p>111, Winwick-road. Ward Sec. to Mr. Hewitt</p>
        <p>Why Mr. Hewitt was so badly Defeated.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l should thank you if you would publish the fallowing remarks on why the Latchford Election majority was sO large. 1. A shameless rumour was set out by some low opponents of Mr. Hewitt that he had allowed both his father and mother to go to the Workhouse and die there. It spread rapidly, and did a great deal of harm to Mr. Hewitt and family. There is not the slightest ground for such a rumour, as both of Mr. Hewitt's parents died at his on house and were Luried from there. Mr. Hewitt had both his father and mother living with him for a great number of years, and were well cared for. _ _</p>
        <p>2. It was also said that Mr. Hewitt did not nay proper wages to his workmen. That also is a lie, 'but it gained Mr. Wilson many votes. Had not these false rumours been spread about the result might have been quite different.—Thanking you in anticipation for publishing this letter,l am yours respectfully, , J. WOODS.</p>
