      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>Warrington Fire Brigade.</head>
        <p>Warrington Fire Brigade.</p>
        <p>Sir,—My attention has been called to a paragraph contained in your report of last week's Council meet- ing, upon the above subject, I wonder the Brigade do not feel annoyed at the remarks made. If the statement that the Brigade were seven-and-a-half minutes in turning out to a fire only about 500 yards distant from the Fire Station is at all correct, it does not reflect much credit on the Fire I3rigade, but only serves to show the need of improvement. I am not a stranger to Fire Brigade work by any means, and I have no wish to east any slur on the Warrington Brigade but I think it would be far wiser if instead of taking the steamer out first to all town fires the rule of other brigades in a like position as Warrington were to he followed, i.e. the hose cart or reel to turn out FIRST in the ease of" day fires," and in the case of an alarm during the night the currical fire escape to turn out first, followed immediately by the hose cart, and only six men to turn out with each machine, the rest being left at the station to guard against an alarm coming from another qua tter. If, after the hose cart had reached the scene of the fire and found it was beyond their power, it would be an easy matter to telephone for the steamer, which of course would be kept in readiness. I make the suggestion for what it is worth, and would not for one moment think of saying or writing anything that may have a tendency to discourage the members of the brigade.—Yours faithfully, AN OLD FIREMAN. Warrington. November 3rd, 1900.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>SKATING RINK ACCIDENT.</head>
        <p>SKATING RINK ACCIDENT.</p>
        <p>County Court Sequel.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before Deputy Judge Sellers, Howard Moston' boilermaker, Manchester, brought a claim under the Employers Liability Act, for damages against Messrs. Forshaw, Ltd., brass founders and engineers, Mersey-street, Warrington, for injuries through falling from a ladder. Mr. Adshead Elliott appeared for the plaintiff and Mr. J. Greaves Lord for the defendants. Mr. Elliott said that in June of this year the defend- ants contracted to construct and fix steel work at the Empire Skating Rine, Warrington. About the end of June, plaintiff and six labourers were put under foreman Massey to do the work. If the job were not finished by August Bth, Messrs. Forshaw were under an obligation to pay £1 a day until it was completed. The tackle was taken to the job by Messrs. Forshaw's labourers, and consisted of ropes, chains blocks and two ladders. The ladders were 191 t. and Aft. long. It was the duty of the foreman to see that the tackle was in a proper condition. OU the 29th July the ladders were spliced together. On that date complainant was on the 4th stave from the top and was actually engaged in making a hole for an iron stave. Whilst he was doing that the ladder broke and down be came. It wasa very old and defective ladder. The ladder was cracked. Both of the ladders in parts had been repaired. One of them broke in the previous March, and their expert would say that it ought to he sawn off where it had broken. Plaintiff was the only man on the ladder and he did not ask any man to follow him. Plaintiff heard two creaks and then he came down and fell on his back, and he was taken to the Warrington Infirmary. After he had been attended to he went home to Manchester, and on August 2nd be went under Dr. Yates and had been attended by him since. He claimed the utmost that was claimable—three years' wages. He earned about £2 a week and since the accident he had lost £2B in wages. He bad been put to extra expense, and there was a prospect also that he would lose several more weeks wages. Even if he did get light work he would not be able to earn £2 a week. The plaintiff was called and bore out Mr. Elliott's statement as to the circumstances of the accident. He stated that since the accident he had suffered from pains in the head and back, and he slept irregularly. It pained him when he stooped. He never gave any orders for a second man to come up the ladder. He could not say whether there was a second man on the ladder at the time of the accident. He thought the laddeg was seven feet from the wall. By Mr. Greaves Lord: He worked under Mr. Massey, but he vies the leading man on the job. He did not 'remember one of the men named Jones throwing him a rope and on plaintiff failing to catch it Jones coming up tbeladder. He was told that if the job was done within the time contracted there mould be a bonus. The two ladders were the best in Messrs. Forshaw's works. He once made a complaint about one of tbe ladders and it received attention.</p>
        <p>• Thomas Mawdsley, builder and general contractor, Manchester, gave evidence to the effect that the ladders bore signs of old defects. He ccnsidered they were not suitable for the purpose for which they had been used. Dr. Yates, of Gorton, said plaintiff came to see him on Monday, August 2nd. He had sustained a wound on the top of the head about half an inch in length and one over the left eye an inch in length. The latter wound had three stitches in it. He complained of having been injured in the back. About a week after the accident witness made a thorough examination and found an injury on the hip. On October 17th he again examined plaintiff and on testing him found he could not bend, stoop or lift things properly owing, to pains in the hack. Plaintiff was only fit to do light work. Mr. Lord submitted that the answers given by the plaintiff had put him out of Court. 'Plaintiff had been negligent in using the ladder at an unsafe angle, the base of the ladder being too far from the wall. Dr. Sankey, surgeon for the Maochester Policp, said he examined the plaintiff on October 9. He found him suffering from a scalp wound and another wound on the forehead over the left eye. He deducted that there were no symptoms which were consistent with any brain injury having occurred.—ln reply to Mr. Elliott witness admitted that there might have been an injury to the lower part of the spine. He agreed with D. Yates that the pains in the back were due to the acci- dent. It would not be advisable for the man in his present condition to attempt to lift heavy weights on account of the sprained joint. Samuel Massey, foreman boilermaker in the employ of defendants, said that the bricks were about 16ft. from the wall. It was a good ladder, and was bought secondhand four or five years ago. Benjamin Makin, in the employ of defendants, said he spliced the ladders. He thought the position of the ladder was dangerous, but he did not tell plaintiff. Harry Jones, labourer in the employ of the defend- ants, said it was about 11ft. away from the wall. Plaintiff wanted a piece of wood, so another man named Hyndes threw it up to him, but as he did not catch it he commenced to take it to him, but when he got half way up the ladder broke. Arthur Edwin Boardmen, managing director of defendant, company said that be saw the place of the accident the following day and the bricks would be quite 15 or 16 feet away from the wall. It was absolutely unsafe to work upon a ladder 16 feet away. Daniel Cooper, builder of the Empire Skating R'nk said that some bricks were put there between the 29th and 30th of July. The bricks would be 12 or 13 feet away from the wall. Samuel Pickton, wheelwright and carpenter said that the ladders were good ones and were quite safe. Thomas Massey, ladder maker said that the ladder would have been safe at a narrow angle, but not at a wide one. NOM His Honour having inspected the ladder held that the accident was not due to a defect in the ladder, but due to it being improperly used. Mr. Elliott opposed Mr. Lord's application for costs being taken out of the compensation. Mr. Lord in reply said it was a case in which there was no hardship as the man had not fought that aCtion singly but was backed up by a trade union. His Honour gave judgment for the defendant with costs under scale C. and allowed a qualifying fee for two experts and a doctor.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Mr, CROSFIELD AND THE PROSPECTS of an ELECTION.</head>
        <p>Mr, CROSFIELD AND THE PROSPECTS of an ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Interview with the Member Yesterdau.</p>
        <p>THE BUDGET'S CHANCES WITH THE LORDS.</p>
        <p>Points from the Great Debate.</p>
        <p>Mr. A. H. Cr,jsfield, M.P., was in Warrington yesterday, aud a representative of the " Examiner " took the opportunity of. ascertaining his impressions of the historic debate on the third reading of the Budget which concluded on Thursday evening, together with his views on the impending political crisis. Mr. Crosfield was privileged to hear the speeches of the Chancellor of the Exchequer, Mr. Balfour and the Prime Minister, which marked the foal stage of the discussion, and he readily consented to indicate their salient features and the effect pro- duced by them on the House. Referring first to the Chancellor of the Exchequer's brilliant speech on Thursday afternoon, Mr. Crosfield said everybody, Liberals and Conservatives alike, who beard Mr. Lloyd-George when he came to Warring- too, and when he was certainly in remarkably fine form, would Le struck then with two characteristics that could not fail to impress the listener—one his strong democratic sympathies and the other his remarkable dramatic faculty as a speaker, combined • with Celtic fire. Those same qualities were very noticeable in the speech which the Chancellor delivered on Thursday afternoon, and the vein of irony which ran through his remarks all the time he was quoting from the Conservative handbook was absolutely irresistible and inimitable, and simply, brought down the whole House.</p>
        <p>Mr. Balfour, continued Mr. Crosfield,.rose shortly after ten o'clock, but not without the House receiving a suitable warning, given with the Speaker's custom- ary weight and authority, that under no circumstances could the intervention by either the Prime Minister or Mr. Balfour a second time in the debate, with himself in the chair, he regarded as a precedent. "And what kind of an impression did Mr. Balfour's speech make on the House ? " asked the interviewer. "I am bound to say" replied Mr. Crosfield. "that it struck one as poor and thin and threadbare.Tb.at Mr. Balfour has had an anxions and worrying time of it we all know, and I think the House could not help noticing regretfully some traces of this as he spoke last night. For my part, I thought then, as I have thought often enough, that Mr. Balfour himself must almost regret at times that in the earlier stages of the • raging and tearing propaganda' he did not take his courage in both hands and make a firmer stand against those crude,ill-thought, impossib(e proposals."</p>
        <p>"So you think the Tariff Reformers did not have a good time of it in the debate?'</p>
        <p>"I don't want to exaggerate but I can only characterise the defence put up by mitics of the Budget—notably, of course, the Tariff Reformers in answer to our challenge to produce any intelligible sort of alternative sheme, as abject. Speaking of Mr. Balfour and this part of the discussion, it certainly has served to clear up one or two very important points for which we are not the least indebted to the really admirable speech delivered by the Lord Advocate on Wednesday evening, when that historic scene in which the Lord Advocate and Mr. Balfour were the chief actors, toook place." "What do you find to be the general opinion in the House on the Balfour—lire controversy ? " "Well it is quite open to suggest, as many members do, that Mr. Ure's criticisms in the country might with advantage have been put a little differently; if he had stated that ruling out food and raw materials, as a section of the Opposition professses to do, it would be absolutely impossible by the taxation of foreign manufactured articles to raise anything like the revenue necessary to pay old age pensions and therefore it would be equally impossible to pay for them unless by means of the very taxes which the Opposition were condentning in the most extravagant language and consistently opposing and voting against from start to finish. Admitting that there was room for some difference of opinion on the matter, the overwhelming feeling of the House at the same time was that there was no earthly excuse for the shamefully exaggerated censure received from Mr. Balfour. Mr. Ure is one of.the most popular members, 'a man who stands high with the whole House not merely because of his splendid ability and intellectual power but because of his fine character and transparent sincerity—in fact he is, every inch of him, a thorough gentleman."</p>
        <p>"So you don't think Mr. Balfour's arguments were effective?"</p>
        <p>"No. lam cne of those who have always admired not only Mr. Balfour's brilliant intellect but his charm of manner and attractive personality, but I must say his speech at the close of this great and protracted debate was woefully dislpp-inting and utterly inadequate to the occasion. This struck one all the more forcibly because of the contrast so noticeable when the Prime Minister rose immediately afterwards to conclude the debate. I have never known Mr. Asquith's powerful personality to be more strikingly and obviously felt in the House than it was in the magnificent speech be delivered last night. Power of compression one has recognized in other speeches and, again, there may and no doubt have been others with an equal • command of language. But I greatly doubt—and those experienced members of the House whom T have consulted en the matter are of the same opinion —whether we have ever had a states- man who possessed both gifts —the wonderful faculty for logical incisive, condensed and at the same time admirable language—to the same degree. I can entlorse what I see is reported in some of the papers to-day, that, the Leader of the Opposition himself obviously enjoyed the intellectual treat of so great a speech, delivered as it was with scarcely tirn e for a note of preparation. As .for the Liberal party, the way they responded to the speech and endorse d the most significant points in it—and some of them wer e very significant— was unmistakable."</p>
        <p>"And what has impressed you as the most striking feature of the whole debate ?"</p>
        <p>"First of all, the repeated exposure of the nostrums and the quackery of the tariff reform propaganda in a way that often made the House roar and even rock with laughter. I can scarcely call the defence of Protection put up in answer to all this pitiably weak, because really there was no defence at all, and the fact that the Leader of the Opposition, whose half- heartedness in the whole matter is much more than suspected, had not a word to say fn explanation, so that the Opposition are hopelessly at sixes and sevens on what is supposed to be the first great constructive part of their future policy, helps to make their plight more woeful. But speaking for the Liberal party, it certainly is significant to see the splendid unanimity with which every section of that party, and of the Labour party—every shade of 'pro-</p>
        <p>gressive opinion in the country, as represented in the House—stands, as one man, to beat down this oil and reactionary policy. And yet, powerful as was the demonstration in favour of Free Trade throughout the debate, the reception given to Mr. Asquith's statement that the House of Commons would maintain its historic right to control the finances of the nation was, to say the least, not less significant. Mr. Asquith at the time was speaking with measured gravity, but his declaration was instantly taken up by the whole Progressive party in a way that can never,. I think be forgotten by anyone present on that memorable night." And what is, then, the opinion as to the prospects of an election? "</p>
        <p>" Well, the general opinion certainly is that we are in for one and that it will be a fight such as the country has not seen for many a long day, with consequences a great deal more far-reaching than may be supposed at the moment by some of the more ignorant and more reckless spirits that tire rushing into. this tremendous national conflict. For my own part, knowing that in the Conservative party itself, and not alone in the House of Commons but equally in the House of Lords, a number of the more moderate and responsible statesmen are frimply furious at the idea of precipitating such a conflict, and at the same time inflicting such an injury on the finances of the country, I have all along believed that ultimately wiser counsels, from the point of view of that party, would prevail. But—and here I am speaking with very high authority indeed— I may quote what was said to me on that authority, namely, that at the present moment the political barometer stands at stormy. It oscillates from day to day and it was hinted to me from the same quarter that in another two or three weeks the political atmosphere might be clearer. Two things, however, are perfectly clear now. In the first place, if the Lords do dare to challenge us on the Budget, the whole Progres,ive Party will instantly accept that challenge and go to the country. Secondly, they will come back. The country will never stand this wanton and selfish action by the House of Lords. Their action strikes me as being no less foolish and short-sighted from their own point of view than it is selfish and shabby from ours. Shabby and selfish, it assuredly is, if one con- siders not merely that the Lords themselves, like other people, have insisted on the necessity of maintaining at a high standard, the defences of the nation and of the Empire, but that social reform in its many phases is a growing necessity for a vast mass of poorer folk in the nation. To my mind, this attempt to evade taxes which affect the wealthy members of the House of Lords,in order to add to the hardships of their less fortunate fellow-countrymen, is both mean and contemptible. It only remains to be 'seen whether the counsels of the more responsible and, I will add, mole public-spirited' men in the Tory party will yet ,prevail."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>MR. LORD AND TOWN HALL WARD ELECTION.</head>
        <p>MR. LORD AND TOWN HALL WARD ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Copy of Letter to COnservative Association. •</p>
        <p>To theY Secretary of the Warrington Consexvative Association.</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,—After the unfair and unconstitutional treatment meted out to me by the Warrington Conservative Association, of which body I have been a member for several years, I feel reluctantly compelled to sever my connection with the party which claims to be so honourable in all its dealings. I have always considered the Conservative Party in Warrington to be an honourable body. Does the way the party have treated me savour of honour? If in the opinion of the Association it does, then I have no desire to be any longer associated with it. Why was J not summoned to attend the meeting of the Town Hall Ward Committee (of which I was a member) when the candidate was being selected? Why was I not summoned to attend the meeting of the Association when the action of the Ward Committee (according to the constitution) came up for conformation? Was this an oversight, or was it deliberately done? If the latter, was it honourable treatment to mete out to one of its members?</p>
        <p>I need say nothing further beyond asking you to accept my resignation.—Yours truly, JOHN LORD,</p>
        <p>128. Bridge-street Warrington. November Bth, 1909. P.S.—I am sending a copy of this letter to the Warrington papers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>BOXING AT THE BARRACKS.</head>
        <p>BOXING AT THE BARRACKS.</p>
        <p>The splendid audience at the Orford Barracks Gymnasium on Thursday evening to witness the boxing tournament were thinking they w ere in for along night, but an unexpected turn of events brought the" show" to a close at the respectable hour of half-past ten.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>CHIEF EVENT.</head>
        <p>THE CHIEF EVENT.</p>
        <p>The chief event of the night wr-t.tlie grand ten-round contest between Corporal Tavlin (Warrington) of the and South Lancashire Regiment, and champion of [reload, and Jonty Heywood (Salford) for the club purse and side stake. When the contestants met. Tavlin was seen to be the bigger min and appeared in fine condition. Heywood seemed as if he was not going to give anything away. In the second round Tavlin pressed and Heywood received a deal of punishment and when the gong 'went both men were breathing heavily. In the second round, also the last, Tavlin scored heavily with a face blow which he quickly followed with a right from the shoulder, which landed on the" point." Heywood dropped like a log and was in danger of rolling under the ropes. He was counted out and Tavlin was proclaimed the winner amid wild cheers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>SPACIAL SIX ROCND CONTEST.</head>
        <p>SPACIAL SIX ROCND CONTEST.</p>
        <p>Much interest was centred in the above contest as Mat Tavlin a Warrington lad met Corp !al Brittan, light weight champion of Cork and Tipperary. Both men were of good phy., inn° but Tavlin bad an advantage with his long rest) In the first round both sparred merrily but Tavlin planted two staggering blows in quick successmn on his opponent's face and body. Brittan however, soon righted and came up smiling, but the gong went for time. The second round saw Tavlin lead off with a right punch from the shoulder on the face and before his opponent could recover he dealt him the knock-out on the jaw. Tavlin's victory was greeted with loud cheers.</p>
        <p>Another six round contest was contested between 11. Hockenhall (Warrington) gnd Young Burns (Widnes). The opening round wis rather sensational, for atter 'clinching" Hockenhall"broke away" and succeeded in planting a staggering blow on his opponent's face. Burns went to the ropes, quickly recovered, but Hockenhall gave him another blow on the same spot which looked like ending the battle. Burns again fell on the ropes but was encouraged by his seconds. Luckily the gong sounded and he was given time to collect his scattered senses.</p>
        <p>Nothing of importance took place in the other rounds, except a few erratic ltmo•bes and on the termina- tion of the last round, Hockenhall was proclaimed the winner of a good fight. Barns had to be medically attended to.</p>
        <p>-- - A good fight was witnessed in the six round contest between young Tighe (Widnes) and Peter Chadwick, (St. Helens). In the third round Chadwick gained an advantage by evading Tighe's defence and" planting" three heavy side blows. Tighe retaliated and with a strong blow knocked his adversary to the ropes. The fourth round saw Tighe make a sudden onslaught with an effective neck blow, which knocked Chadwick to the rcpes. Tighe was given first place on points.</p>
        <p>CLUB MIDDLE WEIGHT FINAL.</p>
        <p>_ A sensaticnsl ending was the result of this contest, as both contestants in the first round were cautioned for ineffective sparring. They continued in the same way and the referee sent them both out of tbe ring. A great uproar took place, but this was quietened by a senior officer saying that the referee was absolntely right in his decision. --</p>
        <p>A special si round contest between Morley Lee, and Phil Owen, both of Warrington, resulted in a victory for the first named on points.</p>
        <p>FE ATH ER WEIO HT COM PETITION.</p>
        <p>Eight men entered for this competition and Private Ellison and Private Wilson both of the 3rd. Kings met in the final. Wilson put up a good fight and was proclairool first. The referee was Mr. Tom Gamble, appointed by the "Sporting Chronicle" and Captain D. 0. B. Dawson, South Lancashire Regiment, timekeeper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
