      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>WORKMEN DIVERTING THE BROOK</head>
        <p>WORKMEN DIVERTING THE BROOK</p>
        <p>Those of our readers who have not recently visited the Ackers Mill Pool at Latchford, will find a surprising change if they walk round there now. The pool has been drained dry, and there are miniature railway lines and trucks running across its bed. Workmen are busy deepening and levelling the pool in furtherance of a scheme by which Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co. mean to provide open-air bathing and boating facilities. An "island" surmounted by shrubs has already been raised in the centre, and the scheme bids fair to make the pool an attractive place in the summer months. Our picture depicts workmen diverting the course of the brook, and the empty bed of the " pool" is discernible in the background.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>"UNEMPLOYMENT AND POVERTY. ' A Novel Remedial Scheme.</head>
        <p>"UNEMPLOYMENT AND POVERTY. ' A Novel Remedial Scheme.</p>
        <p>"Unemployment and poverty" was the subject of a lecture given at the Warrington Adult Scnool on Thursday evening by Mr. John A. J. Morton, President of the Right to Labour League. Mr. James Dodds presided over a good attendance and remarked that the subject efts probably the most important that could be brought forward. It was one of very great difficulty and complexity. Mr. MORTON raid he had endeavoured to deal with the problem on original lines, apart from any political prejudice. Poverty end unemployment were the outcome of the one cause—our chaotic industrial system —and if they were to tackle the c tuse they must tackle the system and not any particular symptoms of it. Mr. Morton proceeded to review the many efforts to deal with poverty by repeated attempts to improve the Poor Law. He contended tbat the report of the Poor Law Com- mission did not embody any new principle. The suggestion contained in his scheme had for its object to make society different from what had existed before and it introduced one or two innovations In working conditions. Work would be provided for those desiring it under certain conditions—thus constituting, within limitations, the right to labour. The conditions were that whoever applied would take up the work to which be was directed, that he would receive the wage that was being paid at that time and further—and that was probably the innovation that would alarm most people —that he would accept payment in the form of paper money which was considered to be the most convenient. The State that undertook this work would have to engage in the retail business, and the paper money would be exchangeable for goods at the State stores. The people employed under these conditions would not be placed in colonies and there would be no difference between the men employed under the new sche,ne and those who worked in private industry. They could not set up State industries of that kind, and offer an ideal wage which would induce men to leave their present employment and flock to the State works. At the outset the wage would not amount to more than 12s. 6d. a week, but that sum would only be an initial wage. As the labour became more efficient and produced a higher value per head, the wage would be correspondingly increased. There would be a central authority in London, and works would be established in various parts of the country under the management of committees. The index of supply and demand would always be at headquarters, and as the wage rose the State works would form a counterpoise to our present industrial system, and higher wages must inevitably follow in private undertakings. The scheme had not a Socialistic ideal In view. It) did not matter what was going to happen in the future so long as they were going to make things better for the present time. (Applause). Dr. GILBERT FOWLER, of Manchester, said there was a danger of the individual coming to trust too much to the State and of the many virtues of independ- ence, self-denial and self-control 'being lost sight of as the individual became forgetful that he was a member of the State and that be must do his own part. He (the speaker) liked Mr. Morton's scheme because, while the State must come in to help it, would not do so in such a way as to pauperise the individual. It would act as a buffer between the individual and destitution, and keep him afloat until by his own efforts he brought himself back into the community of independent men. The money which the State granted would be utilized to get the best value out of the somewhat inferior material they would have to deal with. The cardinal point of the scheme was the use of paper money. They must separate the State works from ordinary industry or they would immediately have them under-cutting and their last state would be worse than their first. On the other hand, if they paid the workers in ordinary coin of the realm more than was due to them, they knew what would be the result. They would create competition and that was what they wanted to get rid of. They wished to keep their normal industries as efficient as possible and get the best results from the State industries with the other class of material. One of the greatest causes of unemployment was the lack of training and he claimed that Mr. Morton's scheme would remedy that to a large extent. They would have a sufliaient variety of industries to keep a large number of different kinds of unemployed busy, and prevent them sinking into the ranks of the totally unemployable. The scheme was well worthy of the serious consideration of responsible people. Mr. J. UPSON said that as a people the thing we were most afraid of was mental effort. Most of us were mentally lazy and shrank from the mental effort required to understand a new scheme such as Mr. Morton had outlined. The scheme met with opposi- tion because people were prejudiced against it by their ignorance. It was not quite original. Years ago, in his introduction to "Unto this last," Ruskin advocated the establishment of national workshops to which men should be sent who were inefficient and unable to com- pete with others in the battle of life. Mr. Morton's scheme was largely to deel with ineflicients. We ought to experiment more than we did in schemes which were intended to improve the conditions of the people. He .believed that in America and on the Continent municipal and State experiments were very much more numerous than they were in this country. Mr. Morton's scheme might be tried in one county or in one large borough to see whether it would effectively answer its purpose. The scheme was not an end in itself but a means to an end. The inefficients would be taken into the State workshops, where they would be trained and become more or less efficient until they were attracted by private industries. We were spending immense sums of money which were absolutely unremunerative in the administration of the Poor Law. The expenditure was enormous and to a very considerable extent it was absolutely valueless. We were making no progress. The number of people receiving Poor Law relief did not decrease and it seemed to him that by Mr. Morton's scheme progress might be made in reducing the number of people who were continually coming upon the rates. He considered the scheme was exceedingly practicable. 'lt was help- ful in that it presented the opportunity and possibility by which the pauper and the inefficient might become a productive instrument in human society. We had passed the stage where the relief of distress and the ameliorative treatment of the wastrels of our industrial system alone wis sufficient. We hal triel to redeem and to help those human wrecks which which were the result of our industrial system but we had got no further. We were beginning to look at the causes and seeking to remove them and he believed one of the ways by which the causes could he touched was by such a scheme as Mr. Morton had outlined. (Applause). Mr. NIGHTINGALE, its II Socialist, supported the scheme. He thought it would be a Goi-send to thou- sands if it could be put in operation to-morrow. Mr. lIAMPSON also supported and thought the State would ultimately get control of the means of produc- tion, distribution and exchange through the operation of such a scheme as Mr. Morton's. Mr. G. WALKER failed to see how the scheme would realise its objects. It would not prevent the disease but only attempt to cure it. The scheme might be adapted to a new community ranch better than to the industrial conditions which existed in a country like this.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks vvas nassed to the chairmln and the lecturer on the proposition of Mr. THORNLEY seconded by Mr. WPM. The CRA:IRM.I.N said he rather sympathised with Mr. Walker's vienvs, He did not see what wnrk all the nnemployel labour was to be put to in bid times and another difficulty was the competition with existing industries.</p>
        <p>HERNIA.—The new Fluid Pad Truss never gets hard and is effectual in most difficult cases. For slight cases the new Waterproof Washable Truss, which is always clean and most durable, is strongly recommended.— WHITS &amp; WRIGHT, Surgical Instrument Makers, 93, Renshaw st., opposite top of Bold at., Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>WORKINGMEN'S MISSION.</head>
        <p>WORKINGMEN'S MISSION.</p>
        <p>Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>A successful sale of work in aid of several special funds in connection with this flourishing mission was held in the sehools, Thewlis-street, on Wednesday and Thursday. The mission workers had taken up the enterprise with much enthusiasm and the heavily laden and well arranged stalls were an evidence of their self- sacrificing labours. The stalls were superintended by the following:—</p>
        <p>Society Class Stell.—Secretary Mrs. J. Hassell; treasurer Mr,. S. Hill.</p>
        <p>Women's Class Stall.—Secretary Mrs. Brimelow ; treasurer Mrs. Moore.</p>
        <p>Young Ladies' Stall.—Secretary Mrs. Tanner; treasurer, Mrs. George Dale. Men's Class Stall.—Secretary, Mr. Thompson; treasurer, Mr. David Hall. _ _</p>
        <p>Young Men's Class Stall.—Secretary, Mr. Harry Foden • treasurer, Mr. Gilbert Haley. Pound Stall.—Secretary, Mrs. N'oden, Refreshment Stall.—Mrs. David Hill. Flower Stall.—Misses A. Dobbins, Thomas, and Fellowes.</p>
        <p>Art Gallery.—Mrs. Whitby and Mrs. Foster. Mr. Thomas Taylor acted as treasurer of the sale of work funds.</p>
        <p>OPENING CEREMONY.</p>
        <p>It had been announced that Mrs. A. H. Crosfleld would open the sale and that Mr. A. H. Crosfield, M.P., would preside. It was, however, impossible for them to be present and Mrs. Crosfield, of Colwyn Bay, a warm friend of the Mission, kindly stepped into the breach. Captain Crosfield presided, and was accompanied on the platform by Mrs. Crosfield, Mr. N. Hough, Mr. T. Dale, and Mr. A. Tilling (secretary). Mr. TILLING read a letter from Mr. A. H. Crosfield explaining that the arrangements for the third reading of the Budget in the House of Commons would make it impossible for his wife and he:to attend the sale. " very greatly regret this," he wrote," but I am sure our friends at Bank Quay will kindly excuse our attendance under these circumstances, and at a very critical time in English political history." Mr. Tilling went on to explain that they hoped to raise .£lOO by the sale. .235 was required to clear off the debt on the organ fund and they also wanted to instal improved electric lights in the chapel, to purchase large type hymn books, and to devote £2O to the League of Help fund for the winter's work. Any balance that remained would form the nucleus of a fund for extending the Sunday school. Captain CROSFIELD wished the workers all success in that excellent undertaking and said he was particularly impressed with the splendid work which their League of Help had done in the Bank Quay district. Ho was glad to know that the work was going to be extended to the whole town. A permanent secretary (Nlr.Rowlands),who was thoroughly in touch with social work, had been appointed, and he (Captain Crosfield) believed there would be no difficulty in getting the admirable work of the mission League of Help to fit into the general scheme. Captain Crosfield concluded by complimenting the workers on the great improvement to the organ which had been effected by the additional stops. Mrs. CROSFIELD said she felt very sorry and dis- appointed that Mrs. Arthur Crosfield was not in her place that day. They all hoped up to a few days ago that she and Mr. Crosfleld would have been present, hut it was impossible under the circumstances, for them to leave London. It seemed to her, continued Mrs. Crosfield, that bazaars in Warrington were not dying out by any means. (Laughter.) Many people were rather tired of them and they wished someone could suggest another way of raising money, but it seemed impossible to do without them if they were to raise a really substantial sum to do any practical good. Bazaars had this merit—that all, rich and poor alike, conld co-operate to help in doing work for .the church or mission to which they belonged. It was very touch- ing to her to see how hard the members of that Mission must have worked to produce all the pretty and useful articles which she saw on the well-filled stalls. She believed one great reason was because they all loved the mission and they had the work so much at heart that it made them practise self-denial. All the objects of the bazaar were good and necessary ones. She had the pleasure of listening to a selection of music on the organ by Mr. Atkinson a few days ago, and she noticed the wonderful improvement in the Instrument. What appealed to her most of all the objects of the sale, was the League of Help which was started last winter. They hoped that early in the new year the Citizens' League would be doing a much needed work throughout the town. She trusted that all present would do their best so that a good sum of money would be realized. (Applause). Mr. T. DALE, in moving a vote of thanks to Mrs. Crosfleld and Captain Crosfield, said the mission League of Help had acted the good Samaritan to those who were in difficulties, irrespective of denomination or politics. • The vote was heartily accorded, and the business of the sale begat. -</p>
        <p>In the evening a number of school children, trained by the Misses Williams and Crompton, gave some pleasing action songs in highly creditable style. (more) THURSDAY'S PROCEEDINGS.</p>
        <p>The second day's proceedings were presided over by Mr. Henry Roberts, J.P., who was supported on the Platform by Mrs. Armitage, Mrs. H. Roberts, Mrs. Moores (leader of the Women's Bible class connected with the mission), and Mr. A. Tillinv. jun. (hon. secre- tary). After the singing of a hymn MN. Moore offered prayer and the Secretary reported the proceeds of the sale for the previous day, viz. :—.E77 Bs. which was certainly better than they had anticipated. Mr. ROBERTS congratulated the workers on their success of the sale up to then, and felt sure they would more than achieve their object. Mrs. ARMITAGE declarerl the ssle open and after a vote of thanks to Mr. Roberts and Mrs. Armitage had been enthusiastically carried the sale was proceeded with.</p>
        <p>In the evening the Warrington Amateur Orchestra gave selections and the varioui side shows were well patronized.. - -</p>
        <p>. The total proceeds for the two days amounted to £ll7 9s. 3d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>IMPORTANT ENGAGEMENT.</p>
        <p>There is an important and attractive engagement for next week at the Palace and Hippodrome, when Air. Arthur Roberts, the famous London comedian is announced to appear. Mr. Roberts' fame ;is world- wide, and his first visit to Warrington should be a memorable event in the record of public entertainments. Few men can be as funny as the genial Arthur, and there will certainly be much keeness to see him in his new success " The importance of being another man's wife." As Fitzawful Smith, a masher, Arthur Roberts should be in his element, and with the help of half-a-dozen other artistes the sketch is sure to be screamingly funny. Among the other items billed for next week are Adams and Ward, a noted pair of acrobatic comedians, Edie Reynolds, violinist, Edith St. Clare, comedienne, Mr. and Mrs. Harold Finden, popular sketch artistes, Gracie Hunt and Harry Breezy, the eccentric sailor.</p>
        <p>S. HELENS TRADERS V. WARRINGTON THURSDAY.— Played at St. Helens before 500 spectators. A very exciting game throughout, Green retiring from the Warrington team ten minutes before the interval with a sprained ankle, The game ending in a goalless draw</p>
      </div>
