      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>MAYORALTY.</head>
        <p>THE MAYORALTY.</p>
        <p>LIVELY CAUCUS MEETING.</p>
        <p>• The caucus meeting for the selection of Mayor held at the Town Hall last evening and largel;'; attended. The debate lasted over two hours, an' there was a good deal of acrimonious speech-making; Eventually Councillor Dr. Joseph said he would accept nomination for the office after the scenes ther`i: had been and a vote was then taken in favour 0:5- Alderman Forshaw's re-election. Only 18 membei voted for the motion and the remainder of thosfot present did not vote at all. Several members had le before the vote was taken. The election of the Mayor will take place oil Tuesday next, at the annual Council meeting i when' if there s no other nomination, Alderman Fors10: will be re-elected in the usual way. The proceeding commence at 12 o'clock noon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1909-11-06_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>ST. JAMES' LATCHFORD.</head>
        <p>ST. JAMES' LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>Sale of Work. The annuai sale of work in connection with St; James's Church, Latchford, took place in the day sche° on Thursday. The various workers of the church ha spared no effort to make the room attractive and a large variety of useful and ornamental goods were on The opening ceremony was performed by Maitland Wood, of Runcorn (wife of the Archdeacon' Macclesfield). The VICAR (Rev. H. B. Firth) said that he was sP— ing to Archdeacon Wood as to the different methods raising money for church purposes, and the Archdenc°. cliticised some of the methods adopted to obtain luk, He had nothing, however, to say against sales of %Oro. For those desirous of raising funds, sales of work We,. good things, but a great deal depended upon the °Pellet Mrs. Wood had come from Runcorn and he did Deo think she had been in that room before, but Archdence f Wood was there about 16 years ago. The object fe to the sale of work was to raise money sufficient for . clergy fund. In that diocese, which had 26 paris; Runcorn was the largest, and next came St. dateesor where 7,000 people were ministered to. It was necesss.d, to have an adequate staff of clergy so they had incressl the staff by an additional curate. £6O was require-1-n but he hoped they could raise £l6O. (Laughter.) ix, conclusion he thanked all those who had helped arrange the sale. (Applause.) • it Mrs. WOOD who was received with applause said be seemed a very stupid thing to open a sale and not , able to make a speech. It, however, gave her gref:f pleasure to be there and she wished the :tale o%' success. She then declared the sale open. Mr. FORSTER in proposing a vote of thanks to - Wood said he was very sorry that the Archdeacon " unable to be present that afternoon. The Rev. H. H. CORSBIE seconded, and the vote heartily passed.. _</p>
        <p>The stallholders were ns follows : Sweets and Fancy Stall.—Mrs. Swift and gis°6s' Chesters and Heath. Pound Stall.—Misses Jefferson, Houghton, WO end Taylor. A thEr Fruit Stall.—Miss Farghar, Miss Davies an-- Misses Bowcock. 0119' Congregational Stall.—Mesdames Forster. We rbor find Guest, Misses Perry, Gibson, Carruthers, Hughes' Woodall.</p>
        <p>Ice Cream Stall.-I‘lisses Foster and Warburtoll,,berr Refreshment St 11.—Mesdames Hallwood, Ftrctionf Grant, Mee, Bowcock, Wil'cocks, Davies, JoutocXl. Newton, Booth. Lingard, Davies, Misses BO Nesbitt, Peers, Berry, and Mr. T. Mee. e Jot. Shooting Gallery.—Messrs. Barry and Warburton.</p>
        <p>Magic ."Irs.—Mr. If. Critchley. Bran Tub.—Miss Grant and the Misses Bowcoclf," The proceeds were nearly £99.</p>
