        <p>THE NAVAL MUTINY IN GREECE. The naval mutiny in Greece was a lively affair while it lasted. The mutinous destroyer " Sphendone " lost three stokers killed, and the destroyer itself was hit by a shell on its forepart and had to be beached. A hundred and twenty mutinous officers, dressed in mufti, gained the shore, and endeavoured to hold up a train, but they were put to flight by the gendarmery. The casualities appear to have been about a dozen, and although over a hundred of the disaffected officers are at large only a few of the ringleaders are likely to give further trouble. Our illustration shows the Arsenal on the island at Salamis, the seat of the mutiny.</p>
