        <p>ST. PETER'S SALE OF WORK. • Success attended the annual sale of work in connec- tion with St. Peter's Church, which was held in the school, John-street, on Monday and Tuesday. The stalls were most atr ram ively arranged and were presided over by the following ladies : Vicarage Stall.—Mrs. Percival, Mrs. Taylor, Mrs. Greaves, Mrs. Kaye and Mrs. East. Churchwardens' Stall.—Mrs. Steel and Mrs. Cowdell. Refreshment Stall.— Mrs. Bamforth, Mrs. Yeomans, Mrs. Webster, and Miss Skelland. Toy Stall.—Nliss Lillie Percival and Miss Houghton. Flower Stall.—Miss Young, Miss S. Young, and Miss McQuillan. Sweet Stall.—Miss Percival, Mrs. Hughes, and Miss Ince. Bran Tub.—Miss Dolly Percival and Miss Plinston. Miss Parr performed the opening ceremony on Monday. The Vicar (iev. B. Percival) explained the needs of the various parochial funds and also drew attention to the fact that a few years ago a mission which had been carried on by the Methodists in Sharp. street had been closed, and lately St. John's Presby- terian Church had been made into a tannery, so that their own church was now responsible for a population of 11,000. A vote of thanks was passed to Miss Parr on the proposition of Conricillor Lashford, seconded by Councillor Steel, and supported by Mr. Robert Pierpoint. The sale was re-opened on Tuesday by the MAYORESS and a short speech was delivered by the MAYOR. who said he had a wear, affection and regard for St. Peter's parish, for when he was a boy of sixteen he helped Mr. Schonberg in his work there, and now they had a beautiful church and vicarage and an excellent *vicar in Mr. Percival, who had dine a very useful and good work in that parish. (Npplause). On the proposition of Mr. BANIFORTH, seconded by the Rev. J. H. GARNETT, a hearty vote of thanks *as accorded to the Mayor and Ma3oress.—The VICAR announced that the proceeds cif the previous day's sale amounted to £56 9.. sd. The total proceeds amounted to .£ll3.</p>
